(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m2-n101"]={"id":"n101","label":"Notes 10.1 · bold main idea first","revision":"ling406-modern-m2-n101-2","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"**Speech perception recovers one stable message from a signal that is messy and that differs from speaker to speaker.** **Speech perception** (recovering a linguistic message from the sound of speech) turns a mess into a message as clear as “I can see you.”","children":["In the signal for “I can see you,” the energy of each sound runs into the next.","Nothing in the signal cleanly separates letters or words.","Real-time MRI of the **vocal tract** from USC SPAN gives the reason. The articulators move without pause, so the movements for neighboring sounds overlap and their acoustic effects overlap."]},{"text":"Listeners also get **the same message from very different physical stimuli**.","children":["Voices and vocal tract sizes differ, so one word has different acoustic properties from each speaker.","Across **76 speakers**, each of ten vowels covers a broad range of first and second formant values.","The ranges **overlap**, so one pair of formant values can be different vowels for different speakers.","Recognizing speech means finding a **stable linguistic identity** inside enormous physical variation."]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"**Listeners must turn a variable, analog signal into abstract symbols, and categorical perception is thought to reflect that translation.** The premise is that the language system can only operate on something more abstract than the signal.","children":["An **analog signal** (one that varies continuously) can shift in frequency, loudness and timing by tiny amounts.","It has no built-in markers of where one speech sound stops and the next starts.","Many small changes leave the speech sound the same."]},{"text":"The abstract form is a **symbolic representation** (one that identifies a language unit and sets its fine physical detail aside).","children":["The unit is the **phoneme** (a sound category that distinguishes words in a language).","/b/ in “bat” and /p/ in “pat” are different phonemes, because swapping them changes the word.","Many slightly different acoustic versions of /b/ count as one phoneme."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is thought to reflect this **“translation” from physical to symbolic**.","children":["Listeners are less sensitive to differences inside a category.","Listeners are more sensitive to equally large differences that cross a category boundary.","Categorical perception is one account of the translation, and other accounts exist."]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"**The shape of the vocal tract decides which resonant frequencies get amplified, and those amplified frequencies are the formants.** This relation is systematic, following **Ladefoged (1996)**.","children":["The **vocal tract** (the passage from the larynx through the throat and mouth to the lips) changes shape as the tongue, jaw and lips move.","A **resonant frequency** (a frequency that a space naturally strengthens) depends on the shape of the tube.","**Formants** are the resonant frequencies the vocal tract emphasizes, and every vowel has its own combination."]},{"text":"The vowels differ in how their formants are arranged.","children":["Across **heed, hid, head and had**, the tongue moves from high and front toward low. The second formant starts far above the first, and the two draw closer.","Across **hod, hawed, hood and who’d**, the tongue moves back and the lips round more. The first and second formants sit close together at the low end.","A vowel is defined by the **relationship among its resonances**, which stays similar across speakers."]},{"text":"A **formant transition** (a change in a formant’s frequency over time) happens as the mouth moves from a consonant into a vowel.","children":["The steady part of a vowel gives information about the vowel.","The moving part gives information about how the neighboring sound is made."]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"**On a spectrogram, vowels differ mainly in how far apart their two lowest formants are.** A **spectrogram** (a map of acoustic energy over time and frequency) has bands of strong energy for each vowel, and these bands are its **formants**.","children":["The words **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood” and booed** come from Rob Hagiwara’s English vowel samples.","All ten begin with /b/ and end with /d/, so the differences come mainly from the vowels.","**F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up. Both run through the whole vowel at once."]},{"text":"The spacing of F1 and F2 changes across the words.","children":["In **bead**, F1 is very low and F2 is very high, above 2,000 Hz, so they are far apart.","Through **bid, bade, bed and bad**, F1 rises and F2 falls, so they move closer together.","In **bode, “bood” and booed**, the back rounded vowels, F2 drops near 1,000 Hz or below and sits near F1.","Bead pairs a low F1 with a high F2. Booed pairs a low F1 with a low F2.","The pattern of these two lowest formants gives most of what tells vowels apart."]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis treats a speech sound as a source shaped by a filter, and this /u/ has low first and second formants.** **Source-filter synthesis** builds speech sounds from those two parts.","children":["The **source** is the sound energy the voice produces.","The **filter** is how the vocal tract shapes that energy.","Separating them makes it possible to change one while the other stays fixed.","The work is done in **Praat** (free speech analysis software from praat.org), which displays recordings, measures formants and resynthesizes sounds."]},{"text":"The recording is a speaker saying **/u/**, the vowel in “booed.”","children":["A **waveform** (a record of air pressure changes over time) captures timing and loudness.","A **spectrogram** breaks the same sound into its frequency components, so it captures the resonance pattern.","The **first formant is at about 300 Hz**.","The **second formant is very low, around 900 Hz**.","A low F1 and a low F2 fit the back rounded quality of /u/."]},{"text":"If changing only the filter changes the vowel, the vowel’s identity must lie in the filter.","children":["The measured formants are the starting point for building new filters."]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"**Praat finds five formants in the /u/, but only the first two are changed in this synthesis.** **Praat can find formants automatically**, by following each resonance through the recording and marking its frequency moment by moment.","children":["It identifies **five formants** in this recording.","Each one is a frequency component of the same sound, and they run at the same time.","No one has to estimate the frequencies by eye."]},{"text":"**Only the first three formants are usually important for phonemic processing**.","children":["F1 is the lowest resonance, F2 is the next and F3 is the third.","F1, F2 and F3 give most of the information that separates phonemes.","Higher formants depend more on the individual voice."]},{"text":"The example deals with only **the first two formants**, near 300 Hz and 900 Hz in this /u/.","children":["Changing these two is enough to turn one vowel into another, because they hold most of the vowel information.","F3 and the higher formants stay fixed, so any change listeners hear comes from F1 or F2 alone."]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"**Removing the filter from the recorded /u/ leaves the source, which sounds like a buzz.** The **source** is **extracted** using a model of the filter built from the measured formant frequencies.","children":["Praat uses the model to strip the filter’s effect from the recording.","What remains is a **more-or-less “unfiltered” version of the voice**, the sound energy the vocal folds supplied.","It is only more or less unfiltered, because the separation is an estimate."]},{"text":"The extracted source **sounds like a buzz**.","children":["Its energy is spread across many frequencies, without the strong low formants of /u/.","Pitch survives, because pitch comes from vocal fold vibration.","Vowel quality disappears, because vowel quality comes from the filter."]},{"text":"The separation is **a little bit like doing a multiple regression analysis**.","children":["In multiple regression, one predictor’s contribution is estimated and removed so the rest can be examined.","Here the filter’s contribution is estimated and removed, and the rest is treated as the source.","New filters can then be applied to the one source, so the effect of the resonance pattern can be tested."]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"**Three filters are applied to one source, and each filter changes only one part of the resonance pattern.** A **filter** (a specification of which frequencies get more emphasis and which get less) is a resonance pattern.","children":["The **first filter** is just the first four formants from the original recording, so it should bring back the original /u/.","The **second filter** raises **the first formant a little** and leaves the other formants roughly where they were.","The **third filter** raises **the second formant a lot** and leaves F1 and the higher formants roughly in place."]},{"text":"The changes are separate, not a shift of every frequency together.","children":["In the original filter, F1 and F2 are both low, which fits /u/.","Raising F1 moves only the lowest resonance up.","Raising F2 moves only the second resonance up, by a much larger amount."]},{"text":"The **sound source stays the same** for all three filters.","children":["No new speaker or recording is needed.","Any change in vowel quality comes from the filter.","Comparing the outputs isolates what each resonance change does."]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"**Combining one source with three different filters gives three different vowels.** **Multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels**, because the output is the source shaped by the filter.","children":["The source provides the raw sound energy.","The filter decides how much of that energy stays strong in each frequency region."]},{"text":"Each filter gives its own result.","children":["With the **original filter**, F1 and F2 stay low and the result keeps the quality of /u/.","With **F1 raised a little**, the lowest resonance rises and the vowel changes.","With **F2 raised a lot**, the second resonance rises far and the vowel changes another way. The back rounded /u/ moves toward a more front vowel."]},{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** separates two contributions that ordinary speech combines.","children":["The voice provides the sound.","The vocal tract resonances shape its quality.","All three outputs share the same buzz, pitch and timing, yet they sound like different vowels.","A researcher can change vowel quality by swapping only the filter, without asking a speaker to record each version."]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"**Formant values vary with speaker, speech rate and context, yet they still describe vowel space well for one careful talker.** The vowel a speaker intends only **partly** determines the formant values.","children":["**Anatomical features** differ between speakers of the same language. A longer vocal tract gives lower resonances overall.","**Different rates of speech** matter, because fast speech gives the articulators less time to reach their targets.","**Context** matters, because the tongue and lips are already heading toward the next sound or leaving the previous one.","Context includes the **phones** (particular spoken realizations of speech sounds) produced before and after a vowel."]},{"text":"Across 76 speakers, each of ten vowels covers a wide range of formant values, and the ranges overlap.","children":["The same F1 and F2 values can be different vowels for different speakers.","No single pair of frequencies defines a vowel for everyone."]},{"text":"Formant values nonetheless describe **vowel space** (the layout of a language’s vowels by their formant frequencies) pretty well, especially for **a specific talker speaking carefully**.","children":["Within one careful talker, the vowels separate far more cleanly than across many talkers.","All else being equal, **reasonable approximations of vowels** can be produced by manipulating **steady-state formants**. These are the resonances in the stable middle of a vowel, not the transitions at its edges."]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"**The pattern playback synthesizer turns a painted spectrogram back into sound, so researchers can control exactly what listeners hear.** Researchers paint bands on a transparent sheet wherever they want acoustic energy in time and frequency, and the **pattern playback synthesizer** plays the pattern aloud.","children":["Changing the painting changes the sound.","Researchers could build controlled, speech-like sounds without relying on natural recordings that vary in many uncontrolled ways."]},{"text":"The machine works by shining light through the painted pattern.","children":["A light source shines through a **cylindrical lens** onto a spinning **tone wheel**, whose rings modulate the light at many frequencies at once.","A lens and a **mirror** send that light onto the painted **spectrogram**, and the painted areas pass along the matching frequencies.","A **light collector** and a **photocell** turn the light into an electrical signal.","An **amplifier** and a **loudspeaker** turn that signal into sound."]},{"text":"A researcher can alter one formant band or one transition and hear the result right away.","children":["Every other part of the pattern stays the same.","Any change in what listeners hear comes from the one painted change.","Series of patterns differing in tiny steps revealed how formant transitions signal consonants."]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"**Stop consonants are told apart by their formant transitions, and Liberman and colleagues (1957) copied those transitions with painted patterns.** The stops **/b/, /d/ and /g/** are made by briefly closing the vocal tract and then opening it.","children":["The audible part of a /b/, /d/ or /g/ happens **while the jaw is opening**.","The vocal tract changes shape quickly during the opening.","The formants glide from the consonant’s position toward the vowel’s position, so the consonant’s identity lies in how the formants move."]},{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the **pattern playback synthesizer** to create speech-like sounds that replicated these **formant transitions** (changes in formant frequency over time).","children":["Each pattern had two formants.","The **lower formant** was nearly the same in every stimulus.","The **upper formant** started at a different frequency in each stimulus and then settled at the same steady vowel level.","The fourteen patterns kept the vowel the same and changed only where the upper transition began."]},{"text":"The direction of the upper transition decided the consonant.","children":["A **rising** transition was heard as /b/.","A roughly **flat** transition was heard as /d/.","A **falling** transition was heard as /g/.","Only the transition differed, so listeners’ responses could be tied directly to it."]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"**The speech continuum changes F2 onset in equal steps, so every neighboring pair of stimuli differs by the same physical amount.** A **speech continuum** (a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property) is built here from 13 stimuli.","children":["**F1 is kept constant**, which removes one possible difference between stimuli.","The **F2 onset frequency** (the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition) is **varied linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13.","Linearly means the onset changes by the same step every time.","So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series.","children":["Early stimuli start F2 well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises.","Middle stimuli start F2 higher, so the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat.","Later stimuli start F2 above the vowel frequency, so the transition falls."]},{"text":"Equal steps make the continuum a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and others straddle a boundary.","A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size.","A categorical listener would hear steps within a category as alike and steps across a boundary as different.","Any advantage for boundary pairs would come from the boundary itself."]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"**If perception were non-categorical, labeling would shift gradually from /b/ to /d/ to /g/ along the continuum.** An **ideal observer model** (a prediction of how a listener would respond under one particular account) is used as a baseline.","children":["This simple version assumes perception is **non-categorical** (it tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries).","Real data can be compared against the baseline to see whether perception is more categorical than that."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**.","children":["A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**.","**Stimulus 2** would be **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**, because it sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype."]},{"text":"Predicted **labeling** changes smoothly.","children":["Responses for one category fade out in a straight slope while responses for the neighboring category fade in.","Two neighboring labels are equally likely halfway between their prototypes.","No label changes in a sudden step."]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"**If perception were linear, discrimination accuracy would be the same for every pair, whatever the labeling looks like.** A **linear** ideal observer perceives equal acoustic differences as equally large. It predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are the **same or different**.","The two tasks are separate, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**.","children":["There are three categories, and listeners are choosing between labels.","Each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away from it.","The peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself."]},{"text":"Discrimination is predicted to be flat.","children":["Pairs are two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**, so every pair has the same physical separation.","If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, discrimination accuracy would also be constant, at about 0.8.","A **flat** result would mean category boundaries play no role in hearing differences.","A **peaked** result would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear."]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957) found sharp labeling boundaries and the best discrimination right at those boundaries.** This pattern is **categorical perception**, and it departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions.","children":["Listeners labeled each stimulus on the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum.","Listeners also judged pairs that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart."]},{"text":"Labeling switched abruptly.","children":["Stimuli 1 through about 3 were labeled /b/ close to 100% of the time.","Around stimuli 4 and 5, responses switched almost completely to /d/, which stayed dominant through about stimulus 8.","Around stimuli 9 and 10, responses switched just as quickly to /g/.","The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**.","children":["Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary.","Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance** (the level expected from guessing).","Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data.","children":["They assumed listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels.","The predictions captured much of the real pattern.","Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity beyond the labels."]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"**Each listener discriminates best near the point where that listener’s own label switches from one word to the other.** Data from students in prior years tested **categorical perception** with a **beach to peach continuum**. The two words differ in the first consonant, /b/ or /p/.","children":["In **identification**, each listener labeled every stimulus from 1 to 9.","In **discrimination**, each listener judged pairs two steps apart, from stimuli 1 and 3 through stimuli 7 and 9."]},{"text":"The first listener switches around the middle of the continuum.","children":["Identification stays on one word for the early stimuli and then **switches to the other word**.","Responses are mixed only in a small region near the switch.","Discrimination is best for stimuli 4 and 6 and for stimuli 5 and 7, the pairs that straddle the boundary.","Pairs well inside one category are hard to tell apart, even with the same physical separation.","Stimuli 6 and 8 fall in between, because stimulus 6 itself received mixed labels."]},{"text":"The second listener **switches later** along the continuum.","children":["Discrimination is best near that later boundary.","Different people can place their category boundaries at different points."]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"**Listeners differ in where their boundary falls, but discrimination is generally best for sounds heard as different categories.** **Not everyone has exactly the same category boundary**.","children":["A sound near the beach to peach boundary for one listener can fall squarely inside a category for another.","A pair that straddles one person’s boundary may stay inside one category for someone else.","Averaging across the group can flatten the boundary peak when individual boundaries differ."]},{"text":"**In general, discrimination is best for stimuli perceived as being in different categories**.","children":["Listeners may miss a physical difference between two versions of the same phoneme.","The same size of difference is far easier to hear when the two sounds are heard as different words."]},{"text":"The discrimination experiment included **only “different” trials**, so the lab could be done in a short amount of time.","children":["Every pair held two physically different stimuli.","The correct answer on every trial was **“different,”** if the listener could discriminate the sounds.","Answering “same” meant missing a real difference.","Accuracy is the share of pairs heard as different, and someone who always said “different” would score perfectly.","Low accuracy on within-category pairs means listeners often failed to hear real differences."]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"**Real data are noisy, and this experiment had too few trials and no trials with identical sounds.** The one iron-clad law of experimental research is that **stuff happens**.","children":["Responses to a carefully built continuum can still bounce around.","An isolated rise or drop in discrimination reflects noise in a few responses.","Identification (which word is chosen) and discrimination (whether a difference is detected) can vary in different ways across trials and people."]},{"text":"More results from prior years compare a **bear to pear continuum** with the **beach to peach continuum**.","children":["For bear to pear, identification switches fairly clearly from one word to the other.","Bear to pear discrimination jumps between 0 and 1 from pair to pair, including a miss on a pair that crosses the boundary.","Another listener’s beach to peach data fluctuate even more. Identification zigzags instead of switching once, and discrimination bounces between high and low values."]},{"text":"The experiment could be done differently in two ways.","children":["Only “different” trials were used, so nothing measured how often listeners said “different” when the sounds were identical.","The short session gave each listener **few trials per pair**, so a single response could move accuracy a great deal.","A better version would add trials with identical sounds and more trials for each pair.","The real test is whether better discrimination lines up with where identification changes, across the whole continuum."]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"**Being worse at hearing differences inside a category helps, because it keeps word recognition stable.**","children":["Categorical perception makes listeners worse at hearing differences within a category.","That can seem like throwing away useful acoustic detail."]},{"text":"The advantage is **stable word recognition**.","children":["Every production of a phoneme differs physically from every other, because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds.","If each small acoustic change were heard as a new identity, everyday versions of one word would sound like unrelated sounds.","Reduced sensitivity within a category lets many acoustic versions count as **one phoneme**."]},{"text":"Differences **between categories** stay easy to hear.","children":["The distinctions that change words are kept.","Variation inside a category is tolerated, because it leaves the word unchanged.","Ignoring small within-category differences frees the listener to focus on the differences that change meaning."]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"**Infants cannot name sounds, so researchers test their speech perception with behaviors that change when the infant notices something new.** **Categorical perception in infants** can be tested this way because infants react to novelty.","children":["The experimenter controls exactly which sound difference is presented.","The infant’s response reveals sensitivity to that difference without any labeling."]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** comes from Janet Werker’s infant studies lab.","children":["An infant learns to turn toward a sound when the sound changes.","A correct turn earns an interesting visual reward, such as a moving toy.","After this link is learned, a head turn following a change means the infant detected the change."]},{"text":"The infant results on categorical perception come from **high-amplitude sucking**.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a recording device, and strong sucks make a sound play.","As the same sound repeats, sucking slows through **habituation** (growing used to a repeated sound).","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change.","Both methods work in infants who are months from saying words."]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971) found that 4-month-old infants notice a voicing change only when it crosses the adult category boundary.** They used **high-amplitude sucking** with sounds that varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**. VOT is the delay between the release of a consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["Adults hear a short VOT as a voiced consonant such as /b/.","Adults hear a longer VOT as a voiceless consonant such as /p/."]},{"text":"Infants heard one synthetic syllable over and over until their sucking slowed, and then the sound changed or stayed the same.","children":["In condition **20D**, the VOT changed from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**, which **crossed** the adult boundary between two consonant categories.","In condition **20S**, the VOT also changed by **20 milliseconds**, from −20 to 0 or from +60 to +80. Both sounds came from the **same** adult category.","In condition **0**, the control group kept hearing the same sound."]},{"text":"Sucking rose at first and then dropped in every group as the sound became familiar.","children":["In 20D, sucking **recovered** sharply after the change.","In 20S, sucking kept falling with little recovery.","In 0, sucking kept declining steadily.","Both changes were 20 milliseconds, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary.","Even at four months, infants perceive voicing **categorically**."]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"**English-learning infants lose the ability to discriminate non-English contrasts during the first year, and this is perceptual narrowing.** **Perceptual narrowing** (experience tuning perception to the sounds of the surrounding language) brings a decline in sensitivity to some distinctions from other languages.","children":["**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested how age and experience affect infants’ ability to discriminate two **non-English contrasts**.","The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two “ta”-like sounds that English does not use.","The **Salish contrast** is a distinction between “ki” and “qi” in Nthlakampx, a Salish language, which English also does not use.","The measure was the percentage of infants reaching a **discrimination criterion** at each age."]},{"text":"English-learning infants succeeded less often at each older age.","children":["At **6 to 8 months**, about 95% met criterion for Hindi and about 80% for Salish.","At **8 to 10 months**, about 67% met criterion for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At **10 to 12 months**, about 20% met criterion for Hindi and about 10% for Salish.","**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** met criterion close to 100% of the time for their own language’s contrast."]},{"text":"The results are described as **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**.","children":["Young infants can detect these nonnative contrasts.","Infants who hear a contrast in their own language keep it, so the decline depends on language experience.","Contrasts the language lacks become hard to hear by about the first birthday."]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"**The compelling narrative says categorical perception is special to language, present early, and tuned from a universal start.** The earlier findings feed **a very compelling narrative** with three linked claims.","children":["First, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**. The mechanism that sorts sounds into categories is specialized for language.","Second, these abilities are **present very early in development**. The infant sucking and head-turn experiments suggest they exist long before children talk.","Third, children **start off as “citizens of the world,”** able to hear **all** contrasts, and then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems to their specific language**."]},{"text":"The third claim has two parts.","children":["“Citizens of the world” means infants could learn any human language and are ready to hear the distinctions of every one.","Tuning means the contrasts of the surrounding language stay available while contrasts from other languages fade, as in the Werker and Tees results."]},{"text":"The narrative covers both the **starting state** and **developmental change**.","children":["It proposes broad, language-specific abilities at birth.","It treats development mostly as narrowing from that broad start.","Each early result seems to fit, since infants discriminate categorically before talking, hear contrasts from many languages and lose nonnative contrasts with experience."]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"**Four findings weaken the compelling narrative that categorical perception is a special language ability with a universal start.** The narrative meets **some issues**, three about **categorical perception** and one about **infant speech perception**.","children":["Categorical perception is **not limited to language**. It also appears with nonspeech sounds, so it does not look special to language.","Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**. Nonhuman animals also perceive speech sounds categorically, so it does not look special to humans.","Categorical perception is **task-dependent**. Results look more or less categorical depending on the task.","Choosing between labels makes responses more categorical than a task that measures fine acoustic differences."]},{"text":"Infant speech perception is **not actually “universal.”**","children":["Some contrasts are harder for infants to detect than others from early on.","Development includes initial differences in sensitivity as well as changes driven by experience.","These issues weaken the strong claim that every speech distinction starts out equally available in a mechanism devoted only to language."]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974) found categorical perception for plucked and bowed sounds, which are not speech.** They tested **non-speech sounds** that varied in **rise time** (how quickly a sound reaches full loudness after it begins).","children":["A **rapid rise time** sounds **plucked**, like a plucked string.","A **slow rise time** sounds **bowed**, like a bowed string.","Pluck and bow are labels for two kinds of sound onset, and they are not phonemes."]},{"text":"The stimuli included **sawtooth waves**, **sine waves** and **speech syllables**, with short and long rise times such as 10 and 70 milliseconds.","children":["With a 10-millisecond rise, the sound reaches full strength almost at once.","With a 70-millisecond rise, the sound builds up gradually."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, labeling switched in a narrow region and discrimination peaked there.","children":["Short rise times were labeled pluck and long rise times were labeled bow, each close to 100% of the time.","The label switched steeply around 30 to 50 milliseconds.","Discrimination of pairs 10 milliseconds apart stayed around 55 to 60% for most pairs, a little above chance.","Discrimination **peaked near 75%** around 30 to 50 milliseconds, right where the labels switched.","The link between a label boundary and better discrimination holds for a sound distinction that has nothing to do with language."]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977) found that infants also perceive non-speech sounds categorically.** They tested **infants** on **non-speech sounds** with **high-amplitude sucking**, which measures whether a change in sound is detected.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until their sucking declined.","The sound was then shifted in one of three ways."]},{"text":"The three conditions were **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**.","children":["Before the shift, sucking declined in every condition as the sound became familiar.","After no change, sucking stayed low.","After a within-category change, sucking also stayed low.","After a between-category change, sucking jumped back up and then declined again as the new sound became familiar."]},{"text":"Infants responded more strongly to a change that crossed a category boundary than to a change within a category.","children":["This mirrors the pattern **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found for speech.","Infants cannot be using knowledge of words or phonemes when they respond to plucked and bowed sounds.","Their categorical response must come from more general auditory processing.","That same processing could also underlie their categorical response to speech."]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"**Chinchillas trained on two speech sounds label the sounds in between as steeply as humans do.** The evidence comes from **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**.","children":["The animals were trained with **operant conditioning** (learning in which consequences change how often a behavior occurs).","Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-millisecond lag** stimulus or the **80-millisecond lag** stimulus was an **SD** for food.","**SD** stands for **discriminative stimulus** (a signal that a response will be rewarded).","The lag is **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, chinchillas and human listeners were tested on the VOTs in between, along the **/ga/ to /ka/ continuum**.","children":["Short VOTs were labeled /ga/ almost every time.","Long VOTs were almost never labeled /ga/.","The switch happened over a narrow range, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds, for both species."]},{"text":"The study also varied the **burst** (the brief noise at the release of a stop consonant). It also varied the **aspiration noise** (the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release).","children":["Making them louder shifted the boundary toward shorter VOTs, made the switch steeper, or both.","This happened for one chinchilla and one human listener, so both species responded similarly.","Animals without human language can sort a speech continuum into categories, which argues against categorical perception being special to human language."]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"**Some contrasts are hard for infants to hear at first, even native ones, and experience makes them easier.** Some contrasts are **less salient** (less easy to notice) than others for infants.","children":["The contrasts tested differ in **place of articulation** (where in the mouth a sound is made).","In **[ma]**, the nasal is made with the lips.","In **[na]**, the nasal is made with the tongue near the ridge behind the teeth.","In **[ŋa]**, the nasal is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate, as at the end of “sing.”"]},{"text":"Researchers compared **looking time** on **same** trials (the syllable stayed the same) and **change** trials (the syllable switched). Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change.","children":["English-hearing infants at **4 to 5 months** looked longer on change trials for the native [ma] and [na] contrast, about 5.4 seconds against about 3.3 seconds.","The same infants looked about equally for the nonnative [na] and [ŋa] contrast, about 4.2 seconds against about 4.5 seconds. English never uses [ŋ] at the start of a syllable.","**Filipino-learning infants** hear [na] and [ŋa] as a contrast in their own language.","At **6 to 8 months**, they looked about 3.8 seconds on both kinds of trials, so they did not yet detect the change.","By **10 to 12 months**, they looked about 4.8 seconds on change trials and about 3.3 seconds on same trials."]},{"text":"Experience with the language **strengthens** sensitivity to such contrasts over the first year.","children":["Development includes gains in sensitivity as well as losses."]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"**Klatt (1979) describes speech perception as a chain of stages that ends in word recognition.** It is a very old and very general model, and each stage transforms the output of the stage before.","children":["The **speech waveform** enters the **peripheral auditory system**, which does a **spectral analysis** (the **cochlea** splits sound into its frequency components).","**Acoustic property detectors** respond to particular properties of the sound.","**Phonetic feature detectors** estimate the probability of features such as voicing at each moment.","**Segmental analysis** combines features into a sequence of speech segments.","**Lexical search** compares that sequence with stored words and yields **lexical hypotheses** (candidate words)."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** fits this model. Between the **analog signal** from the cochlea and the **activation of phonemes**, **information is lost**.","children":["Many physically different sounds become the same phoneme.","This **loss is helpful**, because it lets listeners make contact with higher levels of representation.","The model assumes that to identify words, listeners **first identify their constituent phonemes**."]},{"text":"Two problems challenge this simple view.","children":["In **cascaded processing**, partial information moves on before the earlier decision is finished.","In **feedback**, a higher level such as words sends influence back down to a lower level such as phonemes."]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"**Equal changes in voice onset time are heard as different only when they cross the beach and peach boundary.** The beach and peach continuum is a familiar **voicing continuum**, with /b/ in beach and /p/ in peach.","children":["The two consonants differ mainly in **voice onset time**, or **VOT** (the delay between releasing the lips and the start of vocal fold vibration).","VOT gets shorter in equal steps from “peach” to “beach”.","A long delay sounds like peach. A short delay sounds like beach."]},{"text":"In **identification**, the labels switch sharply.","children":["Stimuli 1 through 5 get one label every time.","Stimulus 6 gets mixed labels, near 0.33.","Stimuli 7 through 9 get the other label every time.","The **category boundary** is the narrow region where the label switches."]},{"text":"In **discrimination**, accuracy follows the boundary.","children":["Pairs inside one category are almost never heard as different.","Pairs 4 and 6 and 5 and 7 straddle the boundary and are heard as different every time.","Pair 6 and 8 is intermediate, near 0.33. Pair 7 and 9 falls back to 0.","A step across the boundary changes the word. A step inside a category does not."]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"**In a threshold model, categorical perception arises at phoneme identification because activity passes on only after a threshold is crossed.** This is one answer to how categorical perception happens.","children":["A **threshold** is a set level of activity that must be reached before anything is sent onward.","Activity below the threshold has no effect on the next level."]},{"text":"Take the voicing evidence for a sound between /b/ and /p/.","children":["At the **feature level**, voicing evidence swings between a **+voiced** threshold and a **−voiced** threshold.","At the **phoneme level**, /b/ and /p/ stay silent while the evidence wavers.","Once +voiced evidence crosses its threshold, **/b/ switches on all at once**."]},{"text":"An ambiguous stimulus either crosses a threshold or does not.","children":["The word level receives a finished /b/ or a finished /p/.","The earlier uncertainty stays behind at the feature level.","Two different sounds that both end up as /b/ are identical at word recognition.","This **all-or-none switch** produces categorical perception at the phoneme level."]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"**In a cascaded model, activity flows forward continuously, so categorical perception might happen later or be an artifact of the task.** Each level responds to whatever activity is present at the moment, while decisions are still forming.","children":["Feature activity and phoneme activity rise and fall together.","More **+voiced** evidence means more **/b/** activation.","Evidence moving toward **−voiced** shifts activation toward **/p/**.","Mixed evidence gives /b/ and /p/ each some activation, in proportion to the evidence."]},{"text":"Graded phoneme activation passes graded activation to words.","children":["A sound near the boundary **partly activates both beach and peach**.","Two sounds both labeled /b/ can differ in how much they supported /p/."]},{"text":"Categorical perception might be an **artifact of the task structure** (a result created by how something is measured, not by the process under study).","children":["An identification task forces a click on beach or peach, even if both words were partly active.","The answer looks categorical because the response choices are categorical."]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"**Making furniture is a metaphor for thresholded activity, where each step needs the finished product of the step before.** Each operation turns one specific input into one specific output.","children":["The **lumber mill** turns **trees** into **lumber**.","The **furniture factory** turns **lumber** into **unassembled furniture**.","The **IKEA customer** turns **unassembled furniture** into finished **furniture**."]},{"text":"Each step waits for a completed product.","children":["Trees become lumber before any furniture is made.","Lumber becomes parts before any assembly begins.","No part of one stage's work moves on until the whole job is done."]},{"text":"**Thresholded processing** works the same way.","children":["A higher level waits until the lower level supplies the representation it needs.","Information moves forward **only as a completed product**."]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"**In a thresholded system, each layer can only take input that the previous layer has transformed completely.** The factory must wait for the shipment of lumber before manufacturing can start.","children":["Many computer programs work this way.","Each stage hands on only its finished output.","A lower stage can keep working while the higher stage sits idle."]},{"text":"In speech, a strict threshold account makes **word processing depend on an identified phoneme**.","children":["A partly resolved acoustic signal stays at the lower level until it is identified as a phoneme.","The word level receives only the finished segment."]},{"text":"This design decides **what information is lost**.","children":["Different sounds that become the same phoneme lose their differences before word recognition.","This helps listeners treat varied sounds alike.","The cost is that later stages never learn how uncertain the earlier stages were."]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"**A food web is a metaphor for cascaded processing, because its levels continuously interact.** The desert food web links desert plants, insects, kangaroo rats, small lizards, large lizards, scorpions, snakes, desert foxes and hawks by who eats whom.","children":["A drought that reduces plants **immediately** affects the health of kangaroo rats.","The change in kangaroo rats then affects the snakes.","No population waits for the level below to reach a finished state."]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** has the same continuous dependence.","children":["Changing feature evidence changes phoneme activity as it happens.","Changing phoneme activity changes support for words."]},{"text":"The food web also has clear **feedback loops**.","children":["More hawks change the populations of snakes, lizards and rats. Those changes then affect the hawks’ own food supply.","In recognition, **feedback** is influence from a higher level back down to a lower one.","Cascading sends evidence forward. Feedback lets a higher level change an earlier representation."]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"**The deciding question is whether phoneme identification for word recognition is thresholded like the lumber mill or cascaded like the food web.**","children":["In a **thresholded account**, word recognition starts only after the phoneme decision is finished.","In a **cascaded account**, a word can gain support while the phoneme is still uncertain."]},{"text":"The accounts differ for two sounds that are both labeled **/b/** but differ in how much they resemble **/p/**.","children":["In a thresholded system, both become the same finished /b/, so pear should **not compete** with bear.","In a cascaded system, the more ambiguous sound passes on more /p/ support, so pear should **compete with bear for a while**."]},{"text":"Both accounts can predict the same final word.","children":["They differ only before the final choice.","The deciding test measures **temporary support for a competing word** during recognition."]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003) used eye-tracking to look for gradient effects of within-category VOT on lexical access.**","children":["**Lexical access** is retrieving a word from memory during recognition.","**Gradient** means changing by degrees.","**Within-category VOT** is the differences in voice onset time among sounds all heard as the same phoneme."]},{"text":"Listeners identified sounds from a continuum between /b/ and /p/ in a **“visual world” display**.","children":["**Eye-tracking** recorded where they looked among the pictures as they heard speech.","Each display had a **target** such as pear and a **competitor** such as bear.","It also had two unrelated **fillers**, a lamp and a ship.","Target and competitor differ only in their first consonant."]},{"text":"Eye movements are fast and continuous during word recognition.","children":["Listeners look at objects as they think about them, often before they know they have decided.","A listener can click bear after a quick look at the pear.","The number and timing of such looks measure how active the competitor was."]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"**The mouse clicks gave a typical step-like identification function, so the final choices look fully categorical.** Participants clicked whichever of the pictures was most consistent with what they heard, such as a “bear” or a “pear”.","children":["Each trial had one sound from the VOT continuum and the four pictures.","The click records the word the listener finally settled on."]},{"text":"The proportion of /p/ responses changed sharply.","children":["From 0 to about 15 milliseconds of VOT, /p/ responses stay near 0.","Between about 15 and 20 milliseconds, they jump up steeply.","From about 20 to 40 milliseconds, they stay near 1.","The **category boundary** is at **17.5 milliseconds**, plus or minus 0.83 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Taken alone, this sharp switch would seem to support the threshold model.","children":["A click records only the word chosen at the end.","Eye movements record which other words got support before that choice.","A sharp identification function can exist together with gradual differences in competition."]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"**Eye movements reveal that ambiguity is gradient, with more looks to the competitor close to the boundary.** Gradient means the uncertainty varies by degree.","children":["The analysis holds the final response constant, comparing only bear clicks or only pear clicks.","So differences in looks cannot come from listeners changing their answer."]},{"text":"Response bear, looks to the **pear**, at VOTs of 0, 5, 10 and 15 milliseconds.","children":["Looks rise after the word begins and peak around 400 to 600 milliseconds. Then they decline as bear wins.","The 15 millisecond VOT, nearest the boundary, gets more and longer looks to the pear than 0 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Response pear, looks to the **bear**, at VOTs of 20, 25, 30, 35 and 40 milliseconds.","children":["The 20 millisecond VOT, just past the boundary, gets the most looks to the bear.","The 40 millisecond VOT, the clearest /p/, gets the fewest."]},{"text":"VOT mainly affects the **duration of activation** (how long the competitor stays active).","children":["Two sounds that both end as bear still produce different amounts of looking at the pear.","The system keeps track of how ambiguous each sound was, which a threshold model would throw away."]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"**Competitor looks increase as VOT approaches the category boundary from either side.** The effect is clearest across the whole VOT range, with the **category boundary** in the middle.","children":["On the bear side, listeners clicked bear, and the measure is looks to the pear.","On the pear side, listeners clicked pear, and the measure is looks to the bear."]},{"text":"Looks were measured in two time windows.","children":["The **early** window is 300 to 1100 milliseconds after the word begins.","The **late** window is 1100 to 1900 milliseconds after the word begins."]},{"text":"Both windows show the same pattern.","children":["On the bear side, late looks to the pear rise from about 0.02 at 0 milliseconds to about 0.045 near 15 milliseconds.","On the pear side, late looks to the bear are about 0.04 near 20 milliseconds and fall to about 0.01 by 40 milliseconds.","Early looks are higher overall but follow the same pattern.","The rise is gradual, not sudden."]},{"text":"Uncertainty about the first sound affects looking throughout recognition.","children":["A sound can support the winner while still supporting its rival.","The closer the sound is to the boundary, the stronger the rival support."]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"**The eye-movement result fits activation that flows in a cascaded way from features to phonemes.** Changing features affect phonemes, and changing phonemes affect words, while recognition is still going on.","children":["Even stimuli identified as /b/ **100% of the time** vary in how strongly the **“voiced” feature** is activated.","A clear /b/ activates voicing strongly. A /b/ near the boundary activates it weakly."]},{"text":"/b/ and /p/ share most of their features.","children":["Both are **bilabial** (the two lips form the closure).","Both are **stops** (airflow is fully blocked and then released).","A stimulus that is obviously **+bilabial** and **+stop** supports both equally.","Only voicing separates them, so weak voicing creates **momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/**.","That ambiguity shows up in the **eye movements** while the participant decides where to click."]},{"text":"The **TRACE** model represents this cascade with three layers.","children":["**Feature layer**, which takes a **pseudo-spectral input representation** (a simplified version of the sound’s frequency content over time). Its detectors are POW, VOC, DIFF, ACU, GRD, VOI and BUR, for power, vocalic, diffuseness, acuteness, gradualness of onset, voicing and burst.","**Phoneme layer**, with units such as /b/, /p/, /r/ and /t/ repeated at every point in time.","**Word layer**, with word units also repeated across time.","Activity passes upward, and units within a layer compete through **inhibition**."]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"**Andruski and colleagues (1994) found that partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level, even from nonwords.** They found **semantic priming** (a faster response to a word after hearing a word related in meaning) with **nonwords**.","children":["The nonword “beace” primes “war”, because beace is peace with a voiced first consonant.","The nonword “ging” primes “queen”, because ging is king with a voiced first consonant.","In **word to word** items, the altered prime is a real word, as when “bill” primes “drug” because bill is an altered form of pill."]},{"text":"Participants did a **lexical decision task** (deciding as fast as possible whether a target is a real word).","children":["Primes were **unaltered**, had VOT reduced by **one third**, had it reduced by **two thirds**, or were **unrelated**.","Unrelated primes gave the slowest responses, near 950 to 970 milliseconds.","Unaltered primes gave much faster responses, around 810 to 860 milliseconds.","With one third removed, responses stayed fast, around 825 to 855 milliseconds.","With two thirds removed, word to nonword items stayed near 820 milliseconds, but word to word items slowed to about 890 milliseconds.","In word to word items, the altered prime also partly activates a competing real word, which reduces the benefit."]},{"text":"**Lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete or imperfect input**.","children":["Beace matches peace closely enough to partly activate it.","That activation spreads to meaning and speeds the response to war, which fits **cascaded processing**."]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"**A thresholded model cannot explain why looks to the other member of the minimal pair rise gradually toward the category boundary.** This eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing**.","children":["A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in one sound, such as bear and pear.","Competitor looks climb steadily as VOT nears the boundary.","This holds even when the final response is the same, so it comes from graded activation within one category."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity never reaches the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation swings between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly switches on.","Every token in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","So word-level competition would be the same for every /b/."]},{"text":"In a cascaded model, graded information does reach the word level.","children":["More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word.","That explains the gradual rise in competitor looks directly."]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"**TRACE also includes feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer.** Activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["In **lexical feedback**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","An uncertain sound can partly activate a word.","That word then strengthens the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"Influence runs in both directions.","children":["Acoustic evidence changes which words are active.","Active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the decision is still forming.","This helps listeners recover from noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**.","children":["Cascading passes incomplete evidence forward.","Feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","An interactive model can have both.","Experiments where word knowledge changes how sounds are heard, such as the **Ganong effect**, motivate adding feedback."]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**Ganong (1980) tested whether knowing which sequences are real words changes how an ambiguous sound is heard.** The finding is the **Ganong effect**. “Gisses and kifts for Valentine’s day” plays on it by swapping the voicing of the first consonants in “kisses and gifts”.","children":[]},{"text":"He made **word and nonword pairs** such as “corpse/gorpse”.","children":["The /k/ end gives corpse, a real word.","The /g/ end gives gorpse, a nonword.","The continua spanned the phoneme boundary in **5 millisecond steps** of VOT."]},{"text":"The design pairs **uncertain phonetic evidence** with a **difference in lexical status**.","children":["The rest of the word, “orpse”, never changes.","It tells the listener which beginning would complete a real word.","If word knowledge matters, an ambiguous first consonant should more often be heard as the sound that makes a word.","The key test is the **ambiguous middle** of the continuum, where word knowledge has the most room to act."]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"**A late correction account predicts a reporting bias toward words that is the same size at every step of the continuum.** In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual**, but there is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound without word knowledge.","At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a word."]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["In a **word-nonword** continuum, the voiced end forms a word.","In a **nonword-word** continuum, the voiceless end forms a word.","In a **neutral** continuum, neither end has a lexical advantage."]},{"text":"A bias added after perception pushes every step toward the word.","children":["Word-nonword responses stay near 0.25 voiced at the long VOT end instead of falling to 0.","Nonword-word responses start near 0.75 voiced at the short VOT end instead of 1.","So even clear sounds are sometimes reported as the word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** (the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions) is a **flat line** near 0.25.","children":["Perception and word knowledge stay separate.","Word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"**If word knowledge shifts the category boundary, the lexical effect should peak at the items closest to the boundary.** In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset, the reading that forms a real word gets extra support.","So categorization itself changes, not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"The conditions differ only in the middle of the continuum.","children":["At both ends, the sound is clear, so voiced responses start near 1 and end near 0 in every condition.","Near the boundary, word-nonword crosses later and nonword-word crosses earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** forms a **peak**.","children":["It is near 0 at the endpoints.","It rises to about 0.35 for items closest to the boundary.","Those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant."]},{"text":"A **flat effect** points to a reporting bias. A **peaked effect** points to a boundary shift.","children":[]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results are strikingly consistent with the second model, the boundary shift.**","children":["At the short and long VOT ends, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give nearly the same answers.","Near the boundary, the two conditions separate the most.","The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"The peak rules out a simple reporting bias.","children":["Word knowledge matters most where acoustic evidence is weakest.","At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance.","At the endpoints, the acoustics decide alone."]},{"text":"Ganong raised several possible explanations, focused on **guessing strategies**.","children":["A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when unsure of the sound."]},{"text":"Later models emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","The whole word can then shape how an ambiguous part of it is heard during recognition."]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"**A TRACE simulation of the Ganong effect shows word and phoneme activations changing as an ambiguous start unfolds into the word plug.** The input is a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**, then /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/.","children":["Activations are recorded in four frames, one as each new sound arrives."]},{"text":"The first frame has only the ambiguous sound.","children":["/b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2.","Word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"The second and third frames add /l/ and the vowel.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl. Partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** all gain activation, because all fit the beginning.","/p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"The fourth frame adds the final **/g/**.","children":["/g/ fits plug but not blood or blush.","**Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, and the alternatives fall away.","**/p/ ends up more active than /b/**."]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"**TRACE explains word effects on phoneme boundaries through top-down feedback from the lexical level to the phoneme level.** **Top-down processing** (higher-level knowledge influencing lower-level analysis).","children":[]},{"text":"Activity accumulates for **“plug”** as the input unfolds.","children":["The biggest gain comes when the **final /g/** arrives.","The /g/ is highly inconsistent with “blood” and “blush”, which were also active in Frame 3.","Blood and blush fit the early input, so they were plausible for a while.","Once /g/ arrives, their endings do not match, so they lose support."]},{"text":"The lexical level then sends **feedback to the phoneme level**.","children":["Feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ gets extra support.","The ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/.","Information at the end of a word can change how an earlier sound is heard."]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"**TRACE is a version of the Interactive Activation model for written word recognition, and many principles are the same.** That model is from **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**.","children":["**Feature** units connect to **letter** units, and letter units connect to **word** units such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK.","Compatible units at different levels support each other.","Competing units in the same level inhibit each other.","Active words send support back down to their letters."]},{"text":"A reader sees **W, O and R** clearly, while ink blots cover part of the last letter.","children":["The visible part of the last letter fits both **R** and **K**.","W, O and R activate WORK and WORD.","Only WORK fits the last letter, so WORK becomes the strongest word.","WORK sends support back to K, so the letter is **perceived as K**, just as plug supports /p/."]},{"text":"A key issue in speech is that **the stimuli unfold over time**.","children":["A written word is all there at once. A spoken word arrives one sound at a time.","TRACE repeats feature, phoneme and word units at every point in time.","A candidate can fit the beginning, gain activation and then weaken when the ending conflicts."]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception still holds, but spoken word recognition is more interactive than a lumber mill.** **Categorical perception** (given the same physical difference, stimuli from the same category are harder to tell apart than stimuli from different categories).","children":["Equal acoustic steps have different effects depending on whether they cross a boundary.","Nothing about cascading or feedback undermines that finding."]},{"text":"The **lexical-phonetic interface** adds two findings.","children":["**Ambiguity about phoneme identity does have an impact on word recognition**. Sounds with the same label still differ in how strongly they activate a competing word.","**Word-level information seems to have an impact on phoneme categorization**. In the Ganong effect, word knowledge shifts the boundary, which TRACE explains with lexical feedback."]},{"text":"So the word recognition system **cannot just sit there waiting** for the phoneme system to finish its job.","children":["Partial information passes forward while uncertainty is still being resolved.","Active words send support back down.","Spoken word recognition works more like the food web.","Labels can be categorical while the processing behind them is graded."]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"**Spoken word recognition is also very fast, and candidate words become active while the word is still unfolding.** **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** used a visual world eye-tracking task.","children":["Participants saw a grid of pictures and geometric shapes and followed spoken instructions to move objects."]},{"text":"The pictures were chosen for their sound overlap with **beaker**.","children":["**Beaker** is the **referent** (the target).","**Beetle** is a **cohort competitor** (shares the beginning).","**Speaker** is a **rhyme competitor** (shares the ending).","**Carriage** is **unrelated** (shares no sounds)."]},{"text":"The measure is the probability of fixating each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds. The spoken word ends a little before 400 milliseconds on average.","children":["Looks to beaker and beetle rise together from around 200 milliseconds, because both fit the beginning.","Looks to beetle peak near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds and fall once the distinguishing sounds arrive.","Looks to beaker pass 0.8 by about 800 milliseconds and approach 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to speaker rise later and stay small, near 0.1.","Looks to carriage stay near zero.","Words that stop matching the input drop out."]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"**A TRACE simulation predicts the eye movements moment by moment, so the looks reveal processing before the final response.** Real fixation probability is compared with TRACE’s predicted fixation probability over 1,000 milliseconds.","children":["For the referent, real and predicted looks both rise steadily from about 300 milliseconds and approach 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","For the cohort competitor, both rise early, peak near 0.17 to 0.2 around 500 milliseconds, and then fall.","For the rhyme competitor, both rise later and stay small, near 0.1.","The predictions closely match the data."]},{"text":"The **granularity** of the simulation is fine (it predicts activity moment by moment, not only the final choice).","children":["**Eye movements give an idea of the changes in “activity”** for different competitors over time.","So researchers can **see cognitive processing before a final response is issued**."]},{"text":"This parallels the **graded effects of category goodness** in categorical perception.","children":["Better and worse examples of a category produce different amounts of competitor activity.","This happens even when the final label is the same."]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell” is grammatical, but its opening words lead readers to build the wrong structure.** It usually feels broken on first reading.","children":["Most readers first take **raced** as the main verb.","Then “the horse raced past the barn” seems to be a complete event in which the horse did the racing.","When **fell** arrives, that structure has no place for it, so the sentence seems to fall apart."]},{"text":"The sentence describes **two events**.","children":["Someone raced the horse past the barn.","The horse then fell.","The intended meaning is “The horse that was raced past the barn fell”.","Here “raced past the barn” tells which horse is meant, and **fell** is the main verb."]},{"text":"The sentence can be **disambiguated** by putting back the missing words.","children":["Adding “that was” makes the modifying relationship explicit, so raced can no longer be the main verb.","Understanding a sentence depends on assigning relationships among words, not only on knowing what each word means."]},{"text":"It could fairly be called **the sentence that launched a thousand experiments**.","children":["Researchers have used it again and again to study how readers build structure as words arrive."]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"**A garden path sentence leads the reader into one interpretation early, and later words reveal that reading to be wrong.** “The horse raced past the barn fell” is the classic case of the **“garden path” effect**.","children":["The name comes from the expression “to lead someone down the garden path”, meaning to mislead.","Raced looks like the main verb.","Fell then conflicts with the structure already built.","The reader must go back and reinterpret “raced past the barn” as “that was raced past the barn”."]},{"text":"The horse sentence is almost as famous as “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”.","children":["“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is grammatical but meaningless.","The horse sentence is grammatical and meaningful but very hard to process."]},{"text":"**Grammaticality and processing difficulty are different things**.","children":["A sentence can have an acceptable structure and still be very hard to follow as it unfolds.","Readers build structure word by word and choose one analysis before the sentence is complete.","If they waited until the end, they would never be led astray."]},{"text":"A dinosaur comic jokes that nobody would ever say the horse sentence.","children":["The reply is that farmers could say it if their horses kept falling over.","The joke works only because the sentence really is grammatical."]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"**A parser has to build a syntactic tree for each sentence while working with limited memory and limited time.** Syntactic explanations of the garden path effect start from this **parser** (a mental process that builds a tree for the sentences we hear or read).","children":["A **syntactic tree** records how words group into phrases and how the phrases relate.","**Limited memory** means the parser can keep only a limited number of structures available at once.","**Time constraints** mean words arrive quickly, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence."]},{"text":"The correct tree for the horse sentence is large.","children":["The subject is a big **NP** (noun phrase) containing “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”.","“That” and “was” are silent, and the clause has an empty subject position.","Inside the clause, raced takes the **PP** (prepositional phrase) “past the barn”.","The main **VP** (verb phrase) contains only fell."]},{"text":"The failed parse is small.","children":["“The horse” is an ordinary NP.","The main VP is “raced past the barn”, with raced as the main verb.","Fell has nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"**Minimal attachment is a strategy in which the parser initially prefers the smaller tree, the one with fewer nodes.** A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":["The grammatical version of the horse sentence is **much more complex syntactically** than the failed parse.","It has **many more nodes**, for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was”.","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"**“Minimal attachment”** saves space.","children":["Each new word is attached to the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes.","Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment."]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**.","children":["That analysis needs the fewest nodes.","The reading where “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause, so the parser passes it over.","When fell arrives, it demands the larger structure, so the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**Late closure is a bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible, so new words join the phrase being built.** It is another parsing strategy in the same framework as minimal attachment.","children":["Keeping a clause “open” means adding incoming words to it instead of closing it and starting a new structure."]},{"text":"**Frazier (1987)** gives two structures for “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”.","children":["The phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big verb phrase starting with “blew open”.","The **preferred** structure attaches the PP to the VP. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, and the dynamite is the **instrument** used to blow open the safe.","The **unpreferred** structure attaches the PP to the NP “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**, and the phrase describes which safe was blown open, the one that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they mean different things.","children":["Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the incoming phrase joins it.","Minimal attachment also prefers VP attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”.","Here the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning easily."]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"**People read more slowly when the sensible meaning of a sentence requires the unpreferred parse.** Late closure is at work in “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels”.","children":["Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe.","The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**.","That meaning needs “with the jewels” attached to the noun phrase “the safe”, the unpreferred NP attachment."]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find the slowdown.","children":["Reading time measures how long readers spend on each part of a sentence.","Eye movement recordings track where readers look and when they jump back to reread.","The extra time appears at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea** that the jewels were used to blow open the safe, and then **retrace their steps**.","children":["The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, as it did for the dynamite.","The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading.","The parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase, and this repair takes measurable time."]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"**The syntactic account assumes a parser that works only on abstract syntactic structure and ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem.** It parallels the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","The parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without consulting meaning or context.","Meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**.","children":["It wants the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**.","It gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","Both limit how much structure must be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"Ignoring ambiguity is **very efficient**.","children":["The parser commits to one likely structure and builds only that one.","Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","When a later word does not fit, the structure must be revised, and that revision causes garden path difficulty."]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"**Sentences with reduced relative clauses are syntactically “difficult”, yet readers often parse them with no trouble.** This is a problem for a purely syntactic explanation. A **reduced relative clause** is a relative clause with “that was” or “that were” left out, as in “the horse raced past the barn fell”.","children":["Trueswell, Tanenhaus and Garnsey (1994) wrote “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a MORPHOLOGICALLY AMBIGUOUS VERB … will be temporarily ambiguous”.","Britt, Perfetti, Garrod and Rayner (1992) wrote “Referential information PROVIDED by the discourse is of no help”.","Frazier and Rayner (1987) wrote “Recent research REPORTED by FERREIRA AND CLIFTON (1986) has demonstrated that syntactic processing is quite independent and that the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence is little influenced by the semantic information ALREADY ANALYZED”.","Altmann and Steedman (1988) wrote “The pattern of results OBTAINED with global reading times is reflected by differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”."]},{"text":"Each verb in capitals introduces a reduced relative, as in the horse sentence.","children":["**But these are not impossibly difficult sentences**.","In “recent research reported by Ferreira and Clifton”, reported describes research that someone reported."]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** argue that **semantic factors** and **statistical regularities** interact with “parsing” from very early on.","children":["Semantic factors concern meaning.","Statistical regularities are patterns in how often words appear in particular structures.","Particular words and their familiar uses can support a reduced relative reading from the start."]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"**Many of the reduced relative examples come from papers that argue reduced relatives are hard to parse.** That makes the collection of examples **wonderfully passive-aggressive**.","children":["Those papers use the difficulty of parsing reduced relatives as evidence for an **autonomous parser**. An autonomous parser builds syntactic structure without help from meaning or context.","Yet the authors wrote reduced relatives in their own articles, and readers understood them easily."]},{"text":"Each capitalized verb modifies the noun before it.","children":["In “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a morphologically ambiguous verb”, followed modifies noun phrase.","In “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence”, assigned modifies analysis.","In “differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”, located modifies differences.","Ferreira and Clifton (1986) wrote “A person PRESENTED with the evidence EXAMINED by the lawyer may interpret the evidence as a theme, not an agent, because examined requires an animate agent”. Presented modifies person, and examined modifies evidence."]},{"text":"The cited examples were **not the only reduced relatives** in those articles.","children":["The rate was about **one per journal page** in this sample of articles.","Reduced relatives are common in ordinary scientific writing.","In a supportive context, a formally complex structure is easy to understand."]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"**Meaning, word statistics and world knowledge interact with the syntax of a sentence from very early on.** This is the argument for why “Recent research reported by…” is easy to read.","children":["**Semantics** helps, because research is **inanimate** (not a living thing) and unlikely to be the subject of a verb like report.","**Lexical statistics** (learned patterns of how often particular words appear in particular structures) help, because “reported” occurs frequently in reduced relatives.","Lexical statistics and **“world knowledge”** help together, because “reported” occurs very frequently in reduced relatives in scientific papers, especially when following the word “research”.","These and many other factors help decide which structure is chosen in the first place."]},{"text":"Two sentences with identical grammar differ in difficulty.","children":["“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today” is easy. Burglars are likely to be arrested, so arrested is read as a reduced relative describing the burglar.","“The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today” is harder. Policemen are likely to do the arresting, so arrested is first taken as the main verb, and “was charged” causes a garden path.","The difference comes from what we know about burglars and policemen."]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"**Listeners briefly treat the empty towel as the place to put the apple, as minimal attachment predicts.** **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested how local **“world knowledge”** can be, using the visual scene in front of the listener.","children":["People saw a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple on a towel.","They heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box” and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","The unambiguous comparison instruction was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like the end of the clause “put the apple on the towel”.","children":["In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**.","In the intended reading, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do look at the empty towel** while planning their movements, as though considering it as a target.","children":["With the ambiguous instruction, looks go from the apple (near 1,000 milliseconds) to the empty towel and then to the box (near 2,000 milliseconds).","With the unambiguous instruction, looks go from the apple straight to the box.","The listener recognizes every word correctly but briefly takes the location phrase as the destination."]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"**With two apples in the display, listeners take “on the towel” as telling which apple, right from the start.** This display is the **two-referent context**.","children":["One apple sits on a towel, and the other sits elsewhere.","The empty towel and the box are still present.","Now “the apple” alone does not say which apple to move, so “on the towel” becomes useful."]},{"text":"Eye movements change in this context.","children":["Participants usually look at the apple on the towel and then at the box.","Looks to the empty towel are much less common.","The **sequence and timing of eye movements**, relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between ambiguous and unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit one with “that’s on the towel”.","children":["The listener needs to know which apple, so a phrase that picks one out is expected.","The destination reading never takes hold.","The same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context differs."]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"**People look at the empty towel much less when the display has two apples.** With two apples it becomes **felicitous** (appropriate and natural in the situation) to give more information about which apple is meant.","children":["With one apple, there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” is easily taken as a destination.","With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the incorrect destination, the empty towel.","children":["In the one-referent context, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials.","In the one-referent context, unambiguous instructions led to almost none.","In the two-referent context, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials.","In the two-referent context, unambiguous instructions led to such looks on roughly 12% of trials."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts, so the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled.","children":["This is an effect of **referential context** (the set of objects a phrase could refer to).","Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard."]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"**The same sentence is parsed differently depending on the scene, and this happens very early, which purely syntactic accounts cannot explain.** The Spivey results are **a big problem** for those accounts.","children":["Different parses of the same sentence are favored under different **semantic** and **pragmatic** conditions.","Semantic conditions concern meaning.","Pragmatic conditions concern how language is used in a situation, such as whether a speaker needs to specify which object is meant."]},{"text":"The words never change, and only the scene changes.","children":["With one apple, the ambiguous sentence led to looks at the empty towel on about 55% of trials.","With two apples, it led to such looks on only about 17% of trials."]},{"text":"The difference appears in **eye movements during the sentence**, before the action is carried out.","children":["The eye movements capture the parse while it is being built.","If context only corrected the parse afterward, listeners in both contexts should first look at the empty towel.","Those looks drop with two apples, so context shaped the **first parse**.","A parser that always starts with the same structure does not predict that."]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"**Syntactic ambiguity bends the path of the hand toward the wrong destination, not only the path of the eyes.** **Farmer and colleagues (2007)** tracked computer **mouse movements** while participants carried out spoken instructions in a version of the apple and towel display.","children":["The actual execution of **arm movements** can be influenced by syntactic ambiguity and its interaction with the **visually defined context**.","Mouse tracking records the whole path of the hand, so a competing interpretation can pull the movement toward another location."]},{"text":"The paths for ambiguous and unambiguous instructions were compared in each context.","children":["In the one-referent context, the ambiguous path **curved toward the empty towel** on the way from the apple to the box.","The two paths separated most in the middle of the movement, and the hand still ended at the correct place.","In the two-referent context, the two paths were nearly identical.","The second apple makes the location phrase useful for picking out an apple, so it is not taken as a destination."]},{"text":"The **Euclidean distance** (straight-line distance) between the two paths was measured at each moment.","children":["In the one-referent context, it peaked at about 200 pixels around 40% of the way through the movement.","In the two-referent context, it peaked lower and earlier, at about 140 pixels around 30%.","Comprehension and action planning interact while both are underway."]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"**After “the”, the parser expects a noun to follow, so one word already limits what can come next.** **The** is a **determiner** (a word that introduces a noun phrase).","children":["“The” does not name anything by itself.","It limits which kinds of words can come next.","An adjective or other modifier may come before the noun, as in “the hot pizza”."]},{"text":"The expectation is about a **grammatical category**, not a particular word.","children":["Many different nouns could follow, such as pizza, dog or book.","The parser predicts the form of the phrase while its meaning is still open.","Words that fit the expectation are processed smoothly.","Words that break the expectation cause difficulty."]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"**“Pizza” completes the noun phrase begun by “the”, so the parser now expects a verb.** **Pizza** is a **noun** and the **head of the noun phrase** (the central word the rest of the phrase is organized around).","children":["“The” combines with “pizza” to form “the pizza”.","“The pizza” can now serve as the subject of the sentence.","The parser expects a verb or verb phrase telling what the pizza did or what happened to it."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word makes two kinds of information available.","children":["Its **category**, noun, tells the parser how it can combine with other words.","Its **meaning**, a kind of food, sets up expectations about plausible events, such as being eaten or baked.","Grammatical expectations and meaning-based expectations both guide how the next words are processed."]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"**“Was” leaves several structures possible, so the parser has to wait for the next words to know its role.** **Was** is a past-tense form of **be**. It can act as an **auxiliary** (a helping verb that combines with another verb to mark tense or voice).","children":["In “the pizza was eaten”, was helps form a **passive** with the participle eaten.","In “the pizza was in the oven”, was links the subject to a phrase giving a location."]},{"text":"The sentence could now continue in several ways.","children":["It could say where the pizza was, and a location phrase would follow with a preposition.","It could say what was done to the pizza, and a passive would follow with a participle such as eaten.","It could say what the pizza was like, and an adjective would follow.","The parser keeps the developing structure available while more words arrive."]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"**“In” starts a prepositional phrase, so the parser now expects a noun phrase that names a location.** **In** is a **preposition** (a word that expresses a relationship such as location or containment).","children":["“In” does not name the location by itself.","It sets up the expectation of a noun phrase that supplies the location, as in “in the oven” or “in the box”."]},{"text":"After “the pizza was”, the word “in” narrows the possible structures.","children":["The sentence is heading toward a statement about where the pizza was.","The expectation is grammatical, so the next word must be able to start a noun phrase.","Words like “the”, “an” or a noun would fit.","Understanding the phrase requires combining the preposition with that noun phrase."]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"**After “in the”, the parser strongly expects a noun, and that expectation is about word category.** This second **the** begins a new **noun phrase** inside the prepositional phrase “in the …”.","children":["“The” does not supply the location by itself.","A noun is still needed, possibly with modifiers before it, as in “in the hot oven”."]},{"text":"The expectation concerns a **word category**, not just a sensible topic.","children":["Whatever comes next must work grammatically as part of a noun phrase.","Knowing the sentence is about pizza makes words like oven or box likely.","The grammatical requirement holds regardless of meaning.","A word that cannot be part of a noun phrase will clash with the expectation."]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"**“Eaten” is a verb form arriving where a noun is required, so it breaks the structure itself.** **Eaten** is the **past participle** of eat.","children":["It cannot complete the noun phrase that “in the” has started.","“In the eaten” is a **word-category violation** (a word of the wrong grammatical category in a position that demands a different one)."]},{"text":"A category error differs from a strange but grammatical meaning.","children":["“The pizza was in the ocean” is odd in meaning but grammatically fine, because ocean is a noun.","“The pizza was in the eaten” cannot be fixed by imagining an unusual situation, because the problem is the grammatical type of the word."]},{"text":"The contrast makes such sentences useful for studying the brain.","children":["Brain responses to category errors and to meaning errors can be compared.","The comparison can reveal whether grammar and meaning are processed differently."]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"**The ELAN is an early brain response over the left front of the scalp to a word of the wrong category.** **ELAN** stands for **early left anterior negativity**. It is an **ERP** (event-related potential, electrical brain activity recorded at the scalp, averaged over many trials and timed to an event). It is elicited by **word class violations**.","children":["A **word class** is a grammatical category such as noun, verb or preposition."]},{"text":"Classic ELAN stimuli are word-category violations.","children":["“The pizza was in the eaten” has a participle where a noun is expected.","“The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem” has the preposition **of** where a noun is expected after “Max’s”."]},{"text":"The ELAN has three features.","children":["It is **early**, about **100 to 200 milliseconds** after the critical word.","It is **left and anterior**, strongest over the left front of the scalp, which raises the question of whether it is specific to language.","It occurs **specifically for word category violations**, such as a noun when a verb is expected, or a preposition in place of a noun."]},{"text":"Incorrect sentences produce the early negative response at left frontal sites, and correct sentences do not.","children":["The response is so fast that it was taken as evidence for a syntactic check of word categories before meaning is processed."]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"**With written words, the early response to a word-category error comes from visual cortex.** **Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)** reported this in “Early occipital sensitivity to syntactic category is based on form typicality”. **Visual cortex** is the region at the back of the brain, in the **occipital** lobe, that processes what we see.","children":["They compared “The beautiful princess …” with “The beautifully princess …”.","Princess is a **bimorphemic noun** (a noun ending in an identifiable noun suffix).","Beautiful is an adjective, which correctly predicts a noun.","Beautifully is an adverb, which predicts a verb or an adjective, so princess is unexpected.","The visual response was **larger** for the unexpected noun at about 120 to 130 milliseconds."]},{"text":"The finding suggests that a prediction can **feed back down to the earliest parts of the visual system**.","children":["When we expect a verb, the prediction covers visual patterns typical of the way verbs are written.","The very early response to the error might reflect a **mismatch** between that prediction and what finally appears on the screen."]},{"text":"Does this only work for items that are **morphologically marked** (containing a part, such as a suffix, that signals the category) as nouns or verbs?","children":["The other possibility concerns **open-class** words (content words that keep gaining members) and **closed-class** words (a small fixed set of function words).","The effect appears for nouns with a noun-marking morpheme and for nouns with a typical noun form.","It does not appear for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues."]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"**The same word form can be a noun or a verb, and words differ in how noun-like or verb-like they sound.**","children":["In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install”, perches is a **noun** (the bars that birds sit on), and seem is the verb.","In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches comfortably in the cage”, perches is a **verb** (what the bird does).","In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met”, needs is a **noun** (the student’s requirements).","In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs to be more focused”, needs is a **verb**.","The **continuation** after the ambiguous word settles its grammatical role."]},{"text":"**Phonological form typicality** is how much a word’s sound pattern resembles the sound patterns of typical nouns or typical verbs.","children":["A corpus analysis placed **3,158 words** by their mean distance in phonological feature space from all nouns and from all verbs.","A small distance means the word sounds similar to that category.","Nouns tend to sound closer to other nouns, and verbs closer to other verbs.","The groups are **noun-like nouns**, **verb-like nouns**, **verb-like verbs** and **noun-like verbs**.","A word can sound like one category while actually belonging to the other."]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"**“Perches” is noun-like and “needs” is verb-like, so each word has one use that matches its form and one that clashes.** These are tendencies in **form typicality**, learned statistical tendencies in how words sound. Both words can still appear in either role.","children":["In “the bird perches seem easy to install”, perches is a noun, which **matches** its noun-like form.","In “the bird perches comfortably in the cage”, perches must be a verb, which **goes against** its noun-like form.","In “the student needs to be more focused”, needs is a verb, which **matches** its verb-like form.","In “the student needs were not being met”, needs must be a noun, which **goes against** its verb-like form."]},{"text":"The four sentences set up a test of form typicality.","children":["The same word form is compared in a matching use and a mismatching use.","The comparison separates what a word’s form suggests from what the grammar of the sentence requires.","If form typicality guides parsing, the mismatching uses should be harder to process.","All four sentences are grammatical."]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"**Readers slow down when the sentence requires the category that does not match how the word sounds.** The measure is reading time at **disambiguation** minus reading time at the **point of ambiguity**, adjusted for word length.","children":["The point of ambiguity is the ambiguous word itself, such as perches or needs.","Disambiguation is the point where the continuation makes the intended category clear, such as “seem” or “comfortably”.","A larger increase signals more difficulty."]},{"text":"**Noun-like homonyms** such as perches were harder to resolve as verbs.","children":["Noun-resolved sentences added only about **50 milliseconds**.","Verb-resolved sentences added about **120 milliseconds**."]},{"text":"**Verb-like homonyms** such as needs were harder to resolve as nouns.","children":["Noun-resolved sentences added about **110 milliseconds**.","Verb-resolved sentences added almost nothing, under 10 milliseconds."]},{"text":"The ambiguous word stays the same in each pair, and only the continuation changes.","children":["The extra cost appears when the continuation demands the category that does not match the form.","Parsing uses learned statistics about how word forms sound, along with the structure the sentence finally requires."]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"**The early brain response to an unexpected noun appears only when the word itself carries a physical cue that it is a noun.** The **ELAN effect** for nouns where a verb is expected is limited to cases with **syntactic** or **statistical form-based** information **physically available**.","children":["Syntactic information means **morphemes** (the smallest meaningful parts of a word, such as a suffix) that indicate noun-ness.","Statistical form-based information means **“nouniness”** (how much a word’s form resembles typical nouns, even without a noun suffix)."]},{"text":"Three kinds of nouns were compared on the **M100** (a brain response peaking about 100 milliseconds after a word appears).","children":["**Bimorphemic nouns** were tested with “The beautiful princess …” and “The beautifully princess …”. Princess has a noun-marking suffix, and the unexpected version gave a significantly larger response.","**Typical nouns** were tested with “The tasteless soda …” and “The tastelessly soda …”. Soda has a statistically noun-like form, and the unexpected version again gave a significantly larger response.","**Neutral nouns** were tested with “The cute infant …” and “The cutely infant …”. Infant has neither cue, and the two versions did not differ."]},{"text":"**No effect** was observed for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues.","children":["An abstract category mismatch alone is not enough to produce this earliest visual response.","An expected category generates predictions about what the next word will look like.","The response appears only when the arriving word carries physical evidence against the prediction."]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"**Four sentences sum up sentence processing, and each one carries one idea.** “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” carries the first idea, that **syntax is independent of semantics** to some extent.","children":["In the limit, a sentence can be grammatically correct but meaningless.","Words can fill a grammatical structure without describing a sensible event."]},{"text":"“The horse raced past the barn fell” carries the second idea, that syntactic structure is often highly **ambiguous** (actually **vague**), but we do not notice except where things fail.","children":["Reading times for “preferred” and “unpreferred” structures suggest that syntactic processes **take shortcuts to save memory**.","They do not activate every possible parse of a sentence.","When the shortcut picks the wrong structure, the reader is led down the garden path."]},{"text":"“Put the apple on the towel in the box” carries the third idea, that **context, pragmatics and other factors** interact with syntactic processing from very early on.","children":["With two apples in view, listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple, not a destination."]},{"text":"“The bird perches were comfortable” carries the fourth idea, that the very earliest syntactic effects observable in the brain are driven in part by **form typicality**.","children":["They are not necessarily driven by **morphosyntactic** structures (grammatical structure signaled by word parts and word order).","They seem to be related to **predictions about the physical forms of words**.","Perches has a noun-like form, so its use as a noun here matches the expectation its form creates."]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"**In the 1870s, German neurologists explained specific losses of language as disconnections between centers in the brain.** **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** began making more systematic observations of **aphasia** (a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage).","children":["A **center** is a brain region that stores one kind of language representation.","A **disconnection** is damage to the pathway between two centers, so information cannot travel between them.","This was reasoning about aphasia before Chomsky."]},{"text":"**Lichtheim’s “House” model** draws the centers and pathways in the shape of a house.","children":["**A** holds **sound images** (the stored sound patterns of words).","**M** holds **movement images** (the stored motor plans for saying words).","**B** holds **concepts**.","Pathway **a** is acoustic and brings heard speech into A. Pathway **m** is motor and carries plans from M to the speech muscles.","Seven numbered sites mark different forms of aphasia, each tied to damage at a particular center or pathway."]},{"text":"Each damage site predicts its own loss.","children":["A break between **A and B** might cause loss of **understanding spoken words**, because the word’s sound cannot reach its concept.","A break between **A and M** might cause loss of **verbatim repetition**, because a heard word cannot pass directly to the plan for saying it."]},{"text":"This **“box and arrow” model** is foundational in **aphasiology** (the study of aphasia) and in modeling typical language function.","children":["The **interactive activation model** of word recognition has the same form, with levels of representation connected by pathways.","The history comes from Eling and Whitaker (2009)."]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"**Geschwind revived the box-and-arrow approach in the 1970s, after linguistics had come to treat syntax as its own system.** The **“Geschwind-Wernicke model”** links language functions to particular brain regions and the pathways between them.","children":["**Broca’s area** is toward the front of the left hemisphere, in the frontal lobe.","**Wernicke’s area** is farther back, in the temporal lobe.","The **arcuate fasciculus** (a bundle of nerve fibers) connects Wernicke’s area with Broca’s area.","The **central sulcus**, or **fissure of Rolando**, separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe behind it.","The **lateral sulcus**, or **fissure of Sylvius**, separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes above it."]},{"text":"In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, the field of **linguistics evolved dramatically**.","children":["Linguists developed a theoretical model in which **syntax was independent** of other aspects of language and had **its own computational principles**.","That opened the door to theorizing about **(double) dissociations** of language functions in more sophisticated ways."]},{"text":"The two terms name patterns across abilities.","children":["In a **dissociation**, one ability is impaired while another is spared.","In a **double dissociation**, one patient loses the first ability and keeps the second, and another patient has the reverse.","A double dissociation is strong evidence that the two abilities depend on **separate systems**.","With syntax, word knowledge and meaning treated as separate parts, a patient can be impaired in one part while the others work."]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia damages the grammatical side of speech, and Wernicke’s aphasia leaves speech fluent but full of wrong words.** These are the classic profiles described by **Fromkin (1997)**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** has three marks in speech.","children":["There are **word-finding pauses**.","There is **loss of grammatical morphemes** (small words or word parts that mark a grammatical relationship, such as “the,” “is” or past-tense “-ed”).","Quite often there is **disturbed word order**.","Comprehension of everyday conversation gives the impression of being generally good, but **controlled testing reveals considerable impairments**."]},{"text":"**Agrammatism** (difficulty producing or understanding grammatical structure) is almost a synonym for Broca’s aphasia.","children":["Some patients with lesions in Broca’s area are **not agrammatic**.","Some agrammatic patients would **not be classified neurologically as Broca’s aphasics**.","So the label names a pattern of behavior that does not always follow damage to Broca’s area."]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** has its own marks.","children":["Speech is **fluent**, with good intonation and pronunciation.","There are **many word substitutions**, some related in meaning to the intended word and some not.","There are **neologisms** (invented words) and **phonological errors** (errors in the sounds of words).","There are **comprehension difficulties**.","Fluency is how smoothly speech flows, which is separate from whether it carries a coherent message."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients keep meaningful words but lose the small grammatical pieces that connect them. Wernicke’s patients keep the grammatical frame but fill it with wrong or invented words.","children":[]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia sounds like fluent word salad with syntax roughly intact, and Broca’s aphasia sounds telegraphic.** This **dichotomy** (a two-way division) is attractive.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**.","children":["Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported it.","Asked about his poor vision, another said “My wires don’t hire right.”","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a fork “a need for a schedule” and a spoon “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** is **“telegraphic” speech**, with difficulty with **word order and grammatical particles** and relative sparing of important **content words**.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported it.","Content words carry the story and the connecting grammatical words are missing."]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"**Broca’s own patients would probably be classified today as having apraxia of speech and not Broca’s aphasia.** The dichotomy says Wernicke’s aphasia is **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon** and Broca’s aphasia is **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words.","**Activation** makes a candidate word available.","**Inhibition** suppresses competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"That is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. He described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”**","children":["Tan could produce only **one jargon syllable**, “tan.”","He seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses.","He seemed to **understand what was being said**."]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["Intact senses matter because they mean the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness” (a phrenology term for love of one’s children).","Examining Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic levels of description."]},{"text":"This is confusing.","children":["Broca’s actual patients were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control problems**.","They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech** (a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak).","Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"**The aphasia dichotomy is attractive because formal linguistics also separates the grammar from the lexicon.** Late 20th-century formal linguistics drew a **sharp distinction** between the two.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences.","The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized.","Wernicke’s aphasia would be a disorder of the lexicon.","Broca’s aphasia would be a disorder of the grammar."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**.","children":["We do not recognize a new sentence because it matches some familiar item in any simple way.","We recognize it because it is **generated by the grammar** each person has internalized.","A sentence never heard before can be understood because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave compelling demonstrations of the **independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","“colorless green ideas sleep furiously” makes no sense, but it is **grammatical** because its word order follows English grammar.","“furiously sleep ideas green colorless” is **not grammatical** because the same words come in an order the grammar does not allow.","Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference comes from **grammar as a separate system**."]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"**In the speech production model, the lexical side is at the functional level and the grammatical side is at the positional level.** The dichotomy fits this difference.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has five levels.","children":["The **message level** is conceptual. The speaker chooses a story angle and arouses concepts of **referents** (the people, objects or events being talked about) and event structure.","The **functional level** is the first linguistic level. It arouses word meanings and **semantic roles** (the parts participants play in an event, such as doer or receiver).","The **positional level** is the second linguistic level. It gets word forms in grammatically appropriate order.","The **phonological encoding level** supplies the sounds of the words.","The **speech gesture level** handles **articulation** (the physical movements of speaking)."]},{"text":"The two key levels work differently.","children":["At the functional level, **lemmas** (lexicon entries linking a word’s meaning to its grammatical properties) have an **arbitrary** relationship to their referents. There is no reason a dog must be called “dog.”","At the positional level, syntactic trees and **affixes** are the product of **abstract syntactic processes**. Affixes are word parts added to a stem, such as “-ed” or “-ly.”"]},{"text":"One objection is that **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** have meanings that are clearly not arbitrary, because they are built from parts.","children":["Speech errors are evidence that **derivational morphology** (building new words from existing parts) is done at the positional level.","In “easy enoughly,” the speaker meant “easily enough,” but “-ly” attached to the wrong word.","The suffix moved as a separate piece, so a grammatical operation added it."]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"**Broca’s patients may generally understand what is said even though they have a core grammatical deficit.** This is possible because conversation gives cues other than grammar.","children":["In ordinary conversation, word meanings, familiar events and context all point toward the same message.","A listener can use these cues without fully computing the grammar.","So a patient can succeed without parsing when meaning and plausibility point toward the right answer."]},{"text":"Popular theories of **sentence processing** say syntactic structure is computed **independently of meaning** as we listen or read.","children":["An autonomous **parser** builds the structure first.","Meaning is worked out afterward."]},{"text":"Isolating these **“parsing” mechanisms** requires **carefully constructed stimulus materials**.","children":["The test sentence is “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite.”","In the **preferred** structure, “with the dynamite” attaches to the **verb phrase**, so the dynamite is the instrument.","In the **unpreferred** structure, it attaches to the **noun phrase** “the safe,” so it tells which safe.","The words are the same and only the attachment changes, so parsing is tested without changing word meanings."]},{"text":"A real test of a parsing deficit has one requirement.","children":["The grammatical relationships cannot be guessable from the most likely event.","**Reversible sentences**, where either person could plausibly do the action, force the listener to use grammar."]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"**To establish that a patient’s difficulty is more generally with syntax, researchers test comprehension.** A comprehension test works for two reasons.","children":["The patient can respond without speaking, so motor problems are removed from the test.","If the patient still struggles to understand grammatical relationships, the deficit **goes beyond articulation**."]},{"text":"**None of this theory was available to Broca in the 1860s.**","children":["For him, it was interesting enough to **dissociate “articulate speech” from comprehension**.","Leborgne could produce only one syllable, yet he seemed to understand what was said to him.","Speaking and understanding coming apart was the key finding at the time."]},{"text":"The modern question is different.","children":["A **syntactic impairment** concerns the organization of grammatical relationships.","Such an impairment should affect **both producing and understanding** sentences.","So the question of whether the difficulty lies in grammar itself requires testing comprehension of specific structures."]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"**Comprehension deficits in Broca’s patients matter because agrammatic speech alone could be only a motor problem.** Three ideas lead to that worry.","children":["**Broca’s aphasia is almost always accompanied by some level of motor dysfunction.**","Agrammatic speech might merely reflect **an attempt to communicate despite motor limitations**. A patient who finds speaking effortful might drop small grammatical words and keep informative content words.","**Hughlings Jackson** said we are observing **what the individual can do despite the impairment**. Agrammatic speech after injury does not literally mean the grammar itself is injured.","Caramazza and Zurif (1976) cite Lenneberg (1973) for a version of this. The strain of speaking is so great that the patient **speaks asyntactically to economize effort**."]},{"text":"**Chomsky’s distinction between competence and performance** applies.","children":["**Competence** is a person’s underlying knowledge of language.","**Performance** is the use of that knowledge, which memory limits, distraction or motor problems can disrupt.","When motor deficits obviously interfere with performance, **comprehension may provide a clearer window into competence**."]},{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** tested comprehension for this reason.","children":["If Broca’s aphasia is a motor problem, comprehension of grammatical structure should be normal.","If it is damage to grammar, comprehension should also suffer.","Both explanations predict telegraphic speech, so only comprehension can tell them apart."]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"**Psycholinguistic tests are needed because ordinary sentences can be understood correctly without any grammar.** In conversation, there are lots of **redundant cues** (several sources of information that all point to the same meaning) to what a speaker intends.","children":["“Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of an unexpected event."]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” gives many ways to get the right meaning.","children":["The listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN** into an event representation, with or without syntax. Dogs bite people far more often than the reverse.","The sentence has **canonical word order**, which is agent, verb, undergoer, or subject, verb, object.","The **agent** is the doer and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English the first noun is usually the agent.","The **discourse context** (the surrounding conversation) clarifies a lot."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients’ comprehension may depend on these **compensatory heuristics** (mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness elsewhere).","children":["A patient might assume the most plausible event.","A patient might assume the first noun is the doer.","Either shortcut gets “The dog bit the man” right with no grammatical analysis."]},{"text":"A better test sentence is “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["The **man** is the biter, although the dog comes first and dogs are likelier biters.","Only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif wrote four groups of sentences that differ in how much meaning helps the listener.** Each group has eight sentences.","children":["**A, control sentences**, are simple and in canonical order. Three are “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”","**B, center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, make the roles obvious from meaning. Three are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.”","**C, reversible center-embedded sentences**, let either participant do the action. Three are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.”","**D, improbable center-embedded sentences**, describe events against expectations. Three are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.”"]},{"text":"A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause in the middle of the main clause.","children":["In every embedded sentence, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the relative clause.","In B, apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone gives the roles.","In C, only grammar gives the roles.","In D, common sense points to the wrong answer."]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should pass A and B and fail C and D.","children":["A listener who parses normally should pass all four."]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"**Broca’s patients failed only when grammar was the sole guide to who did what to whom.** **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task** (hearing a sentence and choosing which picture fits it).","children":["Accuracy tells whether the listener understood who did what to whom, with no spoken answer required.","Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia heard all four sentence types."]},{"text":"Each group had its own pattern.","children":["**Healthy controls** were more or less **equally good** at all stimulus types, near the maximum.","**Wernicke’s patients** were **poor overall**, even on simple control sentences. This is probably a result of their comprehension deficits, which affect words as well as grammar.","**Broca’s patients** scored **high** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning supplies the answer.","**Broca’s patients** scored **low** on reversible and improbable sentences, where syntax conflicted with common sense or probable semantic roles."]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","children":["The deficit appeared in understanding as well as speaking, which places it in the grammar itself.","The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.”","Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** process (grammatical analysis).","They keep the **heuristic** shortcuts (based on meaning)."]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"**A specific theory of the parts of language lets researchers isolate grammar problems from lexicon problems.** This opens new ways of exploring **structure-function relationships** (links between a brain structure and the function it supports).","children":["Before such theories, aphasias could only be described in broad terms.","The old divisions were good or poor comprehension and more or less fluent speech.","The new approach isolates problems with **particular aspects of language**."]},{"text":"Two patients make the difference concrete.","children":["One patient might know word meanings well but assign the wrong roles in complex sentences.","Another patient might produce a fluent grammatical frame filled with words that miss the intended message.","A single fluency rating would hide the difference between them."]},{"text":"The key division is **the grammar versus the lexicon**.","children":["**Grammar** concerns the rules that relate words to one another in a sentence.","The **lexicon** concerns stored words and the ability to retrieve the right ones.","A **grammar deficit** predicts trouble with reversible sentences but not with word meanings.","A **lexical deficit** predicts trouble with word meanings and naming but not with grammatical structure.","Controlled tasks can test whether one operation is impaired and relate it to a particular brain region."]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"**Dick and colleagues described one event with four sentence types, and only two of them put the doer first.** This study begins the **problems with the dichotomy**.","children":["Participants listened to a sentence and had to **indicate which animal is doing the action**.","A picture of a dog and a picture of a cat appeared on a computer screen.","Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","The event was always a dog biting a cat."]},{"text":"Two types put the **doer first**.","children":["**Active** is “The dog is biting the cat.”","**Subject cleft** is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.”","A **cleft** splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft the focused noun is the doer.","Choosing the first animal mentioned gives the **right** answer."]},{"text":"Two types put the **receiver first**.","children":["**Object cleft** is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” The focused noun is the one being bitten.","**Passive** is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","Choosing the first animal mentioned gives the **wrong** answer."]},{"text":"The types separate two kinds of listener.","children":["A listener who always picks the first animal passes actives and subject clefts and fails object clefts and passives.","A listener who parses the grammar passes all four."]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"**Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with object clefts and passives, but their performance was not great overall.** Aphasia patients and healthy aging controls did the task, and the pattern was **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped with the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":["**Elderly controls** scored close to 100% on all four types.","**Anomic** patients (main problem is word finding) scored about 89% on actives and subject clefts, about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","**Conduction** patients scored about 75% on actives and subject clefts and about 42% on object clefts.","**Broca’s** patients scored about 61% on actives, 65% on subject clefts, 42% on object clefts and 54% on passives.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"Object clefts and passives are the **syntactically complex** stimuli.","children":["They are **noncanonical** (they depart from the usual doer-first order)."]},{"text":"Two things are true of the Broca’s group at once.","children":["Even on simple actives, they were far below the elderly controls.","They paid an extra cost on complex structures.","So the pattern is a **general drop** plus an **extra cost** for complex structures.","The groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop."]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"**Healthy undergraduates under stress make the same errors on object clefts and passives that Broca’s patients make.** The same task was run under **conditions that stress the language system**.","children":["One stressor was **compressed** speech (speech played faster than normal).","One was noise.","One was a **low-pass filter** (removes the higher frequencies, so speech sounds muffled).","Two were **auditory digits** and **visual digits** (number lists to remember while listening, a secondary task that uses up memory resources)."]},{"text":"The cost fell on the complex sentences.","children":["With a **single stressor**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts.","With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts and about 91% on passives.","With **two stressors**, the effects grew larger.","With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts.","It was about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives."]},{"text":"**Object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations.**","children":["These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge.","Stress alone produced an aphasia-like profile.","So the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia **may be a “performance” issue after all**.","Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction hurts them first.","The patients may keep their grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"**Broca’s aphasics had difficulty matching an action to an object whether the stimuli were linguistic or not.** **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** used this task to ask whether Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are **language-specific**.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking.","Participants chose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words."]},{"text":"Aphasic patients made more errors than controls in both versions.","children":["Non-linguistic accuracy was **98.5%** for controls and **93.4%** for patients.","Linguistic accuracy was **99.4%** for controls and **87.5%** for patients."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the **distractor** (the type of wrong choice offered).","children":["With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors (related in meaning to the correct object), controls scored 99.2% and patients 86.3%.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, such as a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The non-linguistic version removes language but keeps meaning.","children":["A pantomimed action must be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb is linked to its object.","A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on it.","Difficulty on it points to a **broader problem with action meanings**, shared with language."]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area.** So damage there **affects more than syntax**.","children":["Lesion maps mark the areas linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**.","The maps cover three horizontal slices of the brain.","Colored regions mark where damage went with poorer performance, and red and yellow mark the strongest relationships.","The strongest associations are in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area."]},{"text":"The task did not require language.","children":["Participants only interpreted a pantomimed action and chose an object.","They did not produce a sentence.","They did not work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"Two things follow about Broca’s area.","children":["Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","Broca’s area supports **action understanding** as well as grammatical processing.","Any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients were particularly bad at matching sounds to pictures, and equally bad with words and with environmental sounds.** **Saygin and colleagues** used this task to ask whether Wernicke’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are **language-specific**.","children":["In the **nonverbal** version, a speaker played an environmental sound, such as a cow mooing. Participants chose between pictures such as a cow and a sheep.","In the **verbal** version, the speaker played a spoken phrase, such as “cow mooing.” Participants chose between pictures such as a cow and a violin."]},{"text":"The groups differed in overall accuracy.","children":["**Controls** scored 99.2% verbal and 98.1% nonverbal.","**Right hemisphere damage** patients scored 99.1% and 98.2%.","**Anomic** patients scored 98.6% and 96.7%.","**Broca’s** patients scored 96.3% and 95.2%.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored **90.0%** verbal and **91.3%** nonverbal."]},{"text":"**No significant difference** was observed between the verbal and nonverbal versions.","children":["Wernicke’s patients struggled as much with an animal call as with a spoken phrase.","So the deficit goes beyond understanding spoken words.","The difficulty concerns **linking sounds of any kind to their meanings**, through language or through ordinary sounds."]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"**Deficits in both verbal and nonverbal sound-picture matching are associated with Wernicke’s area.** Lesion overlays mark where damage occurred in the patients impaired on each version.","children":["**Eight** patients were impaired in the **nonverbal** version.","**Ten** patients were impaired in the **verbal** version.","Red and yellow mark areas damaged in the largest number of patients.","In both groups, the greatest overlap is in the **posterior temporal area of the left hemisphere**, around Wernicke’s area.","So both impairments come from damage to the **same general region**."]},{"text":"The two versions differ in input and share one demand.","children":["A spoken phrase conveys the meaning through words.","An animal call conveys the meaning without language.","Both require connecting meaningful sounds to what they represent.","Damage around Wernicke’s area disrupts that connection in both cases."]},{"text":"Wernicke’s area appears to support **meaningful sound processing in general**, with language as one important case.","children":[]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"**How language-specific the deficits in aphasia are is still a controversy.** Two kinds of finding pull in different directions.","children":["Some patients are especially vulnerable to complex grammatical structures. This suggests some aspects of language are handled in **specialized** ways.","The same patients can struggle with action meanings or environmental sounds with no language involved. This suggests language **shares brain systems** with other kinds of meaning.","An explanation has to account for both.","If the deficits were purely linguistic, language would appear to have its own dedicated brain systems."]},{"text":"What is clear is **how the terms of the debate have evolved**.","children":["There are **relatively sophisticated tests** that tap specific aspects of language, such as reversible and improbable sentences, cleft sentences and nonverbal matching tasks.","There are **quantitative analyses of behavior**, measuring accuracy across sentence types, patient groups and stress conditions.","There are **quantitative analyses of structure-function relationships**, relating patterns of brain damage to performance across many patients."]},{"text":"The field has come **a long way from the 19th century**.","children":["Wernicke and Lichtheim drew boxes and arrows from a small number of individual cases.","Modern research tests which specific operations are impaired and maps them onto damaged tissue across large groups of patients."]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"**Across many patients, damage toward the front goes with fluency deficits and damage toward the back goes with comprehension deficits.** **Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping** produced this result by formalizing **structure-function relationships** with MRI data.","children":["The method comes from Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers.","A **voxel** is a small three-dimensional unit of an MRI brain image, like a 3D pixel.","At each voxel, patients with damage there are compared with patients without damage there.","If the damaged patients score much worse, that voxel is associated with the symptom.","Colors run from blue for a weaker association to red for a stronger one."]},{"text":"The **“symptoms”** are **defined very broadly**.","children":["**Fluency deficits** are tied most strongly to the front part of the left hemisphere.","**Comprehension deficits** are tied most strongly to the posterior temporal region, farther back."]},{"text":"The data are consistent with a **rough double dissociation**.","children":["**Posterior regions** are associated with **perception**, or comprehension.","**Anterior regions** are associated with **production**, or fluency.","Each patient contributes a damage pattern and a continuous score, so many individual cases become one statistical map."]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"**Nativists say children acquire grammar because they are born with knowledge of language, not because of reward and punishment.** This position grew out of Chomsky's critique of Skinner. The debate concerns **nature and nurture in child language** (how much of language is built in and how much comes from experience).","children":["**Skinner** was a behaviorist who explained behavior, including language, with general learning mechanisms such as reward and punishment.","**Chomsky** argued that these mechanisms could not explain how children acquire grammar."]},{"text":"For Chomsky, the important feature of language is its **generativity** (the ability to produce and understand an unlimited number of new sentences).","children":["Acquiring a language means **identifying the abstract grammar** that produces the sentences of the language."]},{"text":"General learning mechanisms were **not up to this task**.","children":["Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are **not \"similar\"** in any straightforward way.","Acquisition does not look like **operant conditioning** (shaping behavior with rewards and punishments). Children are not reliably rewarded for grammatical sentences, so the **contingencies do not hold**."]},{"text":"Nativist approaches assert two things instead.","children":["Children have innate knowledge of what language is like, called **Universal Grammar**.","Children use **language-specific acquisition processes** (learning mechanisms built only for language)."]},{"text":"Chomsky argued for this position by writing, \"The fact that all normal children acquire essentially comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity suggests that human beings are somehow specially designed to do this, with data-handling or 'hypothesis-formulating' ability of unknown character and complexity.\"","children":["Every typical child reaches a complex grammar quickly, despite very different experiences."]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"**Categorical perception first looked like something only humans do, and only for speech.** **Categorical perception** (hearing differences between speech sounds in terms of categories) was treated by early theories as language-specific and species-specific.","children":["**Language-specific** means it was thought to occur only for language.","**Species-specific** means it was thought to occur only in humans.","At the time, only humans were known to show it, and only for speech sounds."]},{"text":"The classic evidence is a synthetic consonant continuum of fourteen stimuli.","children":["The upper formant transitions change in equal steps. The lower formant stays the same.","**Labeling** switches sharply from **/b/** to **/d/** around stimuli 4 and 5.","Labeling switches from /d/ to **/g/** around stimuli 9 and 10.","**Discrimination** accuracy peaks near those boundaries and falls inside each category, for one-step, two-step and three-step pairs."]},{"text":"Similarity between stimuli is defined in **linguistic, rather than physical, terms**.","children":["Two sounds in the same category can sound almost identical.","Two sounds on opposite sides of a boundary can sound clearly different.","The acoustic difference can be the same size in both cases.","These patterns were taken as signs of a specialized speech system."]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"**Infants cannot say what they hear, so researchers measure head turns and sucking instead.** Both behaviors change when an infant notices something new.","children":["Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","The experimenter controls exactly which acoustic difference is presented."]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** (training an infant to turn toward a sound when it changes) is used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap.","An experimenter keeps the infant's attention forward with toys.","A correct head turn after a change earns a visual reward, such as a lighted, moving toy.","After the infant learns this link, a head turn means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** relies on the infant's natural interest in something new.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier connected to a pressure recorder.","Strong sucks make a sound play.","As one sound repeats, sucking slows because of **habituation** (getting used to a repeated sound).","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Both methods test whether infants hear the same distinctions that adults hear.","children":["The head turn relies on a learned link between a sound change and a reward.","Sucking relies on a natural response to something new."]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"**Four-month-old infants notice a change in voice onset time only when it crosses an adult category boundary.** **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found this with high-amplitude sucking.","children":["The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or VOT (the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration).","With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than with +10 milliseconds.","Infants heard one syllable repeatedly until sucking declined. Then the sound changed or stayed the same."]},{"text":"Three conditions were compared.","children":["**20D** had sounds 20 milliseconds apart, +20 versus +40, from **different** adult phonemic categories.","**20S** had sounds 20 milliseconds apart, −20 versus 0 or +60 versus +80, from the **same** category.","**0** was the **control**, with the same sound throughout."]},{"text":"Sucking rose above baseline and then declined before the change in every condition.","children":["In 20D, sucking **jumped back up**, to around 50 responses per minute.","In 20S, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling.","In the control, sucking kept declining, to about 15 responses per minute."]},{"text":"The 20D and 20S changes were the same acoustic size.","children":["So the difference comes from **crossing the category boundary**.","The control establishes what repetition alone does."]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"**Categorical perception at four months seemed like evidence for innateness because it appears so early.** Eimas's result was a big deal for that reason.","children":["Categorical perception was considered the first step in turning speech into a **symbolic format** (one that represents a sound as a category, such as /b/).","Later linguistic processes could then use that format.","Finding it in infants suggested the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"Four-month-olds are **really little**.","children":["Gross motor milestones (large body movements) at that age include manipulating objects with two hands.","They also include holding the head and chest up while prone (lying face down).","Color vision and general **visual acuity** (sharpness of vision) are still \"in progress.\"","They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"This is very, very early for an apparently **language-specific** ability to emerge.","children":["Infants categorize speech sounds before they can produce them.","Their other abilities are still immature at that age.","So the ability seemed built in, not learned through long experience."]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"**English-learning infants can hear non-English contrasts at six months and mostly lose them by twelve months.** This decline is **perceptual narrowing** (losing sensitivity to sound distinctions the child's language does not use).","children":["At around six months, English-learning infants discriminate speech contrasts they have never heard before.","As development goes on, the ability to perceive these contrasts declines."]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested two non-English contrasts.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is between two \"ta\"-like sounds.","The **Salish contrast** is between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language.","The measure was the percentage of infants reaching a **discrimination criterion**."]},{"text":"The percentage dropped with age.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, about **95%** reached it for Hindi and about **80%** for Salish.","At 8 to 10 months, about 67% reached it for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At 10 to 12 months, about **20%** reached it for Hindi and about **10%** for Salish.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants reached it close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"This is **perceptual reorganization** during the first year of life.","children":["Infants who hear a contrast keep it, so the decline depends on language experience.","Narrowing is related to categorical perception because native categories form. Sounds the language groups together become harder to tell apart.","A nonnative distinction inside one native category is then heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"**The compelling narrative says infants are born able to hear every contrast, and learning only removes the ones they do not need.** It combines the infant findings into one account.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a special property of the language system.","These abilities are present very early in development."]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world\"** (able to hear all contrasts).","children":["This means they have innate, **language-general** knowledge about speech (knowledge that applies to the speech sounds of every language).","A newborn is ready to learn any human language.","Children then **rapidly tune** their perceptual systems to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"The role of learning is to **select** among the many possible innately specified phonetic contrasts.","children":["Learning narrows the **inventory** (the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive).","Learning removes the contrasts the native language does not use.","Learning keeps the contrasts that are meaningful in the native language.","The native language decides which innate categories survive."]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"**The citizens-of-the-world view has four problems, three about categorical perception and one about infants.**","children":["Categorical perception is **not limited to language**. It also occurs for nonspeech sounds.","Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**. Animals without language also perceive speech sounds categorically.","Categorical perception is **task-dependent** (results look more or less categorical depending on the task). The task could be naming a sound or judging whether two sounds differ.","Infant speech perception is **not actually \"universal.\"**"]},{"text":"Infants do not start out able to hear every contrast equally well.","children":["Some contrasts are hard for young infants to detect.","This is true even for contrasts in their own language."]},{"text":"Each problem targets a different part of the narrative.","children":["Nonspeech and animal findings question whether categorical perception is special to language.","Task effects question how categorical perception really is.","Uneven infant sensitivity questions whether all contrasts are innately available.","A full account needs learning that **builds categories**, not only learning that removes them."]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"**Listeners perceive non-speech sounds categorically too, so categorical perception does not require a speech mechanism.** **Cutting and Rosner (1974)** varied **rise time** (how quickly a sound grows to full loudness after it starts).","children":["A **short rise time** sounds like a **plucked** string.","A **long rise time** sounds like a **bowed** string."]},{"text":"The stimuli were sawtooth waves, speech syllables and sine waves.","children":["Each was made with a 10-millisecond and a 70-millisecond rise time.","A 10-millisecond rise reaches full size almost immediately.","A 70-millisecond rise grows gradually."]},{"text":"The sawtooth results were categorical.","children":["Pluck responses were near 100% at short rise times.","Bow responses were near 100% at long rise times.","The labels switched steeply between about **30 and 50 milliseconds**.","Discrimination of pairs 10 milliseconds apart was near 55 to 60% for most pairs.","Discrimination **peaked near 75%** for pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, where the labels switch."]},{"text":"Pluck and bow are labels for kinds of sound onset, not speech categories.","children":["A label boundary and a discrimination peak appear together without phonemes.","Categorical perception can arise in **general auditory processing**."]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"**Infants also perceive non-speech sounds categorically, so their early categorical perception is a general auditory ability.** **Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested this with high-amplitude sucking.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until sucking declined.","Then the sound was changed."]},{"text":"Three conditions were compared, **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**.","children":["Before the change, sucking fell from near 20 per minute to about 12 in all three.","After no change, sucking stayed near 12.","After a within-category change, sucking stayed near 12.","After a between-category change, sucking **jumped back up** to about 18 and then declined again."]},{"text":"Infants responded more to a change that crossed a category boundary.","children":["Eimas and colleagues found the same pattern for speech.","Adults show categorical labeling and discrimination for plucked and bowed sounds.","Infants show categorical discrimination for nonspeech changes.","Neither group needs language knowledge to show the pattern."]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"**Chinchillas sort speech sounds into categories the way humans do, so categorical perception of speech is not limited to humans.** The evidence is from **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and earlier from **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**.","children":["The animals were trained with **operant conditioning** (a behavior becomes more likely when a reward follows it).","Each learned that the 0-millisecond lag or the 80-millisecond lag stimulus was an **SD** for food.","SD means **discriminative stimulus** (a signal that a response will be rewarded).","The lag is the **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, the animals heard **intermediate VOTs** along the /ga/-/ka/ continuum.","children":["Human listeners heard the same sounds.","At short VOTs, the proportion labeled /ga/ stayed near 1.","At long VOTs, the proportion labeled /ga/ fell to near 0.","The fall was steep, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds.","Chinchilla labeling had the **same steep, categorical shape** as human labeling."]},{"text":"Increasing the intensity of the **burst** and **aspiration** noise changed labeling.","children":["The burst is the brief noise at a stop release. Aspiration is the breathy noise after a voiceless release.","The boundary shifted to a **shorter VOT**, the switch became steeper, or both.","This happened for one chinchilla and one human listener.","Both species responded to these acoustic properties in a similar way."]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"**Four-month-olds do not readily hear the /na/-/ŋa/ contrast, so some contrasts are less salient than others.** **Narayan and colleagues (2010)** compared it with /ma/-/na/, which is relatively **salient** (easy to notice).","children":["Both contrasts use **nasal consonants** in the **onset** (the start of a syllable).","The consonants differ in **place of articulation** (where in the mouth the sound is made).","/m/ is made with the lips.","/n/ is made with the tongue behind the teeth.","/ŋ/, the final sound in \"sing,\" is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate."]},{"text":"Looking time was compared on **same** trials (the syllable did not change) and **change** trials (the syllable switched).","children":["Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change.","English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months were tested.","For native **[ma]-[na]**, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials.","For nonnative **[na]-[ŋa]**, they looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials."]},{"text":"The /na/-/ŋa/ contrast is not readily perceived by four-month-olds, **independent of language experience**.","children":["The difference lies in the sounds themselves, not in experience.","Some contrasts are hard to hear from the beginning.","So not every contrast starts out equally available."]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"**Hearing the /na/-/ŋa/ contrast in Filipino makes infants better at it, so not all contrasts are available innately.** **Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found the change between 6 and 10 months.","children":["In Filipino, [na] and [ŋa] are different syllables.","Filipino-learning infants hear the contrast regularly."]},{"text":"Exposure in a Filipino language environment **increases the salience** of the contrast.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, Filipino-learning infants looked about 3.8 seconds on both same and change trials.","At 10 to 12 months, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 4.8 seconds on change trials.","English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months did not respond to this contrast.","Those English-hearing infants did respond to [ma]-[na]."]},{"text":"Exposure to contrasts in the environment is **necessary for acquisition** in some cases.","children":["The contrast is weak at the start, even for infants whose language uses it.","Experience improves sensitivity over the first year.","Learning can strengthen a contrast as well as preserve it.","Development builds sensitivity to hard contrasts and does not only remove unused ones."]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"**Emergentist approaches say language is learned by general associative learning that picks up statistical patterns in the input.** This is the alternative to the nativist approach.","children":["**Associative learning** (learning that two things go together because they repeatedly occur together) is the mechanism.","**Statistical regularities** (patterns in how often things occur and which things occur together) are what it is sensitive to.","Language structure emerges from learning these patterns in rich input."]},{"text":"Classical conditioning is the simplest case.","children":["There are three neurons, one for the **bell**, one that detects **food** and one for **salivation**.","The food detector already connects strongly to salivation, so food produces salivation.","The bell repeatedly occurs with food.","The **synapse** (a connection between neurons) from the bell neuron to the salivation neuron is **strengthened**.","After enough pairings, the bell alone produces salivation."]},{"text":"The same mechanism can extend to **pattern recognition** (identifying a recurring structure, such as a speech sound, across many varied examples).","children":["Strengthening could link acoustic features that tend to occur together.","Repeated co-occurrence could build categories that were never specified in advance.","In conditioning, one event predicts another. In pattern recognition, many features predict a category.","Infants would learn categories by tracking which sounds occur together and how often."]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"**We may learn each vowel by tracking the distribution of its sounds, with the prototype at the center.** When **Kuhl and others** talk about learning the statistics of distributions in the environment, the American vowels are what they mean.","children":["A **distribution** describes how often each value occurs across many examples.","The **vowel space** is determined by the first and second **formants**."]},{"text":"Each vowel occupies a region of the vowel space.","children":["Each production of a vowel has its own first and second formant values.","Many productions of one vowel cluster together.","The regions of different vowels **overlap** heavily.","One pair of formant values can belong to more than one vowel, so speech categorization is difficult.","Listeners must learn where each vowel is centered and how far it spreads."]},{"text":"Maybe we learn vowels by **extracting the statistics** of these distributions.","children":["Over many exposures, the listener tracks where each vowel's productions cluster.","The categories reflect the shape of the input."]},{"text":"A **\"prototypical\" /a/** is the **mean** (the average value) or perhaps the **mode** (the most frequent value) of all the /a/ sounds we hear.","children":["The prototype comes from experience.","It can differ across languages and listeners."]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"**Infants who heard a two-peaked distribution of sounds treated two test sounds as different, and infants who heard one peak did not.** **Maye and colleagues (2002)** had infants **listen passively** (with no task or reward) to a **[da]-[ta] continuum**.","children":["The continuum had eight stimuli that differed in **voice onset time**.","The study asked whether infants really learn categories from distributions."]},{"text":"Two groups heard different distributions.","children":["The **bimodal** group heard stimuli 2 and 7 most often, 16 times each, and the middle rarely. Two peaks are evidence for **two categories**.","The **unimodal** group heard stimuli 4 and 5 most often and the ends rarely. One peak is evidence for a **single category**."]},{"text":"At test, infants heard **stimuli 3 and 6**.","children":["In **alternating** trials, the two stimuli switched back and forth.","In **non-alternating** trials, one stimulus repeated.","If 3 and 6 sound different to infants, the two kinds of trials should hold attention differently."]},{"text":"Only the **bimodal** infants were sensitive to the difference between trial types.","children":["Bimodal six-month-olds looked 5.66 seconds on alternating and 6.41 seconds on non-alternating trials.","Bimodal eight-month-olds looked 5.45 and 6.15 seconds.","Unimodal six-month-olds looked 4.85 and 4.53 seconds.","Unimodal eight-month-olds looked 4.98 and 5.20 seconds.","The unimodal infants acted as if stimuli 3 and 6 were **members of the same category**.","A few minutes of exposure changed how infants grouped the sounds."]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"**Infants learn speech sounds better from live, responsive people than from recordings.** **Social interaction** is important for learning.","children":["Nine-month-old American infants were exposed to **Mandarin Chinese** in twelve 25-minute sessions.","The **live** group had a Mandarin speaker playing and reading with them in person.","The **auditory or audiovisual** groups heard or watched Mandarin on audio or television.","A control group had English exposure.","All were tested on a Mandarin contrast with the **head-turn technique**."]},{"text":"Only live exposure produced learning.","children":["Live-exposure infants scored about **65% correct**. English controls scored about 56%.","TV and audio infants scored about the same as controls, close to chance."]},{"text":"The second experiment manipulated **social responses** to eight-month-olds' vocalizations.","children":["In the **contingent** condition, mothers responded right after the infant vocalized.","In the **yoked-control** condition, mothers responded on a schedule copied from another infant, so responses were **noncontingent**.","Familiarization lasted 30 minutes, then baseline, social response and **extinction** (responses stopped) lasted 10 minutes each."]},{"text":"Contingent feedback **increased the quantity and complexity** of vocalizations.","children":["At baseline, both groups produced few syllables.","During social response, the contingent group reached about **0.32** and the noncontingent group stayed near 0.05.","During extinction, the contingent group was about 0.2 and the noncontingent group about 0.1."]},{"text":"Contingent social interaction seems to work like a **reinforcing stimulus** (something that makes a behavior more likely when it follows it).","children":["One idea is that humans find social interaction innately reinforcing.","Another idea is that we are innately good at using **social cues** to learn from other people."]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"**Statistical learning is guided by constraints, and constraints means at least three different things.** **Statistical learning with constraints** is learning from patterns in the input that is guided and limited.","children":["The first constraint is on which stimulus properties can be **contrastive** (able to distinguish words, such as voicing in /b/ versus /p/).","**Speaker** identity and **speech rate** change the sound but not the word, so they are not contrastive.","A learner tracking every pattern would form categories for different voices and speeds.","Learning has to focus on the properties that matter for meaning."]},{"text":"The second constraint is **based on earlier learning**.","children":["What is already acquired shapes what is easy to learn next.","Once native categories are in place, distinctions that cut across them are harder to notice and learn.","This kind is central to **sensitive periods** (times when earlier experience limits later learning)."]},{"text":"The third constraint is the **social and interactive context** for learning.","children":["Sounds are learned more effectively in live, responsive interaction than from a recording.","Contingent responses from caregivers shape infants' vocal behavior.","Together the constraints explain why learning is fast and focuses on the distinctions that matter."]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"**Speech has no physical boundaries between words, yet listeners find the words anyway.** The job of finding them is **word segmentation** (working out where one word stops and the next one starts in continuous speech).","children":["In “I hope it’s in my desk,” the sound energy of each word flows straight into the next word.","No silent gap separates the words."]},{"text":"The pauses in the speech stream come from **closures** (moments when the vocal tract is fully blocked, as for /p/, /t/, /d/ and /k/).","children":["Very little sound escapes during a closure, so a brief silence appears.","Closures are **as likely to happen within words as between words**.","In “hope” and “it’s,” the silences fall inside words or at their edges, so they do not match word boundaries.","Treating every silence as a boundary would split some words and merge others."]},{"text":"We are still **really good at finding words** in this “sea of sounds.”","children":["Adults hear separate words with no effort and can lean on the words they already know.","The **harder question** is **how we learn to do this**.","Infants have to discover the words in the stream before they can learn what the words mean."]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"**Several cues point to word boundaries, and no single cue works for every word.**","children":["Some words **appear in isolation**, as in “Hello,” “No,” “Please,” “Go” and “Stop.”","Not all words are heard alone, so this cue covers only part of the vocabulary."]},{"text":"**Phonotactics** (the patterns for which sound sequences a language allows) is a second cue. It works because **some sequences of phonemes are very unlikely to occur within words**.","children":["In “wordboundary,” /db/ almost never occurs inside an English word, so it suggests a break between word and boundary.","In “speechcode,” /tʃk/ suggests a break between speech and code.","In “movement,” /vm/ might suggest a break, but movement is one word."]},{"text":"**Stress** (emphasis that makes a syllable louder, longer or higher in pitch) is a third cue. It works because **in English most words are stressed on the first syllable**.","children":["**Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** familiarized **7.5-month-old infants** with one word and then played passages.","The kingdom passage repeats “kingdom,” as in “Your kingdom is in a faraway place.”","The device passage repeats “device,” as in “Your device can do a lot.”","Infants familiarized with “kingdom” (first-syllable stress) **showed a preference for the kingdom passage**.","Infants familiarized with “device” (second-syllable stress) **did not show the same kind of preference**.","An infant who starts a word at each stressed syllable finds kingdom but cuts device in the wrong place.","Stress **may help signal the beginnings of words**, but not in every language or for every English word."]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"**Syllables inside a word predict each other much more strongly than syllables across two words.** This is the cue from **transitional probabilities**, and it is found through **statistical learning** (picking up patterns in how often things occur together).","children":["Consider “pretty baby.”","In English words, the syllable **/ti/** is very strongly predicted by the syllable **/prɪ/**.","After “pre,” the next syllable is very often “ty,” because pretty is a common word.","Many different words can follow “ty,” so “ba” is poorly predicted."]},{"text":"The comparison can be written with frequencies of occurrence.","children":["The frequency of “pretty” divided by the frequency of “pre” is the chance that “ty” follows “pre.”","The frequency of “tyba” divided by the frequency of “ty” is the chance that “ba” follows “ty.”","The first value is **much greater** than the second."]},{"text":"A learner who counts how often each syllable follows each other syllable can find the joints in speech.","children":["Pairs inside a word recur together again and again.","Pairs across a word boundary recur much less reliably."]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"**Syllable B is more likely to follow syllable A within a word than between words, but only as a matter of probability.** That likelihood is the **transitional probability** from A to B.","children":["Inside a word, the same syllables always come in the same order, so the probability is high.","At a word boundary, almost any word can come next, so the probability is lower.","A drop in transitional probability is a good guess for a word boundary."]},{"text":"The cue is **probabilistic**, so it works most of the time and fails some of the time.","children":["“What’s that in the road a/head?” can be heard as “a head” or as “ahead.”","The sounds are identical, so the meaning of the whole sentence decides the boundary.","“I scream you scream we all scream for ice cream” plays on “I scream” and “ice cream” having almost the same sounds.","One sequence of sounds can be split into words two ways."]},{"text":"Transitional probabilities work alongside stress, phonotactics and words heard alone.","children":["When the cues agree, segmentation is easy.","When the cues clash or are missing, listeners fall back on context."]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996) removed every cue to word boundaries except transitional probability.** They wanted to find out whether **transitional probabilities** alone can be used to identify words, so the stimuli were **tightly controlled**.","children":["The speech was **monotone** (no changes in pitch, duration or amplitude), so stress could not mark word beginnings. Amplitude is loudness.","The syllables were **simple CV syllables** (one consonant followed by one vowel), so there were no phonotactic cues.","The syllables were **presented continuously** (no pauses), so silence could not mark boundaries."]},{"text":"They created an **artificial language** from a set of **three-syllable “words,”** such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila.","children":["The words were joined in random order into one long stream.","The only clue to where a word began and ended was the transitional probability between syllables.","Infants who find the words under these conditions must be using transitional probabilities alone."]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"**The artificial stream has no breaks, so the only signal of the words is the order of the syllables.**","children":["The stream runs like “tokibugikobagopilatipolutokibu” and “gopilatipolutokibugikobagopila” and “gikobatokibugopilatipolugikoba.”","The infant hears exactly this, with no spaces and no pauses.","**Four words** are buried in it, which are tokibu, gikoba, gopila and tipolu.","Each word recurs many times in random order."]},{"text":"The syllables follow **regular patterns** even with no marked boundaries.","children":["Inside **tokibu**, “to” is always followed by “ki.”","Inside tokibu, “ki” is always followed by “bu.”","After “bu,” the stream may go on with “gi,” “go” or “ti,” depending on the next word."]},{"text":"Some syllable pairs are **fully predictable** and others are not.","children":["A learner who tracks which syllables follow which can discover this.","The words come out of the statistics of the sequence."]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"**Each word keeps the same inner order every time, while the words next to it keep changing.**","children":["Marking each word in its own color makes the four words visible to a reader.","Tokibu is red, gikoba is green, gopila is pink and tipolu is purple.","The infant gets no such marking and hears one unbroken run of syllables."]},{"text":"The inner order is fixed and the neighbors vary.","children":["“To-ki-bu” always comes in that order.","Tokibu may be followed by gikoba, gopila or tipolu.","Transitional probabilities are **high inside each word**.","Transitional probabilities are **lower across word edges**."]},{"text":"The word edges are exactly where **transitional probability falls**.","children":["The signal itself contains no spaces.","By noticing where predictability drops, the learner recovers where one word ends and the next begins."]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"**Words were tested against part-words heard equally often, so only transitional probability could tell them apart.**","children":["After a long stream of syllables, infants are trivially more familiar with sequences they heard than with ones they did not.","A preference for heard sequences could come from familiarity alone."]},{"text":"**Two additional details** rule that out.","children":["**Half of the words were repeated more frequently** than the other half.","The familiarity test was against **part-words** (three-syllable sequences that span a word boundary) that were **matched for frequency of occurrence** with real words.","A part-word joins the end of one word to the start of the next."]},{"text":"Take the stream “tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu.”","children":["**GOPILA** and **BU-GIKO** both appear **twice**.","The probability of GI given BU is **2 out of 4**, because BU occurs four times and GI follows it twice.","The transitional probabilities inside GOPILA are both **2 out of 2**, because PI always follows GO and LA always follows PI.","Frequency is equal, so any difference between gopila and bu-giko must come from predictability."]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"**Infants looked longer toward the part-words, so they had learned the statistics and not only the frequencies.** This longer looking is a **novelty effect**.","children":["Words got about 6.8 seconds of looking.","Part-words got about 7.6 seconds of looking.","The difference was statistically significant."]},{"text":"The longer looking is a **novelty preference** (looking longer at whatever seems new).","children":["The words had grown familiar during listening, so they were less interesting.","The part-words felt less familiar, even though infants had heard them just as often."]},{"text":"The pattern suggests that infants **had learned the statistical regularities of the speech stream**.","children":["They were **not merely picking up on simple frequency of occurrence**, because frequency was equal for words and part-words.","Only transitional probabilities separated the two.","After a brief exposure, infants pulled units out of a continuous stream using only how predictable the syllables were."]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"**Finding a word in speech does not tell the child what the word means.** The next problem is **identifying word referents**. A **referent** is whatever a word refers to.","children":["Children can now “find” the words.","They still have to learn what the words mean.","A child may hear “cat” while looking at a cat.","Recognizing the sound pattern “cat” is one achievement.","Knowing that it names the furry animal is a separate achievement."]},{"text":"**Segmentation** delivers a recurring chunk of sound with no information about meaning.","children":["Any scene holds many objects, parts, properties and actions.","The learner needs more evidence to tell which one the speaker has in mind."]},{"text":"Learning a word means **linking its form to the speaker’s intended meaning**.","children":["The child gathers that evidence from the situation in which the word is used."]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"**One experience with a new word fits many possible meanings, so the child cannot tell which one is right.** This is the **inductive problem of early word learning**. **Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and here the rule is a word’s meaning.","children":["A child hears a new word while watching a cat.","It might name a category at some level (**CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**).","It might name this particular cat (**FELIX**).","It might name a property (**FURRY** or **ALIVE**).","It might name an action (**WALKING**).","It might name a part (**WHISKERS** or **LEG**)."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**.","children":["Each candidate fits what the child sees.","Nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends."]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**.","children":["A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","“Gavagai” might mean rabbit, undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast.","Joint attention, cues to the speaker’s intention and default strategies cut down the possibilities."]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"**Joint attention ties a new word to the object the speaker is attending to.** **Joint attention** means sharing an attentional focus with another person.","children":["Two people look at the same object.","Each knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following** (looking where someone else is looking) **emerges around 9 months of age**.","children":["An adult looks over at an object.","The infant follows the adult’s eyes to it."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**.","children":["The infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent.","Joint attention shrinks the hypothesis space (the set of possible meanings) for the word."]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned a new word from an engaged adult but not from a voice behind a screen.** **Baldwin and colleagues** ran this study, and it supports the claim that **joint attention optimizes word learning**.","children":["Two groups of infants heard a novel word while playing with an object.","In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The two conditions matched closely.","children":["In both, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","They differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"The 18-month-olds **learned the novel word in the joint attention group** and **not in the no joint attention group**.","children":["Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough.","Infants needed evidence that a speaker meant the word to refer to that object.","The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, so the difference came from the visible, engaged speaker."]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"**Children narrow the meaning of a new word by using cues to what the speaker intends, such as where the speaker looks.** These are **intentional cues** (signals of what the speaker means to refer to), and they narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words.","children":["**Eye gaze direction** is one intentional cue.","A speaker who looks toward one toy marks that toy as the object of attention."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of 16 to 17 months and of 18 to 19 months in two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on while saying the new word.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket, a different toy, while saying the new word."]},{"text":"Discrepant labeling pulls the infant’s focus apart from the speaker’s focus.","children":["Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy.","Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket.","Comparing the conditions reveals whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of meaning."]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**A child can follow a speaker’s gaze to find which of several objects a new word names.**","children":["A speaker who looks at one object while saying a new word ties the word to that object.","The child follows the speaker’s eyes to the **intended referent** (the thing the speaker means)."]},{"text":"The speaker’s focus can be **different from the child’s own focus**.","children":["Following it means using information about someone else’s attention.","The child can attach a new word to an object it was not looking at when the word was spoken."]},{"text":"**Gaze direction** narrows the possible meanings even though the word’s sound gives no clue.","children":["The child combines the new word with evidence of what the speaker intends.","Following gaze rests on understanding that a speaker’s eyes point to what the speaker means.","The mapping is far more specific than linking the word to everything in view."]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"**Infants use a speaker’s facial expression to work out which object a new word names.** **Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant.","Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult’s expression picked out."]},{"text":"These are intentional cues from the speaker, and they come in two kinds.","children":["**Affective cues** are emotional expressions. An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for.","**Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions. Reaching for something can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"The speaker’s reaction can single out one object among several.","children":["A satisfied expression signals that the speaker found the desired object.","A disappointed expression signals that the object is not the one meant.","Word learning involves grasping the goal behind the interaction."]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"**A speaker’s emotional reaction to each object tells the learner which object the new word is for.** The reaction works as an **intentional cue** (a signal of what the speaker means to refer to).","children":["Satisfaction or excitement can tie one object to what the speaker was trying to find or do.","The learner uses that tie to interpret a new label."]},{"text":"**Intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction**.","children":["Facial expressions also reveal what a speaker means.","Actions also reveal what a speaker means.","A word may be said near several objects that are not all equally likely referents."]},{"text":"The helpful information lies in how the adult responds to each object.","children":["Connecting the label to that response shrinks the word’s **hypothesis space** (the set of meanings it might have).","Infants who use emotional reactions are following the **speaker’s goals**.","The word attaches to the object that meets the goal.","Objects that disappoint the speaker drop out."]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"**Children assume by default that a new word names a whole object.** This is the **whole object assumption**, one of the **word learning strategies**.","children":["Word learning strategies are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**.","A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","The strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words.","They give children a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The whole object assumption favors the object over everything else about it.","children":["The word is not expected to name a part of the object.","The word is not expected to name its color or texture.","The word is not expected to name something it is doing.","A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat favors “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption rules out many unlikely meanings at once and fits how adults usually name objects for children.","children":["Later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties."]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"**Children assume by default that an object has only one name, so a new word goes to an object without a name.** This is **mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**.","children":["As a default, **objects can have only one name**.","The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","It **emerges during the second half of the second year** (roughly 18 to 24 months)."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child hears a new word.","The dog already has a name, so the child takes the word to mean the parrot.","No gaze or emotional cue is needed."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity combines with the whole object assumption.","children":["The whole object assumption says a new word names an object.","Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet.","Later learning can override the default when an object turns out to have two names."]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"**The words a child already knows decide which object gets a new word.** With **mutual exclusivity** (the default that an object has only one name), the child gives an unfamiliar word to an object with no known name.","children":["An object the child can already name is an unlikely target for a new word.","The choice comes from comparing the objects in view."]},{"text":"**Prior vocabulary** determines which objects count as already named.","children":["Two children in the same scene can guess differently if they know different words.","The strategy builds directly on earlier learning."]},{"text":"Vocabulary growth feeds itself.","children":["A child who knows many names can rule out many objects.","The unnamed objects left over become the likeliest referents for new words.","Every word the child knows helps narrow the meaning of the next."]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"**A short utterance can come from several different processes, so length does not reveal how the child built it.** In production, it is difficult to tell what is going on when children produce only one, two or three words at a time.","children":["**Menn** distinguishes among four kinds of early utterance.","**“Just a bunch of relevant words”** is words that fit the situation, with no grammar linking them.","**Formulas** are phrases memorized as whole units, without the child knowing they have separate parts.","**Word combinations** are separate words joined productively into new combinations.","**Stable constructions** are consistent patterns for building utterances, and they are **not necessarily adult-like**."]},{"text":"A child might say “Up!” or “Pick you up!” or “Mommy up!”","children":["“Pick you up!” sounds more advanced but may be a formula copied whole from adult speech.","“Mommy up!” is shorter but may be a real combination of two separate words."]},{"text":"A **full description of the context** in which an utterance is produced is **necessary but often not sufficient** to distinguish among these.","children":["Context reveals what the child intends and how the utterance is used across situations.","Even with complete context, the source of an utterance can remain uncertain."]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"**Children who speak only one word at a time can already use transitive word order to understand sentences.** So **comprehension** runs ahead of **production**.","children":["The **one-word stage** is when a child speaks only in single words.","A **transitive** sentence has a verb with a subject and an object.","English transitive order puts the actor first and the one acted on after the verb."]},{"text":"**Gertner and Fisher** describe this with the **structure-mapping account** of early **syntactic bootstrapping** (using sentence structure to help learn meaning).","children":["The input sentence is “The boy is tickling the girl!”","The child builds a **partial sentence representation**, which is tickle with boy as the first noun and girl as the second noun.","The observed scene is a boy tickling a girl.","The child builds **conceptual representations** of possible events from the scene.","The events are giggle (girl as agent), tickle (boy as agent, girl as patient), play (boy as agent) and play (girl as agent).","An **agent** performs an action, and a **patient** is affected by it.","A **one-to-one mapping between nouns and participant roles** lines up the two nouns with the two participants in the tickling event."]},{"text":"A child who says only single words can use noun order to tell who did what.","children":["Matching nouns to participants also helps the child figure out what the verb means."]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"**Two-year-olds understand a transitive sentence with a made-up verb, so they have learned the structure itself.** This is a **generalization test**. If children have really learned the **transitive structure**, they will generalize it to new verbs.","children":["A child who only memorized particular sentences is lost with an unfamiliar verb.","A child who knows the structure can use word order to interpret any verb."]},{"text":"The sentence is “The duck is gorping the bunny,” and the child must select which of two novel actions it could depict.","children":["“Gorp” is invented, so only noun order can help.","The first noun is the doer and the second noun is the one acted on."]},{"text":"**Gertner and colleagues (2006)** tested two-year-olds.","children":["Children first heard “Hey look! The duck’s gonna gorp the bunny!”","Two scenes appeared side by side while children heard “The duck is gorping the bunny! See?”","In one scene, a person dressed as a duck pushed a person dressed as a bunny in a wagon.","In the other scene, the bunny acted on the duck.","After a short break they heard “The duck gorped the bunny! Find gorping!” and the scenes appeared again.","Children **looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the rabbit** than at the reverse."]},{"text":"At this age, children typically have a **mean length of utterance (MLU)** of **around 2**. MLU is the average number of morphemes (meaningful words or word parts) in each utterance.","children":["Their three-morpheme utterances might be like “Duck gorp bunny.”","Their own speech is short, yet they understand the full transitive structure."]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"**A critical period strictly limits learning to one window, while a sensitive period makes learning best during a window.** Both describe how the timing of experience affects learning.","children":["A **critical period** has a **discrete, “programmed” onset and offset** (the window opens and closes at fixed points in development).","In a critical period, learning (change in response to the environment) is **strictly limited to this period**.","In a critical period, there is **no sensitivity to the environment before or after**."]},{"text":"A **sensitive or “optimal” period** is a looser link between timing and learning.","children":["In a sensitive period, **learning is possible throughout the life span**.","In a sensitive period, learning is **optimal during particular windows**.","These windows can have a **gradual onset and offset**, which experience can itself affect."]},{"text":"The two make different predictions for late learners.","children":["A critical period predicts no late learning, so any late improvement counts against it.","A sensitive period predicts late learning that is harder but still possible, with a lasting early advantage.","Even the classic critical period in vision fits the term sensitive period better. The same question arises for language."]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia is poor vision in one eye even though the eye itself works normally.** The full definition is poor vision in one eye despite normal **peripheral function** (the working of the eye itself). The problem lies in how the brain handles that eye's input.","children":["**Strabismus** (misaligned eyes) sends the brain two conflicting images.","A **cataract** (clouding of the lens) blurs or blocks the light reaching the back of the eye.","**Anisometropia** (a large difference in focusing power between the eyes) makes one eye's image much blurrier."]},{"text":"These conditions cause **periods of deprivation from normal visual input**.","children":["The deprivation can result in **poor vision long after the conditions have been remediated** (corrected).","Correcting the eye restores its input.","The brain may still fail to use that input well."]},{"text":"A temporary early problem can leave a **lasting effect**, so the timing of the problem matters as much as the problem itself.","children":["A cataract removed early may leave little lasting damage.","A cataract removed late may leave poor vision even after the lens is clear."]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"**Amblyopia happens because the two eyes compete for neurons in visual cortex, and the weaker eye loses.** Amblyopia occurs because of **competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**.","children":["When both eyes work normally, they share the cortical neurons evenly.","Abnormal vision in one eye can lead to **“colonization”** (the other eye takes over that eye's cortical representation)."]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation** (blocking or degrading input to one eye) in mice reveals the steps.","children":["Within **2 to 3 days**, there is **increased spine motility**. **Dendritic spines** (small projections where neurons receive connections) become unstable and move around.","Within **3 to 4 days**, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration** (breakdown of nerve fibers cut off from their source). Connections from the deprived eye disappear.","After **more than a week**, there is **axon sprouting and new spine birth**. The open eye's fibers form connections in the space the deprived eye lost."]},{"text":"A restored eye **remains at a competitive disadvantage**.","children":["The other eye now holds the connections.","Ordinary viewing with both eyes keeps favoring the stronger eye.","So **normal cortical responses cannot be restored by normal experience**."]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"**Patching the good eye treats amblyopia because it gives the weaker eye a chance to win back cortex.** Treatments for amblyopia are **based on competition**.","children":["One of the most effective treatments is **intermittently patching the “good” eye**.","The patch blocks the stronger eye's input for part of the day.","The brain then has to rely on the weaker eye."]},{"text":"Patching **restores the competitive balance between the eyes**.","children":["The balance permits the brain's own **plasticity** (its ability to change connections in response to experience) to restore normal function.","The weak eye's input can win back cortical connections.","The patch does not repair the eye itself. It changes the experience the brain receives."]},{"text":"Timing decides how well patching works.","children":["The treatment is **most effective early in development**, because a young brain is highly plastic.","The treatment **may be ineffective by late childhood (10 years)**, because it helps less and less as the visual system matures."]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"**Adults with amblyopia can still improve with intensive training, so plasticity is not abolished entirely after the critical period.** **Levi and Polat (1996)** tested adults, and some patients were **in their 50s**.","children":["Adults did **long-term, intensive training** on specific **psychophysical tasks** with the affected eye. These tasks measure the relationship between a physical stimulus and what a person perceives.","Practice ran over many **kilotrials** (thousands of trials).","Improvement was measured as a falling **threshold** (the smallest stimulus a person can reliably detect). A lower threshold means better vision."]},{"text":"The threshold fell in steps across about 50 kilotrials.","children":["Over the first **19 kilotrials**, the threshold **dropped steadily**, a highly significant change.","From about **20 to 35 kilotrials**, the threshold **leveled off**.","Around **36 kilotrials**, the threshold **dropped again** and stayed at the new lower level."]},{"text":"Training improved **both task performance and general visual function**.","children":["The benefits spread beyond the trained task.","The adult visual system keeps some **experience-dependent plasticity**.","The right kind and amount of training can still produce change."]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"**Playing action video games improved visual acuity and visual attention in adults with amblyopia.** **Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** ran the study.","children":["Adults played an action video game with their amblyopic eye.","The game was a first-person shooter set in a battle scene.","**Visual acuity** (sharpness of vision, the ability to see fine detail) improved.","**Visual attention** (the ability to pick out and focus on the relevant parts of a scene) improved."]},{"text":"Action games demand fast visual judgments and constant attention.","children":["Those demands push the visual system to use the amblyopic eye's input.","The adults were long past childhood visual development, so the gains came from the training itself.","The gains came well after the age when patching usually works best."]},{"text":"The study adds to the evidence for adult **plasticity** in amblyopia.","children":["A demanding, engaging visual task can drive change in a mature visual system.","An early optimal window can close without removing every chance of improvement."]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"**Amblyopia is a classic sensitive period, because early deprivation has lasting effects yet adults can still improve.** Three features mark the early window.","children":["A **peripheral disturbance in early experience** can lead to **life-long disruption in visual function**.","The long-term loss is the **sequela** (lasting result) of a temporary deficit during **experience-dependent organization of the brain**.","The brain organized itself around abnormal input, and that organization stays.","**Treatment is most effective during a limited developmental window**. Patching works best early."]},{"text":"New evidence suggests the **right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood**.","children":["Intensive perceptual training improved adults' vision.","Video game play improved adults' vision.","Late learning is harder and needs special conditions, but it happens."]},{"text":"The pattern fits a **sensitive period** better than a strict critical period.","children":["A strict critical period would rule out any late improvement.","A sensitive period has a strong early window with some ability to change later."]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"**Two lines of work relate the vision findings to language, Lenneberg's account of brain development and studies of early deprivation.** Each line takes up a different question.","children":["**Lenneberg** tried to synthesize **developmental neurology** (the study of how the nervous system develops) with a **“language acquisition device”** (a proposed innate capacity for learning language).","**Studies of early deprivation** use data from **neglected children** and **Deaf children with limited input to sign**."]},{"text":"Lenneberg asked whether the maturing brain opens and closes a window for language.","children":["He linked language milestones to brain development.","If a window exists, language learning depends on the state of the brain as well as the input."]},{"text":"Deprivation studies ask what happens when language input arrives late.","children":["Neglected children were cut off from ordinary language input.","Some Deaf children have no access to sign language in early childhood.","Comparing them with typical learners tests whether the age of first exposure affects the final outcome."]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967) argued that language develops in stages on a biological timetable, alongside motor development.** In **Biological Foundations of Language**, he tried to synthesize contemporary linguistics with what was known about brain development.","children":["Studies were finding a fairly robust and **stage-like pattern** of language development.","Austrian, British and American children follow the same order between 8 and 36 months.","**Words** come first, mostly between about 10 and 18 months.","**Two-word phrases** come next, mostly between about 18 and 26 months.","**Sentences of five words or more** come last, between about 28 and 36 months."]},{"text":"Language milestones pair with motor milestones at each age.","children":["At **8 months**, infants stand holding on and pick up small objects. **Reduplicated** babbling (repeated syllables) becomes frequent, with intonation that can signal emphasis and emotion.","At **10 months**, infants creep and pull to standing. Vocalizations mix with sound play. Infants try to imitate sounds and begin to tell words apart.","At **12 months**, infants walk with one hand held. Words such as **mama** or **dada** emerge, and some words and simple commands are understood.","At **18 months**, vocabulary is more than three but fewer than fifty words, and comprehension progresses rapidly."]},{"text":"**Maturational milestones** (developmental achievements tied to physical growth) were seen as consistent with the **unfolding of a biological program**.","children":["Language and motor milestones line up, which suggests a common timetable.","Lenneberg proposed that brain maturation sets the pace for both."]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"**Lenneberg proposed that the critical period for language opens after the rapid brain growth of the first two years.** His data on typical brain development were sparse.","children":["The data were almost entirely based on **post-mortem** (after death) analyses of tissue.","Researchers could not follow the same children's brains as they grew.","They pieced development together from children who had died at different ages."]},{"text":"**Neuron size** (cell body volume of neurons in cortical layers III, IV and V) grows rapidly in the first two years, then levels off.","children":["**No data were available between ages 2 and 18**.","That gap overlaps much of the period of language acquisition."]},{"text":"Lenneberg observed abrupt changes in neuron size and **packing density** (how closely neurons are crowded together) before age two.","children":["These changes might be **permissive** (making language acquisition possible without directly causing it) for the beginning of language acquisition.","The brain first goes through a burst of growth, and the window opens once that growth settles.","Lenneberg filled the data gap with a reasonable guess that later research would need to test."]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"**Children can develop language after losing the left hemisphere and adults cannot, so Lenneberg tied the closing of the critical period to cerebral dominance.** More extensive data came from studies of recovery from stroke.","children":["**Aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions** (areas of damaged brain tissue).","In extreme cases, surgeons performed a **hemispherectomy** (removing or disconnecting one whole cerebral hemisphere), typically to relieve seizures in epilepsy.","**Children** whose left hemisphere is removed still seem to **develop typical language abilities**.","**Adults do not recover language** after this procedure."]},{"text":"Lenneberg's summary of outcomes from **Basser (1962)** sorts cases by age at lesion.","children":["Before the teens, left removal left **49** with speech not affected or improved and **3** with permanent aphasia.","Before the teens, right removal left **38** with speech not affected or improved and **5** with permanent aphasia.","All of those children with permanent aphasia already had aphasia before the operation.","During puberty, one left removal had slow improvement, with some aphasia remaining to the end of life 27 months after surgery.","In adults, left removal left **none** with speech unaffected or improved and **6 with permanent aphasia**.","In adults, right removal left **25** with speech unaffected or improved and none with permanent aphasia."]},{"text":"Lenneberg hypothesized that a key factor in closing the critical period is adult-like **cerebral dominance** (specialization of one hemisphere, usually the left, for language).","children":["In young children, language is not yet fixed in the left hemisphere, so the right can take over.","In adults, dominance is established, so the right can no longer take over."]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"**Children with perinatal strokes do much better than adults with similar injuries, but they do not fully catch up.** A **perinatal stroke** happens between the prenatal period and 28 days after birth.","children":["Systematic studies find outcomes **clearly much better than for adults with similar injuries**.","The children still have some effects of the injury."]},{"text":"**Early language deficits** appear in **both right-hemisphere and left-hemisphere patients**.","children":["In adults, language problems mainly follow left-hemisphere damage.","In young children, damage to either side causes early language delays.","**Some deficits are worse with right-hemisphere damage**, the opposite of the adult pattern."]},{"text":"Language outcomes are generally in the **low-normal range**.","children":["That range suggests a **persistent delay** rather than a catching up.","Recovery is strong but incomplete.","Early plasticity is real but limited. A young brain can reorganize for language, yet the reorganized system may work less well than the original would have."]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Genie was isolated and abused from 20 months of age and had no speech when researchers met her at 13 years and 7 months.** **Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied her as a case of the **effects of early deprivation**.","children":["Genie was born in **April 1957**.","When researchers first met her, she was so thin that she looked six or seven."]},{"text":"Her early life involved extreme isolation and abuse.","children":["From **20 months**, she was confined to a small room under increasing physical restraint.","She was mostly harnessed to an infant's potty chair. Otherwise she was in a homemade sleeping bag in a crib covered with wire mesh.","Her mother, who was rapidly losing her sight, gave minimal care, and Genie was fed only infant food.","Her father **physically punished her if she made any sounds**.","Her father and older brother did not speak to her but at times barked at her like dogs.","The home had no television or radio, and contact with other people was extremely limited."]},{"text":"She was hospitalized for malnutrition.","children":["She **could not stand up straight or chew food**.","She was **not toilet trained**.","She **did not speak, cry or produce any vocal sounds**.","Her lack of language came with severe physical, social and emotional deprivation, so the two **cannot be separated**.","Her case still reveals what happens when first-language learning starts in adolescence."]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"**In four years of observation, Genie learned to understand hundreds of words, some morphology and some prepositions.** She learned quite a bit.","children":["She came to **comprehend hundreds of words**.","She came to comprehend some aspects of **morphology** (the system of meaningful word parts, such as the plural ending “-s”). She was **particularly good with plurals**.","She came to **comprehend some prepositions** (words for spatial relationships between objects)."]},{"text":"A preposition test used **buttons and plastic boxes** of different colors and sizes.","children":["Genie moved the buttons and boxes according to spoken instructions.","A sample item was **“Put the red box behind the blue box.”**","The test covered **under, over, in, on, behind, in front of, in back of** and **next to**."]},{"text":"From October 1972 to August 1973, **every preposition had more correct than incorrect responses**.","children":["In had 37 correct and 4 incorrect. On had 36 correct and 9 incorrect.","Over had 11 correct and 2 incorrect. Under had 17 correct and 7 incorrect.","In front of had 20 correct and 6 incorrect. In back of had 7 correct and 3 incorrect.","Next to had 7 correct and 1 incorrect. Behind had 12 correct and 5 incorrect."]},{"text":"**Some language can be acquired late**, after puberty and long after the window Lenneberg proposed.","children":["The learning was uneven across different parts of language."]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"**Genie's speech lagged far behind her understanding, and her grammar developed much more slowly than her vocabulary.** Her **production** (what she said) lagged severely behind her **comprehension** (what she understood).","children":["**Some lag is typical of development**. All children understand more than they can say.","Genie was also **punished for making sounds** as a child, which could limit how much she spoke.","Her speech therefore revealed less than she actually knew."]},{"text":"A test of **conjunctions** placed **five familiar objects in a row** and asked her to point to one or more.","children":["Items included **“Show me the fork and pencil”**, **“Show me the crayon or the knife”** and **“Show me either the spoon or the crayon”**.","**And** requires pointing to every named object.","**Or** requires pointing to one named object, which depends on understanding a logical relationship."]},{"text":"From October 1971 to December 1973, she passed and but failed or.","children":["**Early responses** treated or sentences like and sentences, so she pointed to both objects.","**Later responses** distinguished and from or, but she still did not understand **either/or**.","And sentences had **100 correct**, 3 incorrect and 0 unanalyzable.","Or sentences had **7 correct**, **51 incorrect** and 6 unanalyzable.","Strong vocabulary with weak grammar fits the idea that **grammar depends more on early exposure than vocabulary does**."]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"**Genie's gap between vocabulary and grammar is widely interpreted as suggesting that first-language grammar learning is impaired after a sensitive period.** The full claim is that grammar learning in a first language is **specifically impaired after a sensitive period**.","children":["She learned hundreds of words but made little progress with grammar.","On this interpretation, vocabulary can be learned at any age, while grammar depends on early exposure."]},{"text":"Her case leaves **lots of questions**.","children":["Was Genie **learning disabled**? She may have had difficulties from birth that would have affected her language anyway.","What were the **residual impacts of neglect and abuse** on her social development? Severe trauma could affect learning in many ways beyond missing language input.","One case cannot separate disability, abuse and late exposure."]},{"text":"**After four years, funding to study her was pulled**.","children":["Nobody can know what she might have learned after a longer time.","The evidence covers only those four years.","Her pattern still became an influential piece of evidence for a sensitive period in first-language learning."]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"**Deaf people who had any early language, signed or spoken, learn a later language much better than those with none.** **Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** tested this with deaf people.","children":["Many deaf children **do not get sign language input** but have an **otherwise typical upbringing**.","Unlike Genie, they grow up in ordinary, caring homes, so they are a cleaner test of first-language timing."]},{"text":"Two groups learned **American Sign Language (ASL)** late.","children":["Children aged **9 to 15** learning ASL as their **first language** had **difficulty**.","A comparison group who **went deaf after first learning a spoken language** did **much better**.","Both groups learned ASL late, but only one had an earlier language.","**These differences persist into adulthood**."]},{"text":"Two adult tasks measured the difference.","children":["In **recall of complex ASL sentences**, adults with no early language scored about **36%**. Adults with an early spoken language scored about **78%**.","In **judging whether complex English sentences were grammatically correct**, chance is 50%.","Deaf adults with no early language scored about **52%**, close to chance.","Deaf adults with ASL in infancy scored about **80%**.","Hearing adults with an early spoken language other than English scored about 80%.","Early ASL helped later English as much as an early spoken language did."]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"**First-language learning clearly depends on age, but the evidence cannot settle whether the window is a critical or a sensitive period.** Three points bear on the question for a first language (**L1**).","children":["**Recovery from stroke**, and certainly from **hemispherectomy**, looks **very different in childhood and adulthood**.","Children can develop language after damage that would leave an adult with permanent aphasia."]},{"text":"**Deprivation of input does not appear to extend the critical period**.","children":["One might expect a window to stay open until language input arrives.","Genie and the deaf adults who learned sign late have lasting difficulty, especially with grammar.","The window seems to follow a maturational schedule, whether or not language has been experienced."]},{"text":"The data come from **highly unusual cases with lots of other things going on**, so it is **difficult to reach firm conclusions**.","children":["Stroke, brain surgery, abuse and neglect each bring complications beyond the timing of input.","There is a **lack of animal models**, because animals do not learn human language.","**Gene knockout experiments** (disabling specific genes to test their role) cannot be done with people."]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"**Adults learn a second language faster at first, but children more often end up native-like.** Babies are overrated as learners, so **sensitive periods** for a second language (**L2**) should not be taken too far.","children":["A typical child by 18 months uses at least 20 words.","That child also understands \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" and points to body parts when asked.","That child answers \"Where's teddy?\" or \"What's that?\" with words or gestures and gives teddy a pretend drink.","That child makes at least four consonant sounds, such as b, n, d, g, w and h. That child enjoys being read to and points to pictures with one finger.","An adult who knew only about 20 words after 18 months of instruction would be a very poor learner."]},{"text":"**Adults initially learn more quickly** than children, for two main reasons.","children":["**Transfer from L1** (using what they know from the first language) gives them a head start.","**Better general cognitive skills** (memory, attention and reasoning) help too."]},{"text":"Adults do worse on **ultimate attainment** (the final level of skill a learner reaches).","children":["Adults rarely become native-like.","That may also be due in part to transfer.","Early speed and final success are separate measures, and children and adults rank differently on each."]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"**Many toddler language milestones are general conceptual abilities that adult learners already have.** So later learners mainly learn how the new language expresses old ideas.","children":["Understanding \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" rests on **spatial relations between objects**.","**Pretend play**, such as giving teddy a drink, rests on **event representations** (mental models of who does what in an event).","A toddler builds these ideas and the words at the same time."]},{"text":"Later learners of a second language have much more of this in place.","children":["An adult learning Spanish already has the concepts behind \"in\" and \"on\".","The adult can focus on how these concepts are represented in the **target language** (the language being learned)."]},{"text":"Later learners also already know another language, which affects how the second language is learned.","children":["The first language offers ready-made ways to express concepts.","Some fit the second language and help.","Others clash with it and cause errors."]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer means using first-language habits for a second language, and it can help or hurt.**","children":["**Transfer** (the use of native language, or L1, processes and representations for a second language, or L2) covers sounds, sentences and words.","The learner arrives with a complete working language system."]},{"text":"Transfer can have positive and negative impacts on learning and performance.","children":["**Positive transfer** happens when an L1 pattern matches the L2, so the learner gets a head start.","**Negative transfer** happens when an L1 pattern conflicts with the L2, so the learner makes errors."]},{"text":"A cartoon gives an example of negative transfer in grammar.","children":["A man in a pub asks \"Do you not be happy with me as the translator of the books of you?\"","The words are English.","The word order and phrasing come from another language, carried over by word-by-word translation."]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"**English speakers hear two different Hindi consonants as one English sound, so transfer makes the Hindi contrast hard to learn.**","children":["**Categorical perception** leads to **acquired similarity** (sounds in one category come to sound more alike with experience)."]},{"text":"**Minimal pairs in Hindi** (two words that differ in only one sound) reveal the problem.","children":["Two words sound like \"tap\" to English ears. One means hot, and one means a kind of beat.","Two words sound like \"dab\". One means pressure, and one means a kind of fruit.","Two words sound like \"ari\". One means saw (the tool), and one means crooked.","Each pair has a **dental** consonant (tongue against the teeth) and a **retroflex** consonant (tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth)."]},{"text":"Native English speakers use their **alveolar** category (tongue on the ridge behind the teeth, as in /t/ or /d/) for both.","children":["English has only the alveolar category.","Both Hindi sounds fall into it and seem identical.","So the learner must separate sounds the first language taught them to treat as one."]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"**English speakers struggle with Chinese classifiers because English grammar taught them to ignore the cues classifiers depend on.**","children":["**Classifiers** are a somewhat unusual grammatical feature across languages.","A **classifier** (a word between a number or demonstrative and a noun) depends on the kind of thing the noun names.","The order is demonstrative pronoun, nominal classifier, noun, as in **yí gè rén** (\"one person\")."]},{"text":"Each Chinese classifier goes with a kind of noun.","children":["**Gè** is the general classifier.","**Bǎ** goes with objects that have a handle.","**Běn** goes with books and similar objects.","**Kuài** goes with objects divided into pieces.","**Liàng** goes with wheeled vehicles.","**Tái** goes with devices and gadgets.","**Xiē** marks an indeterminate amount, or the plural of pronouns and demonstratives.","**Zhāng** goes with flat objects.","**Zhī** goes with long, thin objects."]},{"text":"English does not care about noun meaning here.","children":["Saying \"a chair\", \"that dog\", \"the car\" or \"one book\" needs no attention to the noun's meaning.","In Chinese, different nouns require different classifiers.","So learners must notice shape and function again."]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender causes transfer problems because English speakers have no habit of tracking it, yet it controls agreement.**","children":["**Grammatical gender** (a system sorting nouns into classes such as masculine and feminine) is different from social or cultural gender.","Nothing is \"male\" about a book or \"female\" about liberty, yet French has le livre and la liberté.","Other masculine nouns are le cheval (the horse), le chien (the dog) and le bruit (the noise).","Other feminine nouns are la colombe (the dove), la chemise (the shirt) and la maison (the house)."]},{"text":"Gender matters for **agreement** (related words must match the noun).","children":["Pronouns, adjectives and some verb forms must be coordinated with noun gender."]},{"text":"French has synonyms with different genders.","children":["**Auto** and **voiture** both mean car.","**Rivière** and **fleuve** both mean river.","**Vélo** and **bicyclette** both mean bicycle.","In each pair one is masculine and one is feminine, so gender is learned word by word."]},{"text":"Word endings give clues.","children":["Masculine endings include -age (le fromage), -r (le professeur), -t (le chat) and -isme (le capitalisme).","Feminine endings include -ie (la boulangerie), -ion (la nation), -ité or -tié (la fraternité), -ance (la balance), and -nne, -mme or -lle (la fille, l’indienne)."]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"**Transfer is not all bad, because similar words across languages make vocabulary easier to learn.**","children":["French **auto** resembles English \"auto\".","French **rivière** resembles English \"river\".","French **bicyclette** resembles English \"bicycle\".","Japanese **コロッケ (korokke)** means croquette.","Japanese **モノレール (monoreru)** means monorail."]},{"text":"The similarity has two sources.","children":["**Shared etymology** (a shared word history) gives **cognates**, such as rivière and river.","More recent **borrowing** gives **loan words** (words taken from another language), such as korokke and monoreru.","Loan words get their sounds adapted, as Japanese did with these European words."]},{"text":"Both give **positive transfer** for vocabulary.","children":["The learner already knows a similar form with a similar meaning.","That familiar anchor makes the new word easier to store and recall."]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"**Not all similar-sounding words are cognates, and the pull to link them can cause embarrassing errors.**","children":["A **false friend** (a word that sounds like a word in another language but means something else) misleads learners.","The bias to read L2 words as related to similar L1 words can be very strong."]},{"text":"\"Arnold está embarazado\" does not mean Arnold is embarrassed.","children":["Spanish **embarazado** means **pregnant**.","An English speaker going by sound gets it badly wrong."]},{"text":"The link forms before the learner checks the meaning.","children":["When meanings match, the link speeds learning, as with cognates.","When meanings differ, the link causes errors.","A cognate and a false friend differ only in whether the meanings match."]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000) tested in the lab whether cognate status helps people learn new second-language words.**","children":["They used **paired-associate learning** (studying pairs, then recalling one member when given the other) with made-up words.","Participants were Dutch speakers, so Dutch stood in for L1 and the made-up words for an L2."]},{"text":"The made-up words varied in two ways.","children":["**Similarity** to their Dutch partners made some cognate-like and some not.","**Concreteness** made some concrete (something seen or touched) and some abstract (an idea)."]},{"text":"Crossing the two gave four kinds of pairs.","children":["Gitaar and gettar (\"guitar\") is concrete and cognate.","Tijger and duunze (\"tiger\") is concrete and non-cognate.","Domein and domaan (\"domain\") is abstract and cognate.","Herstel and groop (\"repair\") is abstract and non-cognate."]},{"text":"Participants learned the pairs.","children":["They were simply shown each Dutch word with its made-up partner.","They were tested on **retention** a week later."]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"**Cognates were learned faster and remembered better, so cognate status helps from the very first stage of second-language word learning.** De Groot and Keijzer (2000) tested recall in three training sessions, then in session 4 after a week without training.","children":["Concrete cognates went from 69.7% to 93.4% recall by session 3.","Concrete non-cognates went from 43.7% to 92.0%.","Abstract cognates went from 42.7% to 86.0%.","Abstract non-cognates went from 19.0% to 72.7%."]},{"text":"After the week, the kinds of words separated again.","children":["Concrete cognates dropped to 78.4%.","Concrete non-cognates dropped to 69.0%.","Abstract cognates dropped to 68.4%.","Abstract non-cognates fell sharply to 36.0%.","Responses were faster for cognates in every session."]},{"text":"**Concreteness** also had a large effect.","children":["The two factors add together, so abstract non-cognates were hardest.","**Context availability** (concrete words are easier to link with a supporting context) is one explanation."]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"**The experiment is convincing because it tested productive vocabulary, not just receptive vocabulary.**","children":["**Receptive** testing gives the made-up word and asks for the Dutch word.","**Expressive** testing, which De Groot and Keijzer call **productive** testing, gives the Dutch word and asks for the made-up word."]},{"text":"A receptive test alone would not be very impressive.","children":["A cognate-like word gives away its Dutch partner.","Gitaar, tijger and domein could be guessed without Dutch training, since they resemble guitar, tiger and domain."]},{"text":"The concrete cognates are very close to the Dutch words.","children":["Broer and breur, brood and brodde, gitaar and gettar, jongen and jange, lepel and lippel.","Lichaam and lechem, naald and nadel, paraplu and parredu, rivier and riffer, schaar and skare.","Stier and stire, trein and trinen, varken and vark, viool and vialo, vrouw and vrauk."]},{"text":"Productive testing removes the shortcut.","children":["The learner must recall the exact new form, such as gettar.","A cognate advantage there means real learning."]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"**Cognates and concrete words were forgotten less over the week in both test directions, so participants truly learned the new forms.**","children":["Recall was measured right after learning (session 3) and a week later (session 4).","The drop over the week is the measure that matters."]},{"text":"In productive testing, learners produced the new word.","children":["Concrete words fell from 92.7% to 73.7%, a 19.0 point drop.","Abstract words fell from 79.3% to 52.2%, a 27.1 point drop.","Cognates fell from 89.7% to 73.3%, a 16.4 point drop.","Non-cognates fell from 82.3% to 52.5%, a 29.8 point drop."]},{"text":"In receptive testing, learners gave the Dutch word.","children":["Concrete words fell from 97.7% to 88.9%, an 8.8 point drop.","Abstract words fell from 91.5% to 64.9%, a 26.6 point drop.","Cognates fell from 98.5% to 90.7%, a 7.8 point drop.","Non-cognates fell from 90.7% to 63.0%, a 27.7 point drop."]},{"text":"Receptive scores were higher, because recognizing is easier than producing.","children":["The cognate advantage held in productive testing too.","Similarity to a known word helped build a lasting memory."]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"**Researchers ask whether similar first-language words always become active while a person processes a second language.** There have been many, many studies of this.","children":["An L2 word might also activate L1 words that sound like it.","That could happen automatically, even when only the L2 is being used.","If it does, the two languages are never fully separate in the mind."]},{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** gave one clear example with a **visual world experiment**. In this kind of experiment, people hear spoken instructions while viewing objects, and their eye movements are recorded.","children":["Looks toward an object whose L1 name sounds like the spoken L2 word would mean the L1 word was active.","Eye movements reveal this without asking for a report.","The person still chooses the correct object."]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"**Eye movements reveal which words compete during recognition, even when the final choice is correct.** The same method was used earlier to study categorical perception.","children":["**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** studied gradient effects of within-category **VOT** (voice onset time) on **lexical access** (finding a word in memory).","Participants saw a lamp, a ship, a bear and a pear.","They heard a word from a continuum between \"bear\" and \"pear\".","Bear and pear differ only in the first consonant, and the lamp and ship are fillers."]},{"text":"Looks to a competitor reveal what participants are considering.","children":["A participant can click the bear after glancing at the pear.","Those glances mean \"pear\" was active."]},{"text":"Competition between BEAR and PEAR depended on **distance from the category boundary**.","children":["Sounds near the /b/ and /p/ boundary drew more looks to the competitor.","Sounds far from it drew fewer.","The same method can test competition across two languages."]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994) found the cohort effect, where words sharing a target's beginning compete early in recognition.**","children":["A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","Participants saw a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","The display had a beaker (target), a beetle (same beginning), a speaker (rhyme) and a carriage (unrelated)."]},{"text":"Looks were tracked over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and beetle rose together early.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds, then fell once the words diverged.","Looks to the beaker kept rising toward 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and peaked near 0.1, because its overlap comes at the end.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds.","children":["They drop out when the input stops matching.","Eye movements track this moment by moment."]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey tested whether a Russian word competes with an English target when the two words only begin with the same sounds.**","children":["A **cross-language competitor** is an object whose name in the other language begins like the target.","They hypothesized that if L1 words are always activated, Russian speakers hearing English \"speaker\" should look at matches.","English **speaker** sounds like Russian **спички (spichki)**, meaning matches."]},{"text":"Each English target had an English and a Russian competitor.","children":["Speaker had spear and spichki (matches).","Boot had book and buben (tambourine).","Shark had shovel and sharik (balloon).","Chair had chess set and cherepaha (turtle).","Marker had marbles and marka (stamp).","Barbed wire had bark and barhat (velvet).","Plug had plum and platye (dress).","Gun had gum and gaika (nut).","Lock had lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For Russian targets the roles were reversed.","children":["Spichki had English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","Marka had English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","Platye had English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The tie is only sound, since speakers and matches share no meaning.","children":["So looks to the matches measure activation of spichki."]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"**While listening in their second language, bilinguals looked at objects with similar first-language names, so the first language stays active.** Russian and English bilinguals followed instructions in English.","children":["The **between-language competitor** had a similar Russian name.","The **within-language competitor** had a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["Between-language competitor alone drew looks on 18% of trials.","Within-language competitor alone drew looks on 18% of trials.","With both present, the between-language one drew 13% and the within-language one 19%.","A control filler with no competitor drew only 7%."]},{"text":"With one competitor, both kinds drew looks to the same extent.","children":["The Russian name pulled the eyes as strongly as an English one."]},{"text":"With both present, the within-language competitor drew more looks.","children":["The between-language competitor still drew about twice as many as the control.","Bilinguals still chose correctly, but L1 words competed along the way."]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"**While listening in their first language, Russian speakers did not reliably activate English words, so the effect runs one way.** The same bilinguals followed instructions in Russian.","children":["The between-language competitor now had a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was again the percent of trials with a look.","children":["Between-language competitor alone drew 8%.","Within-language competitor alone drew 14%.","With both present, the between-language one drew 8% and the within-language one 13%.","The control filler drew 5%."]},{"text":"The small difference between the between-language and control conditions is **not statistically significant** (within chance variation).","children":["So English words do not seem to be activated in the Russian task.","The within-language competitor still produced a cohort effect in Russian."]},{"text":"The result is **asymmetric**.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","This fits the idea that the first language is more firmly established and more easily activated."]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"**In the Revised Hierarchical Model, activation spreads more easily from second-language words to first-language words, because learning emphasizes that direction.** Kroll and Stewart built the model on **spreading activation** (activating one representation partly activates those linked to it).","children":["The model has an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a store of **concepts** (meanings).","**Lexical links** join L1 and L2 words directly.","**Conceptual links** join each vocabulary to the concepts."]},{"text":"The L1 lexicon is assumed to be larger.","children":["L1 words are strongly connected to relevant concepts.","L2 words are more weakly connected to concepts."]},{"text":"The L2 lexicon has strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon.","children":["The link from L2 to L1 is strong.","The link from L1 to L2 is weak."]},{"text":"Learning builds this pattern.","children":["Learners usually attach each new L2 word to its L1 translation.","The direct link from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early on."]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"**The Revised Hierarchical Model explains why Russian competitors drew looks in the English task but English competitors did not in the Russian task.** Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994) proposed the model.","children":[]},{"text":"In the L2 task with the target \"speaker\", activation spreads in steps.","children":["The concept for \"matches\" partially activates the Russian word spichki, since L1 words are tightly linked to concepts.","The sounds of speaker also partly activate spichki, since the two begin alike.","This activity feeds back to the concept for \"matches\".","Conceptual activation for \"matches\" leads to eye movements toward the L1 competitor."]},{"text":"Speaker and spichki share only **sound overlap**.","children":["Speakers and matches share no meaning.","The boost to spichki strengthens the concept of matches and pulls the eyes there."]},{"text":"In the L1 task, the conceptual links with L2 might be too weak to create this interference reliably.","children":["An English competitor would need a strong link from its English word to its concept.","L2 words have only weak conceptual links.","So the effect appears in one direction only."]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"**The model predicts that balanced bilinguals are not two monolinguals in one, because both languages compete all the time.**","children":["A **balanced bilingual** (someone about equally skilled in both languages) has similarly strong conceptual links for both languages.","This prediction is counter-intuitive.","One might expect each language to run separately."]},{"text":"Strong links in both languages keep both vocabularies active.","children":["Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages.","**Competitive dynamics** (words of the two languages competing for selection) are engaged all the time.","**Inhibition** (suppressing the word from the language not in use) is engaged all the time too."]},{"text":"The prediction led to a lot of work on bilingual advantages in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks.","children":["Cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","Constant practice choosing one language might improve attention control in general."]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"**Stopping use of the first language after learning a second separates the effect of early learning from the effect of continued use.**","children":["The usual sequence is to begin learning L1.","Then the person learns L2.","The person continues learning and using L1, so both stay in use."]},{"text":"In the other sequence, L1 use stops after L2 arrives.","children":["The person begins L1 and later learns L2.","The person does not continue using L1.","L2 takes over."]},{"text":"The comparison has two possible outcomes.","children":["If early learning leaves a permanent foundation, traces of L1 should remain without use.","If continued use is needed, L1 might fade or disappear."]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Pallier and colleagues tested Korean adoptees in France to see whether a second language can replace the first.** The authors were Pallier, Dehaene, Poline, LeBihan, Argenti, Dupoux and Mehler, using brain imaging of language plasticity in adopted adults.","children":["They tested adults aged 20 to 32.","These adults were adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between ages 3 and 8.","They learned Korean as young children, then heard only French at home.","They stopped using Korean entirely, so they are a natural case of L1 use stopping."]},{"text":"The test looked for **residual knowledge** (any remaining trace) of Korean.","children":["Monolingual French speakers served as the comparison group.","If adoptees beat them on Korean tasks, some Korean survived.","Any survival would reveal what early learning leaves behind."]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"**Korean has three kinds of stop at each place in the mouth, which makes a good test of retained Korean.**","children":["A **stop** is a consonant made by fully blocking airflow and releasing it, such as /p/, /t/ or /k/.","English and French have two kinds at each place, such as /b/ and /p/.","Korean has **aspirated** (strong puff of air), **plain** (lighter release) and **tense** (stiffened throat) stops."]},{"text":"The UCLA Phonetics Archive has three series.","children":["The **bilabial** series (lips) is pʰul (\"grass\"), pul (\"fire\") and p’ul (\"horn\").","The **dental** series (tongue at teeth) is tʰal (\"mask\"), tal (\"moon\") and t’al (\"daughter\").","The **velar** series (back of tongue) is kʰo (\"nose\"), kok (\"music\") and k’ok (\"exactly\")."]},{"text":"Each series shares a place and differs only in stop type.","children":["French speakers tend to hear the three sounds as two.","Korean speakers hear three.","Adoptees who kept Korean sound knowledge should hear the differences better than French speakers."]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"**Ventureyra and colleagues found that Korean adoptees heard Korean stop contrasts no better than French speakers who had never learned Korean.**","children":["Listeners heard pairs of sounds and judged whether they were different.","Adoptees, French native speakers and Korean native speakers were compared."]},{"text":"On the Korean contrasts, only Korean speakers usually said \"different\".","children":["Plain and tense p and k drew about 96% from Koreans, 8% from adoptees and 3% from French speakers.","Plain and aspirated p and k drew about 90%, 19% and 26%.","Tense and aspirated p and k drew about 95%, 23% and 20%.","Plain and tense s drew about 79%, 26% and 23%."]},{"text":"Two controls checked that everyone could do the task.","children":["A vowel contrast drew \"different\" on about 97 to 99% for all groups.","Identical pairs drew \"different\" only about 3 to 13% of the time."]},{"text":"So the difficulty was specific to the Korean stops.","children":["Early Korean experience left no measurable advantage."]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"**Pallier and colleagues looked for any remaining trace of Korean in the adoptees with unusual tasks, and found none.** In a more extensive study, adoptees were no better than monolingual French speakers on three tasks.","children":["These are not standard psycholinguistic tasks.","They were designed to catch even weak knowledge."]},{"text":"Distinguishing Korean from largely unrelated languages tested familiarity with its sound.","children":["Both groups rated Korean sentences around 4 on a 1 to 7 scale.","Japanese got around 4 to 5, Polish about 2, and Swedish and Wolof about 2.5 to 3.","Adoptees did not pick out Korean better."]},{"text":"Identifying the correct Korean translation of French words tested vocabulary.","children":["Adoptees scored about 55% and French speakers about 50%, both near chance."]},{"text":"Deciding whether a short excerpt was repeated from a sentence just heard tested sound sequences.","children":["Both groups scored about 95% on French.","Both scored about 80% on Korean, Japanese and Polish.","Adoptees had no Korean advantage."]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"**Brain imaging found that the adoptees' brains treated Korean as a foreign language, just as French speakers' brains did.**","children":["Listening to French activated the **classical language network** in both groups.","That network includes **superior temporal** regions (upper temporal lobe, speech sounds).","It also includes **inferior frontal** regions (lower frontal lobe, including Broca's area)."]},{"text":"Pallier and colleagues subtracted activity for another language from activity for French.","children":["They computed French minus Polish, French minus Japanese and French minus Korean.","Each was computed for the French group and the Korean group (adoptees).","French always produced more language-network activity."]},{"text":"French minus Korean could have differed between groups if residual Korean processing remained.","children":["Adoptees still processing Korean would show a smaller difference.","No such difference appeared.","The imaging agrees with the behavioral tests."]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"**An unused first language could lose its brain connections to the second language through competition.** L2 and L1 interactions can be seen as **competitive dynamics**.","children":["Imagine L1 as a set of connections within and between brain regions.","Use builds and maintains these connections."]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation** (depriving one eye of input) works this way in mice.","children":["After 2 to 3 days, the deprived eye's connections show **increased spine motility**.","After 3 to 4 days, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration** (breakdown of cut-off nerve fibers).","After more than a week, the other eye shows **axon sprouting and new spine birth** and takes over the space."]},{"text":"If L1 connections are never activated again, L2 could take them over.","children":["L2 is used constantly, so its connections strengthen.","L1 is unused, so its connections weaken.","L2 replaces L1 by winning the same neural resources."]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"**A second language can apparently replace the first, but traces of the first might still be salvaged.**","children":["The adoptees showed no behavioral or brain sign of their childhood Korean.","As far as these tests could tell, French had replaced Korean."]},{"text":"The open question is whether traces of L1 are left behind.","children":["**Salvaging** a trace means recovering or using a leftover effect of the earlier language.","A trace might not show on a test of current ability.","It could still make relearning easier."]},{"text":"The two kinds of test ask different questions.","children":["A recognition test asks what the person can do now.","A relearning test asks whether new learning benefits from something left over.","Relearning is the measure that could reveal a trace."]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"**Students who overheard Spanish as children later pronounced it much more like native speakers, but their grammar was no better.** Terry Au and colleagues at UCLA (Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh, \"Overhearing a language during childhood\") studied undergraduates who overheard Spanish often but never spoke it.","children":["All started classroom Spanish around age 14.","All were tested at the end of second-year college Spanish.","They were compared with native speakers and typical late learners."]},{"text":"Values are given as natives, overhearers and late learners.","children":["**Voice onset time** (delay before voicing starts) for word-initial voiceless stops was 19.3, 19.6 and 36.2 milliseconds.","Word-medial voiceless stops were 22.4, 23.4 and 31.2.","**Lenition** (softening of voiced stops between vowels) was 43%, 24% and 1% word-initially.","It was 72%, 63% and 23% word-medially.","Accent ratings out of 5 were 4.3, 3.5 and 2.2 for voiceless stops, and 4.4, 3.3 and 2.7 for voiced.","Childhood slang production was 79%, 17% and 3%, and comprehension was 88%, 22% and 3%."]},{"text":"Grammar showed little advantage.","children":["Gender agreement was 93%, 70% and 68%.","Number agreement was 92%, 88% and 85%.","Grammaticality judgment accuracy was 94%, 64% and 63%.","Reaction times were 1,042, 2,475 and 2,209 milliseconds."]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"**Removing a first language can make it seem fully replaced, yet some knowledge may survive and resurface with later learning.**","children":["The adoptee and overhearer data are taken together.","If L1 is removed from the environment entirely, L2 can appear to completely replace it.","The adoptees showed no Korean on tests of current ability."]},{"text":"The replacement is not necessarily complete.","children":["**Residual knowledge** (a leftover trace) may be \"unmasked\" with later learning.","The overhearers' pronunciation is the example."]},{"text":"The two groups also differ in important ways.","children":["The adoptees had no new Korean instruction.","The overhearers had studied Spanish through a second-year college course, giving hidden knowledge a chance to surface."]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"**The adoptees and overhearers differ in many ways besides early exposure, so their results are hard to compare.** L2 can appear to replace L1, but residual knowledge may be unmasked later.","children":["Korean is rarely heard in France, but Spanish is common in Los Angeles.","A **heritage language** (spoken by family members) differs from a language nobody you know speaks.","**Cognates** are common between Spanish and English but rare between Korean and French.","A **shared writing system** links Spanish and English, while Korean uses a different script from French."]},{"text":"These differences could explain the different outcomes.","children":["Overhearers may have kept hearing Spanish, often from relatives.","Adoptees lost all contact with Korean speakers."]},{"text":"This is a general issue for research on second language acquisition and bilingualism.","children":["Languages and learning situations differ in many, many ways.","Those ways are hard, perhaps impossible, to control experimentally.","Researchers cannot assign people to grow up with particular languages."]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967) tied the sensitive period to the brain becoming specialized for language on one side.** L2 learning serves this research as a **model system**. A model system is a common, easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":["His theory says the sensitive period has to do with the development of **cerebral dominance** (one hemisphere, usually the left, becoming specialized for language).","The same idea is called developmental change in **language laterality** (how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both).","The window for learning language closes as language becomes fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory.","children":["Brain development roughly aligned with language milestones. Cortical neurons grow fast for about two years and then level off, with no measurements from age 2 to 18.","Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","A review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery** gave the second kind. Children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language. Adults did not recover it."]},{"text":"The theory concerns **maturational state** (the brain's stage of physical development).","children":["A young, less specialized brain can shift language to other tissue.","A mature, specialized brain has fewer options, so language learning becomes harder."]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"**Second-language learning lets researchers study the timing of the sensitive period in large groups of ordinary learners.** Most of what is known about first-language (L1) sensitive periods comes from **idiosyncratic** (particular to one individual) cases or relatively rare phenomena.","children":["One source is children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere.","Another source is Genie, who was isolated from language until age 13.","Each case carries unusual circumstances such as injury, illness or abuse, so the cause of the outcome is hard to identify."]},{"text":"In contrast, many people learn second languages at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**.","children":["L2 learners are common, so large groups can be studied.","L2 learners start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood.","Their final success can be measured."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** are used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive or critical period**.","children":["If later starters reliably end at lower levels, the age where the decline appears could mark when the period closes.","The first language complicates this. Every L2 learner already knows a language, and it shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989) found that people who arrived in the United States younger ended up with better English grammar.** **Lenneberg (1967)** hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** (from early infancy until puberty). Johnson and Newport asked whether that also holds for a second language.","children":["In its basic form, the critical period hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition.","If it extends to a second language, young children should learn an L2 better than adults and reach higher final proficiency.","Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language."]},{"text":"They compared the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**.","children":["Age on arrival in the United States ranged from 3 to 39.","Time lived in the United States ranged from 3 to 26 years.","A **grammaticality judgment task** tested a wide variety of structures of English grammar."]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage**.","children":["Up to puberty, test performance was **linearly related to age of arrival**. Each extra year meant a lower score.","After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable** and unrelated to age of arrival.","The effect was not an accident of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The effect appeared on every grammatical structure tested, although later learners mastered some structures far better than others."]},{"text":"The authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"**Each example set on the grammaticality judgment test pairs a correct sentence with an incorrect one to test one rule of English.** **Johnson and Newport (1989)** devised this **GJT**. Participants listened to recorded sentences and gave a **grammaticality judgment** (a decision about whether a sentence follows the rules of the language) for each one.","children":["The test was **untimed**, so participants could take as long as they needed.","(1a) “The farmer bought two pigs at the market” and (1b) “The farmer bought two pig at the market” test **plural marking** on nouns.","(2a) “The little boy is speaking to a policeman” and (2b) “The little boy is speak to a policeman” test the **progressive -ing** form of the verb.","(6a) “Tom is reading a book in the bathtub” and (6b) “Tom is reading book in the bathtub” test whether a needed **determiner** is present, here the article “a.”","(7a) “The boys are going to the zoo this Saturday” and (7b) “A boys are going to the zoo this Saturday” test whether determiner and noun agree in number.","(8a) “Larry went home after the party” and (8b) “Larry went the home after the party” test a spot where English does not allow an article.","(23a) “When will Sam fix his car?” and (23b) “When Sam will fix his car?” test **wh-question** word order, where the helping verb comes before the subject.","(24a) “What do they sell at the corner store?” and (24b) “What they sell at the corner store?” test the need for the helping verb “do” in a question."]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"**Age of arrival predicts grammar scores strongly for people who arrived as children and hardly at all for people who arrived as adults.** Each participant's score is items correct out of 276, set against **age of arrival** (AOA).","children":["The participants are split at puberty, the end of the critical period in Lenneberg's proposal.","A **correlation** (r) measures how closely two variables are related, on a scale from −1 to +1. A value near −1 means one variable falls steadily as the other rises. A value near 0 means little relationship."]},{"text":"**Early arrivals**, aged 3 to 15, had a correlation of **−0.87**.","children":["Arrivals at about 3 to 7 scored near the maximum, around 270.","Scores fell to around 250 near age 10 and to around 210 to 230 near age 15.","Age of arrival predicts much of the score in this group."]},{"text":"**Late arrivals**, aged 17 to 39, had a correlation of only **−0.16**.","children":["Scores were lower overall and widely scattered, from about 160 to 250.","Some scored around 250, while others of similar age scored below 180.","Age of arrival predicts little about who does well in this group."]},{"text":"The pattern is a **strong age effect in childhood** and **lower, highly variable performance in adulthood**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"**The youngest arrivals score like native speakers, later child arrivals score lower, and adult arrivals are scattered.** These are the three features of the **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data.","children":["There is a **strong relationship between AOA** (age of arrival) **and accuracy**. Earlier arrival goes with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect** (native-like, close to native English speakers).","**Late arrivals** have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy** (age of arrival barely predicts the score)."]},{"text":"The first two features are about people who arrived as children.","children":["The youngest arrivals reach native-like grammar.","Arrivals between about 8 and 15 reach progressively lower levels.","So the decline starts well before puberty."]},{"text":"“Nearly random” has a specific meaning.","children":["It means age of arrival and score are only weakly related among late arrivals.","Late arrivals still scored well above chance.","Scores varied greatly from person to person. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989) argue that both halves of their data fit a sensitive period for L2 learning that closes.** They call it a **“critical period.”**","children":["First, **ultimate attainment** (the final level a learner reaches) depends on AOA.","Scores drop steadily with later arrival in childhood, which is consistent with a **loss of language-learning ability over development**.","The timing is not entirely consistent with Lenneberg's hypothesis. He put the end at puberty, but the decline here starts around age 7 or 8.","Lenneberg was working with a large gap between early childhood and adolescence. He had no brain measurements between ages 2 and 18, so his timing was a rough estimate."]},{"text":"Second, attainment in late acquisition is **all over the place**.","children":["Adults who arrived at the same age reached very different levels.","After the sensitive period closes, attainment depends on **idiosyncratic learning abilities**, not the usual language acquisition processes. These abilities are individual differences in things such as memory, motivation or analytical skill.","Children share one natural acquisition process, so they reach similar levels at similar ages.","Adults no longer have that process, so they rely on abilities that differ greatly between people."]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport's data fit the maturational state hypothesis, because learners with a normal first language still did worse when they started later.** There are **two versions of the “critical period” hypothesis**. Both say that early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":["The **exercise hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation if it is not exercised during this time.","It also says that if the capacity is exercised, further language-learning abilities remain intact throughout life.","It predicts that an adult with a normal L1 learns an L2 as well as a child.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used.","It predicts that even an adult with a normal L1 learns an L2 less well."]},{"text":"Every participant had a normal first language, yet later starters learned the L2 less well.","children":["That fits the maturational state hypothesis, the idea named in the paper's title."]},{"text":"This changes how the **L1 critical period data** are interpreted.","children":["In cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised.","Both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases, so those cases cannot separate them.","Only second-language data can, because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"**An untimed grammaticality judgment task may measure explicit knowledge about rules instead of the implicit knowledge used in fluent language.** The task is **very different from fluent language use**, which is a methodological issue of test design.","children":["In fluent use, people speak and understand quickly without thinking about rules.","In the task, people consider each sentence and decide whether it is correct.","The worry is **“metalinguistic”** or **“explicit” knowledge** (conscious knowledge about language, such as knowing that English plurals add “-s”)."]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** contrasts **implicit** and **explicit knowledge** on several characteristics.","children":["In awareness, implicit is **intuitive** awareness of linguistic norms and explicit is **conscious** awareness.","In type of knowledge, implicit is **procedural** (knowing how) and explicit is **declarative** (knowing facts that can be stated).","In systematicity, implicit is variable but systematic and explicit is often anomalous and inconsistent.","In accessibility, implicit uses **automatic processing** and explicit uses **controlled processing** (effortful).","In use of L2 knowledge, implicit serves **fluent performance** and explicit serves a speaker who is planning and having difficulty.","In self-report, implicit is **nonverbalizable** (people cannot explain it) and explicit is **verbalizable**.","In learnability, implicit is potentially only learnable **within the critical period** and explicit is learnable at **any age**."]},{"text":"A consciously applied taught rule and automatic knowledge give the same score, so the test may miss what the critical period is supposed to affect.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"**The untimed GJT may measure explicitly learned “metalinguistic” knowledge better than it measures grammar acquisition.** The reason is its task demands. **Ellis (2005)** compared the design features of five tests.","children":["**Imitation** and **oral narrative** rely on feel, have time pressure, focus on meaning and require no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **timed GJT** relies on feel, has time pressure, focuses on form and requires no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **untimed GJT** relies on rules, has no time pressure, focuses on form and does allow metalinguistic knowledge.","The **metalanguage test** (people state rules) relies on rules, has no time pressure, focuses on form and requires metalinguistic knowledge."]},{"text":"Ellis related each test to starting age and to years of formal instruction, and only two relationships were significant.","children":["Starting age was related to the **timed GJT**, with a correlation of **−0.23**.","Years of formal instruction were related to the **untimed GJT with ungrammatical items**, with a correlation of **0.27**.","The other tests had only weak relationships with either measure."]},{"text":"Consistent with this, untimed GJT performance is correlated with **years of formal education in L2**.","children":["Classroom instruction teaches explicit rules, which help on the untimed test.","Starting age matters for the timed test, which limits the use of explicit rules.","So an age effect on an untimed GJT may partly reflect explicit, taught knowledge instead of natural grammar acquisition."]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"**If the two languages were more similar, learners could get more help from transfer, so the age pattern might come out differently.** **Transfer** (using first-language knowledge in learning a second language) is another methodological and theoretical issue.","children":["Languages that are **typologically more similar** (similar in structure, such as word order and word forms) can benefit more from transfer.","Cognates and loan words give learners familiar words to build on, such as French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette**, and Japanese **korokke** and **monoreru**."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested people with English as L2 and **Chinese or Korean as L1**.","children":["Chinese and Korean are very different from English in grammar and vocabulary.","Those learners got little help from transfer."]},{"text":"A learner whose L1 is close to English can reuse much of what they already know.","children":["Their attainment might relate to age differently.","If so, the age pattern in Johnson and Newport's data depends on the particular languages as well as on age."]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"**Late learners find some English rules much harder than others, partly following the order children learn them and partly following transfer.** **Johnson and Newport (1989)** compared errors on **12 types of English rules**. The groups were native speakers and arrivals at ages 3 to 7, 8 to 10, 11 to 15, 17 to 24 and 25 to 39.","children":["The rule types are determiners, plurals, **verb subcategorization** (which kinds of words or phrases can follow a verb), past tense, pronouns, particles and auxiliaries. The rest are third-person singular, wh-questions, yes/no questions, word order and progressive -ing.","Native speakers and the youngest arrivals make very few errors on any rule.","The latest arrivals make the most errors on **determiners**, around 65 to 68%, and **plurals**, around 45 to 55%.","Errors on **word order** and **progressive -ing** are under about 10% for every group."]},{"text":"Some of this looks like the **order in which children learn language**.","children":["Progressive -ing and word order are easiest, and they are among the first structures children master."]},{"text":"Some could be **transfer**.","children":["**Chinese has no determiners**, so Chinese speakers have no L1 category for “a” and “the” and find English articles especially hard.","Children learn questions very late, but questions are among the **easiest structures for late learners**.","Adults may use general cognitive skills or L1 knowledge for question forms.","Difficulty depends on the structure itself and on what the learner's first language provides."]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"**Spanish speakers learning English had almost the reverse age pattern, so the age effect depends on the language pairing as well as on age.** **Birdsong and Molis (2001)** repeated the Johnson and Newport study with Spanish speakers.","children":["For **Chinese and Korean speakers**, early arrivals (aged 3 to 15) had a correlation of **−0.87** and late arrivals (aged 17 to 39) had **−0.16**.","For **Spanish speakers**, early arrivals (aged 3 to 16) had a correlation of only **−0.23** and late arrivals (aged 17 to 44) had **−0.69**.","Spanish-speaking early arrivals scored near the maximum regardless of age, with only a slight decline around 16.","Spanish-speaking late arrivals fell from around 260 near age 18 to around 160 after 40."]},{"text":"The consequence concerns **maturational constraints** (limits on learning caused by the brain's development).","children":["A fixed maturational schedule should give the same pattern for every first language.","Instead, the pattern changed with the learners' first language.","Spanish is much closer to English than Chinese or Korean. So **transfer** may help Spanish speakers reach native-like grammar, even when they arrive late in childhood."]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"**Older learners reach lower L2 attainment overall, but how much lower depends on factors that are hard to separate.** These findings may not even count as “conclusions.”","children":[]},{"text":"The size of the age effect depends on three things.","children":["It depends on **what constructions are tested**. Determiners are much harder for late learners than word order.","It depends on **how they are tested**. Timed and untimed tests can differ because the untimed test allows explicit knowledge.","It depends on **language pairings**. Spanish speakers and Chinese or Korean speakers have different age patterns when learning English."]},{"text":"Three pairs are **hard to dissociate** (hard to separate).","children":["It is hard to separate **age of acquisition** from the **amount and quality of experience**. Younger arrivals usually have more years of exposure and more immersion, such as schooling in the language.","It is hard to separate **transfer effects** from **L2 acquisition**. L2 performance reflects what was learned and what carries over from the L1.","It is hard to separate **performance** (how well knowledge is used on a particular task) from **competence** (underlying knowledge of the language). A test score measures performance, so it may not reveal competence directly."]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-54":[{"text":"**Strong first-language links to concepts make Russian competitors draw looks during English listening, but weak second-language links do not work in reverse.** Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994) built the **Revised Hierarchical Model** on **spreading activation** (activating one representation partly activates those linked to it).","children":["The model connects an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a shared store of concepts.","**Lexical links** join words across the two languages.","**Conceptual links** join each vocabulary to meanings.","L1 words link strongly to concepts, and L2 words link weakly."]},{"text":"In the Marian and Spivey task in L2, Russian and English bilinguals hear an English instruction to pick up the speaker.","children":["The concept for \"speaker\" partially activates the Russian word for \"matches\", spichki.","Speaker and spichki share only **sound overlap**, not meaning.","The activity of spichki feeds back to the concept for \"matches\".","Conceptual activation for \"matches\" leads to eye movements toward the L1 competitor.","The listener still picks up the speaker."]},{"text":"In the task done in L1, the conceptual links with L2 might be too weak to create this interference reliably.","children":["An English competitor would need a strong link from its English name to its concept.","L2 words have only weak conceptual links.","So interference runs from the first language into the second, not the other way."]}],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"**The speech continuum changes F2 onset in equal steps, so every neighboring pair of stimuli differs by the same physical amount.** A **speech continuum** (a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property) is built here from 13 stimuli.","children":["**F1 is kept constant**, which removes one possible difference between stimuli.","The **F2 onset frequency** (the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition) is **varied linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13.","Linearly means the onset changes by the same step every time.","So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series.","children":["Early stimuli start F2 well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises.","Middle stimuli start F2 higher, so the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat.","Later stimuli start F2 above the vowel frequency, so the transition falls."]},{"text":"Equal steps make the continuum a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and others straddle a boundary.","A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size.","A categorical listener would hear steps within a category as alike and steps across a boundary as different.","Any advantage for boundary pairs would come from the boundary itself."]}],"review-3":[{"text":"**If perception were non-categorical, labeling would shift gradually from /b/ to /d/ to /g/ along the continuum.** An **ideal observer model** (a prediction of how a listener would respond under one particular account) is used as a baseline.","children":["This simple version assumes perception is **non-categorical** (it tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries).","Real data can be compared against the baseline to see whether perception is more categorical than that."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**.","children":["A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**.","**Stimulus 2** would be **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**, because it sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype."]},{"text":"Predicted **labeling** changes smoothly.","children":["Responses for one category fade out in a straight slope while responses for the neighboring category fade in.","Two neighboring labels are equally likely halfway between their prototypes.","No label changes in a sudden step."]}],"review-4":[{"text":"**If perception were linear, discrimination accuracy would be the same for every pair, whatever the labeling looks like.** A **linear** ideal observer perceives equal acoustic differences as equally large. It predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are the **same or different**.","The two tasks are separate, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**.","children":["There are three categories, and listeners are choosing between labels.","Each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away from it.","The peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself."]},{"text":"Discrimination is predicted to be flat.","children":["Pairs are two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**, so every pair has the same physical separation.","If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, discrimination accuracy would also be constant, at about 0.8.","A **flat** result would mean category boundaries play no role in hearing differences.","A **peaked** result would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear."]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957) found sharp labeling boundaries and the best discrimination right at those boundaries.** This pattern is **categorical perception**, and it departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions.","children":["Listeners labeled each stimulus on the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum.","Listeners also judged pairs that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart."]},{"text":"Labeling switched abruptly.","children":["Stimuli 1 through about 3 were labeled /b/ close to 100% of the time.","Around stimuli 4 and 5, responses switched almost completely to /d/, which stayed dominant through about stimulus 8.","Around stimuli 9 and 10, responses switched just as quickly to /g/.","The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**.","children":["Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary.","Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance** (the level expected from guessing).","Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data.","children":["They assumed listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels.","The predictions captured much of the real pattern.","Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity beyond the labels."]}],"review-6":[{"text":"**A thresholded model cannot explain why looks to the other member of the minimal pair rise gradually toward the category boundary.** This eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing**.","children":["A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in one sound, such as bear and pear.","Competitor looks climb steadily as VOT nears the boundary.","This holds even when the final response is the same, so it comes from graded activation within one category."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity never reaches the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation swings between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly switches on.","Every token in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","So word-level competition would be the same for every /b/."]},{"text":"In a cascaded model, graded information does reach the word level.","children":["More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word.","That explains the gradual rise in competitor looks directly."]}],"review-7":[{"text":"**TRACE also includes feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer.** Activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["In **lexical feedback**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","An uncertain sound can partly activate a word.","That word then strengthens the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"Influence runs in both directions.","children":["Acoustic evidence changes which words are active.","Active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the decision is still forming.","This helps listeners recover from noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**.","children":["Cascading passes incomplete evidence forward.","Feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","An interactive model can have both.","Experiments where word knowledge changes how sounds are heard, such as the **Ganong effect**, motivate adding feedback."]}],"review-8":[{"text":"**A late correction account predicts a reporting bias toward words that is the same size at every step of the continuum.** In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual**, but there is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound without word knowledge.","At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a word."]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["In a **word-nonword** continuum, the voiced end forms a word.","In a **nonword-word** continuum, the voiceless end forms a word.","In a **neutral** continuum, neither end has a lexical advantage."]},{"text":"A bias added after perception pushes every step toward the word.","children":["Word-nonword responses stay near 0.25 voiced at the long VOT end instead of falling to 0.","Nonword-word responses start near 0.75 voiced at the short VOT end instead of 1.","So even clear sounds are sometimes reported as the word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** (the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions) is a **flat line** near 0.25.","children":["Perception and word knowledge stay separate.","Word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"review-9":[{"text":"**If word knowledge shifts the category boundary, the lexical effect should peak at the items closest to the boundary.** In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset, the reading that forms a real word gets extra support.","So categorization itself changes, not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"The conditions differ only in the middle of the continuum.","children":["At both ends, the sound is clear, so voiced responses start near 1 and end near 0 in every condition.","Near the boundary, word-nonword crosses later and nonword-word crosses earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** forms a **peak**.","children":["It is near 0 at the endpoints.","It rises to about 0.35 for items closest to the boundary.","Those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant."]},{"text":"A **flat effect** points to a reporting bias. A **peaked effect** points to a boundary shift.","children":[]}],"review-10":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results are strikingly consistent with the second model, the boundary shift.**","children":["At the short and long VOT ends, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give nearly the same answers.","Near the boundary, the two conditions separate the most.","The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"The peak rules out a simple reporting bias.","children":["Word knowledge matters most where acoustic evidence is weakest.","At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance.","At the endpoints, the acoustics decide alone."]},{"text":"Ganong raised several possible explanations, focused on **guessing strategies**.","children":["A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when unsure of the sound."]},{"text":"Later models emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","The whole word can then shape how an ambiguous part of it is heard during recognition."]}],"review-11":[{"text":"**A TRACE simulation of the Ganong effect shows word and phoneme activations changing as an ambiguous start unfolds into the word plug.** The input is a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**, then /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/.","children":["Activations are recorded in four frames, one as each new sound arrives."]},{"text":"The first frame has only the ambiguous sound.","children":["/b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2.","Word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"The second and third frames add /l/ and the vowel.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl. Partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** all gain activation, because all fit the beginning.","/p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"The fourth frame adds the final **/g/**.","children":["/g/ fits plug but not blood or blush.","**Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, and the alternatives fall away.","**/p/ ends up more active than /b/**."]}],"review-12":[{"text":"**TRACE explains word effects on phoneme boundaries through top-down feedback from the lexical level to the phoneme level.** **Top-down processing** (higher-level knowledge influencing lower-level analysis).","children":[]},{"text":"Activity accumulates for **“plug”** as the input unfolds.","children":["The biggest gain comes when the **final /g/** arrives.","The /g/ is highly inconsistent with “blood” and “blush”, which were also active in Frame 3.","Blood and blush fit the early input, so they were plausible for a while.","Once /g/ arrives, their endings do not match, so they lose support."]},{"text":"The lexical level then sends **feedback to the phoneme level**.","children":["Feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ gets extra support.","The ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/.","Information at the end of a word can change how an earlier sound is heard."]}],"review-13":[{"text":"**A parser has to build a syntactic tree for each sentence while working with limited memory and limited time.** Syntactic explanations of the garden path effect start from this **parser** (a mental process that builds a tree for the sentences we hear or read).","children":["A **syntactic tree** records how words group into phrases and how the phrases relate.","**Limited memory** means the parser can keep only a limited number of structures available at once.","**Time constraints** mean words arrive quickly, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence."]},{"text":"The correct tree for the horse sentence is large.","children":["The subject is a big **NP** (noun phrase) containing “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”.","“That” and “was” are silent, and the clause has an empty subject position.","Inside the clause, raced takes the **PP** (prepositional phrase) “past the barn”.","The main **VP** (verb phrase) contains only fell."]},{"text":"The failed parse is small.","children":["“The horse” is an ordinary NP.","The main VP is “raced past the barn”, with raced as the main verb.","Fell has nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]}],"review-14":[{"text":"**Minimal attachment is a strategy in which the parser initially prefers the smaller tree, the one with fewer nodes.** A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":["The grammatical version of the horse sentence is **much more complex syntactically** than the failed parse.","It has **many more nodes**, for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was”.","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"**“Minimal attachment”** saves space.","children":["Each new word is attached to the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes.","Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment."]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**.","children":["That analysis needs the fewest nodes.","The reading where “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause, so the parser passes it over.","When fell arrives, it demands the larger structure, so the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**Late closure is a bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible, so new words join the phrase being built.** It is another parsing strategy in the same framework as minimal attachment.","children":["Keeping a clause “open” means adding incoming words to it instead of closing it and starting a new structure."]},{"text":"**Frazier (1987)** gives two structures for “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”.","children":["The phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big verb phrase starting with “blew open”.","The **preferred** structure attaches the PP to the VP. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, and the dynamite is the **instrument** used to blow open the safe.","The **unpreferred** structure attaches the PP to the NP “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**, and the phrase describes which safe was blown open, the one that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they mean different things.","children":["Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the incoming phrase joins it.","Minimal attachment also prefers VP attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”.","Here the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning easily."]}],"review-16":[{"text":"**People read more slowly when the sensible meaning of a sentence requires the unpreferred parse.** Late closure is at work in “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels”.","children":["Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe.","The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**.","That meaning needs “with the jewels” attached to the noun phrase “the safe”, the unpreferred NP attachment."]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find the slowdown.","children":["Reading time measures how long readers spend on each part of a sentence.","Eye movement recordings track where readers look and when they jump back to reread.","The extra time appears at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea** that the jewels were used to blow open the safe, and then **retrace their steps**.","children":["The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, as it did for the dynamite.","The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading.","The parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase, and this repair takes measurable time."]}],"review-17":[{"text":"**The syntactic account assumes a parser that works only on abstract syntactic structure and ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem.** It parallels the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","The parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without consulting meaning or context.","Meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**.","children":["It wants the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**.","It gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","Both limit how much structure must be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"Ignoring ambiguity is **very efficient**.","children":["The parser commits to one likely structure and builds only that one.","Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","When a later word does not fit, the structure must be revised, and that revision causes garden path difficulty."]}],"review-18":[{"text":"**Listeners briefly treat the empty towel as the place to put the apple, as minimal attachment predicts.** **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested how local **“world knowledge”** can be, using the visual scene in front of the listener.","children":["People saw a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple on a towel.","They heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box” and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","The unambiguous comparison instruction was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like the end of the clause “put the apple on the towel”.","children":["In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**.","In the intended reading, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do look at the empty towel** while planning their movements, as though considering it as a target.","children":["With the ambiguous instruction, looks go from the apple (near 1,000 milliseconds) to the empty towel and then to the box (near 2,000 milliseconds).","With the unambiguous instruction, looks go from the apple straight to the box.","The listener recognizes every word correctly but briefly takes the location phrase as the destination."]}],"review-19":[{"text":"**With two apples in the display, listeners take “on the towel” as telling which apple, right from the start.** This display is the **two-referent context**.","children":["One apple sits on a towel, and the other sits elsewhere.","The empty towel and the box are still present.","Now “the apple” alone does not say which apple to move, so “on the towel” becomes useful."]},{"text":"Eye movements change in this context.","children":["Participants usually look at the apple on the towel and then at the box.","Looks to the empty towel are much less common.","The **sequence and timing of eye movements**, relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between ambiguous and unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit one with “that’s on the towel”.","children":["The listener needs to know which apple, so a phrase that picks one out is expected.","The destination reading never takes hold.","The same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context differs."]}],"review-20":[{"text":"**People look at the empty towel much less when the display has two apples.** With two apples it becomes **felicitous** (appropriate and natural in the situation) to give more information about which apple is meant.","children":["With one apple, there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” is easily taken as a destination.","With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the incorrect destination, the empty towel.","children":["In the one-referent context, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials.","In the one-referent context, unambiguous instructions led to almost none.","In the two-referent context, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials.","In the two-referent context, unambiguous instructions led to such looks on roughly 12% of trials."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts, so the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled.","children":["This is an effect of **referential context** (the set of objects a phrase could refer to).","Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard."]}],"review-21":[{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia sounds like fluent word salad with syntax roughly intact, and Broca’s aphasia sounds telegraphic.** This **dichotomy** (a two-way division) is attractive.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**.","children":["Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported it.","Asked about his poor vision, another said “My wires don’t hire right.”","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a fork “a need for a schedule” and a spoon “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** is **“telegraphic” speech**, with difficulty with **word order and grammatical particles** and relative sparing of important **content words**.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported it.","Content words carry the story and the connecting grammatical words are missing."]}],"review-22":[{"text":"**Broca’s own patients would probably be classified today as having apraxia of speech and not Broca’s aphasia.** The dichotomy says Wernicke’s aphasia is **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon** and Broca’s aphasia is **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words.","**Activation** makes a candidate word available.","**Inhibition** suppresses competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"That is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. He described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”**","children":["Tan could produce only **one jargon syllable**, “tan.”","He seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses.","He seemed to **understand what was being said**."]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["Intact senses matter because they mean the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness” (a phrenology term for love of one’s children).","Examining Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic levels of description."]},{"text":"This is confusing.","children":["Broca’s actual patients were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control problems**.","They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech** (a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak).","Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"review-23":[{"text":"**The aphasia dichotomy is attractive because formal linguistics also separates the grammar from the lexicon.** Late 20th-century formal linguistics drew a **sharp distinction** between the two.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences.","The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized.","Wernicke’s aphasia would be a disorder of the lexicon.","Broca’s aphasia would be a disorder of the grammar."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**.","children":["We do not recognize a new sentence because it matches some familiar item in any simple way.","We recognize it because it is **generated by the grammar** each person has internalized.","A sentence never heard before can be understood because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave compelling demonstrations of the **independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","“colorless green ideas sleep furiously” makes no sense, but it is **grammatical** because its word order follows English grammar.","“furiously sleep ideas green colorless” is **not grammatical** because the same words come in an order the grammar does not allow.","Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference comes from **grammar as a separate system**."]}],"review-24":[{"text":"**Psycholinguistic tests are needed because ordinary sentences can be understood correctly without any grammar.** In conversation, there are lots of **redundant cues** (several sources of information that all point to the same meaning) to what a speaker intends.","children":["“Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of an unexpected event."]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” gives many ways to get the right meaning.","children":["The listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN** into an event representation, with or without syntax. Dogs bite people far more often than the reverse.","The sentence has **canonical word order**, which is agent, verb, undergoer, or subject, verb, object.","The **agent** is the doer and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English the first noun is usually the agent.","The **discourse context** (the surrounding conversation) clarifies a lot."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients’ comprehension may depend on these **compensatory heuristics** (mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness elsewhere).","children":["A patient might assume the most plausible event.","A patient might assume the first noun is the doer.","Either shortcut gets “The dog bit the man” right with no grammatical analysis."]},{"text":"A better test sentence is “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["The **man** is the biter, although the dog comes first and dogs are likelier biters.","Only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif wrote four groups of sentences that differ in how much meaning helps the listener.** Each group has eight sentences.","children":["**A, control sentences**, are simple and in canonical order. Three are “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”","**B, center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, make the roles obvious from meaning. Three are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.”","**C, reversible center-embedded sentences**, let either participant do the action. Three are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.”","**D, improbable center-embedded sentences**, describe events against expectations. Three are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.”"]},{"text":"A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause in the middle of the main clause.","children":["In every embedded sentence, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the relative clause.","In B, apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone gives the roles.","In C, only grammar gives the roles.","In D, common sense points to the wrong answer."]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should pass A and B and fail C and D.","children":["A listener who parses normally should pass all four."]}],"review-26":[{"text":"**Broca’s patients failed only when grammar was the sole guide to who did what to whom.** **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task** (hearing a sentence and choosing which picture fits it).","children":["Accuracy tells whether the listener understood who did what to whom, with no spoken answer required.","Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia heard all four sentence types."]},{"text":"Each group had its own pattern.","children":["**Healthy controls** were more or less **equally good** at all stimulus types, near the maximum.","**Wernicke’s patients** were **poor overall**, even on simple control sentences. This is probably a result of their comprehension deficits, which affect words as well as grammar.","**Broca’s patients** scored **high** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning supplies the answer.","**Broca’s patients** scored **low** on reversible and improbable sentences, where syntax conflicted with common sense or probable semantic roles."]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","children":["The deficit appeared in understanding as well as speaking, which places it in the grammar itself.","The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.”","Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** process (grammatical analysis).","They keep the **heuristic** shortcuts (based on meaning)."]}],"review-27":[{"text":"**Dick and colleagues described one event with four sentence types, and only two of them put the doer first.** This study begins the **problems with the dichotomy**.","children":["Participants listened to a sentence and had to **indicate which animal is doing the action**.","A picture of a dog and a picture of a cat appeared on a computer screen.","Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","The event was always a dog biting a cat."]},{"text":"Two types put the **doer first**.","children":["**Active** is “The dog is biting the cat.”","**Subject cleft** is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.”","A **cleft** splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft the focused noun is the doer.","Choosing the first animal mentioned gives the **right** answer."]},{"text":"Two types put the **receiver first**.","children":["**Object cleft** is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” The focused noun is the one being bitten.","**Passive** is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","Choosing the first animal mentioned gives the **wrong** answer."]},{"text":"The types separate two kinds of listener.","children":["A listener who always picks the first animal passes actives and subject clefts and fails object clefts and passives.","A listener who parses the grammar passes all four."]}],"review-28":[{"text":"**Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with object clefts and passives, but their performance was not great overall.** Aphasia patients and healthy aging controls did the task, and the pattern was **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped with the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":["**Elderly controls** scored close to 100% on all four types.","**Anomic** patients (main problem is word finding) scored about 89% on actives and subject clefts, about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","**Conduction** patients scored about 75% on actives and subject clefts and about 42% on object clefts.","**Broca’s** patients scored about 61% on actives, 65% on subject clefts, 42% on object clefts and 54% on passives.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"Object clefts and passives are the **syntactically complex** stimuli.","children":["They are **noncanonical** (they depart from the usual doer-first order)."]},{"text":"Two things are true of the Broca’s group at once.","children":["Even on simple actives, they were far below the elderly controls.","They paid an extra cost on complex structures.","So the pattern is a **general drop** plus an **extra cost** for complex structures.","The groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop."]}],"review-29":[{"text":"**Healthy undergraduates under stress make the same errors on object clefts and passives that Broca’s patients make.** The same task was run under **conditions that stress the language system**.","children":["One stressor was **compressed** speech (speech played faster than normal).","One was noise.","One was a **low-pass filter** (removes the higher frequencies, so speech sounds muffled).","Two were **auditory digits** and **visual digits** (number lists to remember while listening, a secondary task that uses up memory resources)."]},{"text":"The cost fell on the complex sentences.","children":["With a **single stressor**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts.","With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts and about 91% on passives.","With **two stressors**, the effects grew larger.","With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts.","It was about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives."]},{"text":"**Object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations.**","children":["These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge.","Stress alone produced an aphasia-like profile.","So the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia **may be a “performance” issue after all**.","Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction hurts them first.","The patients may keep their grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"review-30":[{"text":"**Broca’s aphasics had difficulty matching an action to an object whether the stimuli were linguistic or not.** **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** used this task to ask whether Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are **language-specific**.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking.","Participants chose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words."]},{"text":"Aphasic patients made more errors than controls in both versions.","children":["Non-linguistic accuracy was **98.5%** for controls and **93.4%** for patients.","Linguistic accuracy was **99.4%** for controls and **87.5%** for patients."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the **distractor** (the type of wrong choice offered).","children":["With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors (related in meaning to the correct object), controls scored 99.2% and patients 86.3%.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, such as a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The non-linguistic version removes language but keeps meaning.","children":["A pantomimed action must be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb is linked to its object.","A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on it.","Difficulty on it points to a **broader problem with action meanings**, shared with language."]}],"review-31":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area.** So damage there **affects more than syntax**.","children":["Lesion maps mark the areas linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**.","The maps cover three horizontal slices of the brain.","Colored regions mark where damage went with poorer performance, and red and yellow mark the strongest relationships.","The strongest associations are in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area."]},{"text":"The task did not require language.","children":["Participants only interpreted a pantomimed action and chose an object.","They did not produce a sentence.","They did not work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"Two things follow about Broca’s area.","children":["Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","Broca’s area supports **action understanding** as well as grammatical processing.","Any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"review-32":[{"text":"**Infants cannot say what they hear, so researchers measure head turns and sucking instead.** Both behaviors change when an infant notices something new.","children":["Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","The experimenter controls exactly which acoustic difference is presented."]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** (training an infant to turn toward a sound when it changes) is used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap.","An experimenter keeps the infant's attention forward with toys.","A correct head turn after a change earns a visual reward, such as a lighted, moving toy.","After the infant learns this link, a head turn means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** relies on the infant's natural interest in something new.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier connected to a pressure recorder.","Strong sucks make a sound play.","As one sound repeats, sucking slows because of **habituation** (getting used to a repeated sound).","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Both methods test whether infants hear the same distinctions that adults hear.","children":["The head turn relies on a learned link between a sound change and a reward.","Sucking relies on a natural response to something new."]}],"review-33":[{"text":"**Four-month-old infants notice a change in voice onset time only when it crosses an adult category boundary.** **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found this with high-amplitude sucking.","children":["The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or VOT (the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration).","With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than with +10 milliseconds.","Infants heard one syllable repeatedly until sucking declined. Then the sound changed or stayed the same."]},{"text":"Three conditions were compared.","children":["**20D** had sounds 20 milliseconds apart, +20 versus +40, from **different** adult phonemic categories.","**20S** had sounds 20 milliseconds apart, −20 versus 0 or +60 versus +80, from the **same** category.","**0** was the **control**, with the same sound throughout."]},{"text":"Sucking rose above baseline and then declined before the change in every condition.","children":["In 20D, sucking **jumped back up**, to around 50 responses per minute.","In 20S, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling.","In the control, sucking kept declining, to about 15 responses per minute."]},{"text":"The 20D and 20S changes were the same acoustic size.","children":["So the difference comes from **crossing the category boundary**.","The control establishes what repetition alone does."]}],"review-34":[{"text":"**Categorical perception at four months seemed like evidence for innateness because it appears so early.** Eimas's result was a big deal for that reason.","children":["Categorical perception was considered the first step in turning speech into a **symbolic format** (one that represents a sound as a category, such as /b/).","Later linguistic processes could then use that format.","Finding it in infants suggested the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"Four-month-olds are **really little**.","children":["Gross motor milestones (large body movements) at that age include manipulating objects with two hands.","They also include holding the head and chest up while prone (lying face down).","Color vision and general **visual acuity** (sharpness of vision) are still \"in progress.\"","They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"This is very, very early for an apparently **language-specific** ability to emerge.","children":["Infants categorize speech sounds before they can produce them.","Their other abilities are still immature at that age.","So the ability seemed built in, not learned through long experience."]}],"review-35":[{"text":"**English-learning infants can hear non-English contrasts at six months and mostly lose them by twelve months.** This decline is **perceptual narrowing** (losing sensitivity to sound distinctions the child's language does not use).","children":["At around six months, English-learning infants discriminate speech contrasts they have never heard before.","As development goes on, the ability to perceive these contrasts declines."]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested two non-English contrasts.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is between two \"ta\"-like sounds.","The **Salish contrast** is between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language.","The measure was the percentage of infants reaching a **discrimination criterion**."]},{"text":"The percentage dropped with age.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, about **95%** reached it for Hindi and about **80%** for Salish.","At 8 to 10 months, about 67% reached it for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At 10 to 12 months, about **20%** reached it for Hindi and about **10%** for Salish.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants reached it close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"This is **perceptual reorganization** during the first year of life.","children":["Infants who hear a contrast keep it, so the decline depends on language experience.","Narrowing is related to categorical perception because native categories form. Sounds the language groups together become harder to tell apart.","A nonnative distinction inside one native category is then heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"review-36":[{"text":"**The compelling narrative says infants are born able to hear every contrast, and learning only removes the ones they do not need.** It combines the infant findings into one account.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a special property of the language system.","These abilities are present very early in development."]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world\"** (able to hear all contrasts).","children":["This means they have innate, **language-general** knowledge about speech (knowledge that applies to the speech sounds of every language).","A newborn is ready to learn any human language.","Children then **rapidly tune** their perceptual systems to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"The role of learning is to **select** among the many possible innately specified phonetic contrasts.","children":["Learning narrows the **inventory** (the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive).","Learning removes the contrasts the native language does not use.","Learning keeps the contrasts that are meaningful in the native language.","The native language decides which innate categories survive."]}],"review-37":[{"text":"**One experience with a new word fits many possible meanings, so the child cannot tell which one is right.** This is the **inductive problem of early word learning**. **Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and here the rule is a word’s meaning.","children":["A child hears a new word while watching a cat.","It might name a category at some level (**CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**).","It might name this particular cat (**FELIX**).","It might name a property (**FURRY** or **ALIVE**).","It might name an action (**WALKING**).","It might name a part (**WHISKERS** or **LEG**)."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**.","children":["Each candidate fits what the child sees.","Nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends."]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**.","children":["A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","“Gavagai” might mean rabbit, undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast.","Joint attention, cues to the speaker’s intention and default strategies cut down the possibilities."]}],"review-38":[{"text":"**Joint attention ties a new word to the object the speaker is attending to.** **Joint attention** means sharing an attentional focus with another person.","children":["Two people look at the same object.","Each knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following** (looking where someone else is looking) **emerges around 9 months of age**.","children":["An adult looks over at an object.","The infant follows the adult’s eyes to it."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**.","children":["The infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent.","Joint attention shrinks the hypothesis space (the set of possible meanings) for the word."]}],"review-39":[{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned a new word from an engaged adult but not from a voice behind a screen.** **Baldwin and colleagues** ran this study, and it supports the claim that **joint attention optimizes word learning**.","children":["Two groups of infants heard a novel word while playing with an object.","In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The two conditions matched closely.","children":["In both, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","They differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"The 18-month-olds **learned the novel word in the joint attention group** and **not in the no joint attention group**.","children":["Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough.","Infants needed evidence that a speaker meant the word to refer to that object.","The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, so the difference came from the visible, engaged speaker."]}],"review-40":[{"text":"**Children narrow the meaning of a new word by using cues to what the speaker intends, such as where the speaker looks.** These are **intentional cues** (signals of what the speaker means to refer to), and they narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words.","children":["**Eye gaze direction** is one intentional cue.","A speaker who looks toward one toy marks that toy as the object of attention."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of 16 to 17 months and of 18 to 19 months in two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on while saying the new word.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket, a different toy, while saying the new word."]},{"text":"Discrepant labeling pulls the infant’s focus apart from the speaker’s focus.","children":["Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy.","Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket.","Comparing the conditions reveals whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of meaning."]}],"review-41":[{"text":"**Infants use a speaker’s facial expression to work out which object a new word names.** **Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant.","Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult’s expression picked out."]},{"text":"These are intentional cues from the speaker, and they come in two kinds.","children":["**Affective cues** are emotional expressions. An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for.","**Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions. Reaching for something can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"The speaker’s reaction can single out one object among several.","children":["A satisfied expression signals that the speaker found the desired object.","A disappointed expression signals that the object is not the one meant.","Word learning involves grasping the goal behind the interaction."]}],"review-42":[{"text":"**Children assume by default that a new word names a whole object.** This is the **whole object assumption**, one of the **word learning strategies**.","children":["Word learning strategies are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**.","A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","The strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words.","They give children a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The whole object assumption favors the object over everything else about it.","children":["The word is not expected to name a part of the object.","The word is not expected to name its color or texture.","The word is not expected to name something it is doing.","A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat favors “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption rules out many unlikely meanings at once and fits how adults usually name objects for children.","children":["Later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties."]}],"review-43":[{"text":"**Children assume by default that an object has only one name, so a new word goes to an object without a name.** This is **mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**.","children":["As a default, **objects can have only one name**.","The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","It **emerges during the second half of the second year** (roughly 18 to 24 months)."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child hears a new word.","The dog already has a name, so the child takes the word to mean the parrot.","No gaze or emotional cue is needed."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity combines with the whole object assumption.","children":["The whole object assumption says a new word names an object.","Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet.","Later learning can override the default when an object turns out to have two names."]}],"review-44":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967) tied the sensitive period to the brain becoming specialized for language on one side.** L2 learning serves this research as a **model system**. A model system is a common, easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":["His theory says the sensitive period has to do with the development of **cerebral dominance** (one hemisphere, usually the left, becoming specialized for language).","The same idea is called developmental change in **language laterality** (how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both).","The window for learning language closes as language becomes fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory.","children":["Brain development roughly aligned with language milestones. Cortical neurons grow fast for about two years and then level off, with no measurements from age 2 to 18.","Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","A review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery** gave the second kind. Children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language. Adults did not recover it."]},{"text":"The theory concerns **maturational state** (the brain's stage of physical development).","children":["A young, less specialized brain can shift language to other tissue.","A mature, specialized brain has fewer options, so language learning becomes harder."]}],"review-45":[{"text":"**Second-language learning lets researchers study the timing of the sensitive period in large groups of ordinary learners.** Most of what is known about first-language (L1) sensitive periods comes from **idiosyncratic** (particular to one individual) cases or relatively rare phenomena.","children":["One source is children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere.","Another source is Genie, who was isolated from language until age 13.","Each case carries unusual circumstances such as injury, illness or abuse, so the cause of the outcome is hard to identify."]},{"text":"In contrast, many people learn second languages at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**.","children":["L2 learners are common, so large groups can be studied.","L2 learners start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood.","Their final success can be measured."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** are used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive or critical period**.","children":["If later starters reliably end at lower levels, the age where the decline appears could mark when the period closes.","The first language complicates this. Every L2 learner already knows a language, and it shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"review-46":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989) found that people who arrived in the United States younger ended up with better English grammar.** **Lenneberg (1967)** hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** (from early infancy until puberty). Johnson and Newport asked whether that also holds for a second language.","children":["In its basic form, the critical period hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition.","If it extends to a second language, young children should learn an L2 better than adults and reach higher final proficiency.","Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language."]},{"text":"They compared the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**.","children":["Age on arrival in the United States ranged from 3 to 39.","Time lived in the United States ranged from 3 to 26 years.","A **grammaticality judgment task** tested a wide variety of structures of English grammar."]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage**.","children":["Up to puberty, test performance was **linearly related to age of arrival**. Each extra year meant a lower score.","After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable** and unrelated to age of arrival.","The effect was not an accident of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The effect appeared on every grammatical structure tested, although later learners mastered some structures far better than others."]},{"text":"The authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"review-47":[{"text":"**The youngest arrivals score like native speakers, later child arrivals score lower, and adult arrivals are scattered.** These are the three features of the **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data.","children":["There is a **strong relationship between AOA** (age of arrival) **and accuracy**. Earlier arrival goes with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect** (native-like, close to native English speakers).","**Late arrivals** have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy** (age of arrival barely predicts the score)."]},{"text":"The first two features are about people who arrived as children.","children":["The youngest arrivals reach native-like grammar.","Arrivals between about 8 and 15 reach progressively lower levels.","So the decline starts well before puberty."]},{"text":"“Nearly random” has a specific meaning.","children":["It means age of arrival and score are only weakly related among late arrivals.","Late arrivals still scored well above chance.","Scores varied greatly from person to person. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]}],"review-48":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport's data fit the maturational state hypothesis, because learners with a normal first language still did worse when they started later.** There are **two versions of the “critical period” hypothesis**. Both say that early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":["The **exercise hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation if it is not exercised during this time.","It also says that if the capacity is exercised, further language-learning abilities remain intact throughout life.","It predicts that an adult with a normal L1 learns an L2 as well as a child.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used.","It predicts that even an adult with a normal L1 learns an L2 less well."]},{"text":"Every participant had a normal first language, yet later starters learned the L2 less well.","children":["That fits the maturational state hypothesis, the idea named in the paper's title."]},{"text":"This changes how the **L1 critical period data** are interpreted.","children":["In cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised.","Both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases, so those cases cannot separate them.","Only second-language data can, because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"review-49":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994) found the cohort effect, where words sharing a target's beginning compete early in recognition.**","children":["A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","Participants saw a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","The display had a beaker (target), a beetle (same beginning), a speaker (rhyme) and a carriage (unrelated)."]},{"text":"Looks were tracked over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and beetle rose together early.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds, then fell once the words diverged.","Looks to the beaker kept rising toward 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and peaked near 0.1, because its overlap comes at the end.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds.","children":["They drop out when the input stops matching.","Eye movements track this moment by moment."]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey tested whether a Russian word competes with an English target when the two words only begin with the same sounds.**","children":["A **cross-language competitor** is an object whose name in the other language begins like the target.","They hypothesized that if L1 words are always activated, Russian speakers hearing English \"speaker\" should look at matches.","English **speaker** sounds like Russian **спички (spichki)**, meaning matches."]},{"text":"Each English target had an English and a Russian competitor.","children":["Speaker had spear and spichki (matches).","Boot had book and buben (tambourine).","Shark had shovel and sharik (balloon).","Chair had chess set and cherepaha (turtle).","Marker had marbles and marka (stamp).","Barbed wire had bark and barhat (velvet).","Plug had plum and platye (dress).","Gun had gum and gaika (nut).","Lock had lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For Russian targets the roles were reversed.","children":["Spichki had English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","Marka had English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","Platye had English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The tie is only sound, since speakers and matches share no meaning.","children":["So looks to the matches measure activation of spichki."]}],"review-51":[{"text":"**While listening in their second language, bilinguals looked at objects with similar first-language names, so the first language stays active.** Russian and English bilinguals followed instructions in English.","children":["The **between-language competitor** had a similar Russian name.","The **within-language competitor** had a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["Between-language competitor alone drew looks on 18% of trials.","Within-language competitor alone drew looks on 18% of trials.","With both present, the between-language one drew 13% and the within-language one 19%.","A control filler with no competitor drew only 7%."]},{"text":"With one competitor, both kinds drew looks to the same extent.","children":["The Russian name pulled the eyes as strongly as an English one."]},{"text":"With both present, the within-language competitor drew more looks.","children":["The between-language competitor still drew about twice as many as the control.","Bilinguals still chose correctly, but L1 words competed along the way."]}],"review-52":[{"text":"**While listening in their first language, Russian speakers did not reliably activate English words, so the effect runs one way.** The same bilinguals followed instructions in Russian.","children":["The between-language competitor now had a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was again the percent of trials with a look.","children":["Between-language competitor alone drew 8%.","Within-language competitor alone drew 14%.","With both present, the between-language one drew 8% and the within-language one 13%.","The control filler drew 5%."]},{"text":"The small difference between the between-language and control conditions is **not statistically significant** (within chance variation).","children":["So English words do not seem to be activated in the Russian task.","The within-language competitor still produced a cohort effect in Russian."]},{"text":"The result is **asymmetric**.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","This fits the idea that the first language is more firmly established and more easily activated."]}],"review-53":[{"text":"**In the Revised Hierarchical Model, activation spreads more easily from second-language words to first-language words, because learning emphasizes that direction.** Kroll and Stewart built the model on **spreading activation** (activating one representation partly activates those linked to it).","children":["The model has an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a store of **concepts** (meanings).","**Lexical links** join L1 and L2 words directly.","**Conceptual links** join each vocabulary to the concepts."]},{"text":"The L1 lexicon is assumed to be larger.","children":["L1 words are strongly connected to relevant concepts.","L2 words are more weakly connected to concepts."]},{"text":"The L2 lexicon has strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon.","children":["The link from L2 to L1 is strong.","The link from L1 to L2 is weak."]},{"text":"Learning builds this pattern.","children":["Learners usually attach each new L2 word to its L1 translation.","The direct link from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early on."]}],"review-55":[{"text":"**The model predicts that balanced bilinguals are not two monolinguals in one, because both languages compete all the time.**","children":["A **balanced bilingual** (someone about equally skilled in both languages) has similarly strong conceptual links for both languages.","This prediction is counter-intuitive.","One might expect each language to run separately."]},{"text":"Strong links in both languages keep both vocabularies active.","children":["Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages.","**Competitive dynamics** (words of the two languages competing for selection) are engaged all the time.","**Inhibition** (suppressing the word from the language not in use) is engaged all the time too."]},{"text":"The prediction led to a lot of work on bilingual advantages in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks.","children":["Cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","Constant practice choosing one language might improve attention control in general."]}]}};
